export const KB = {"duke":"## Part 1 — Verified application facts (from the Founder's Compass dashboard)\n\n# Duke University (id: duke)\n\nPlatform: Common App | Test: Test-optional | Selectivity: {\"rate\":\"4.7%\",\"tier\":\"Most selective\",\"note\":\"Class of 2030 (most recent completed cycle): 61,935 applications, 2,930 admits. Early Decision 13.8% (847 of 6,159) vs Regular Decision 3.7% (2,083 of 55,776) — roughly a 3.7x gap, and ED fills about half the class. Duke runs a single ED round; there is no ED II.\",\"cycle\":\"ok\"}\n\nDeadlines: [{\"plan\":\"Early Decision (binding)\",\"date\":\"Nov 2\",\"note\":\"Decisions mid-December\",\"cycle\":\"ok\"},{\"plan\":\"Regular Decision\",\"date\":\"Jan 4\",\"note\":\"Decisions late March / early April\",\"cycle\":\"ok\"}]\n\nApp note: Duke uses the Common App: the personal statement (250–650 words) plus two required Duke essays and one optional prompt chosen from three. Duke does not accept resumes. You may add one optional personal recommendation from an employer or mentor — useful if someone has supervised your venture work.\n\nEntrepreneurship headline: Entrepreneurship at Duke is a cross-campus layer, not a department: you bolt the Innovation & Entrepreneurship (I&E) Certificate onto any major and tap a growing Durham innovation district.\n\nDegrees: I&E Certificate (no standalone major) · pairs naturally with Pratt Engineering, Computer Science, Economics or Public Policy\n\n## Supplements (verified 2026-27 where cycle=ok)\nTwo required essays, then one optional prompt chosen from three (250 words each).\n\n- **Why Duke? (required)** [Required, 250 words, cycle=ok]: What is your impression of Duke as a university and community, and why do you believe it is a good match for your goals, values, and interests? If there is something specific that attracts you to our academic offerings in Trinity College of Arts and Sciences or the Pratt School of Engineering, or to our co-curricular opportunities, feel free to include that, too.\n- **Communities (required — NEW for 2026-27)** [Required, 250 words, cycle=ok]: We all belong to communities defined by place, faith, family, culture, interests or shared experience. Tell us about a community that has shaped who you are, any way it has set you apart, and what you’ve learned from being part of it that you hope to bring to Duke.\n- **Viewpoints & experiences (optional — pick one of three)** [Optional, 250 words, cycle=ok]: We believe a wide range of viewpoints and experiences is essential to maintaining Duke’s vibrant living and learning community. Please share anything in this context that might help us better understand you and your potential contributions to Duke.\n- **Difference of opinion (optional — pick one of three)** [Optional, 250 words, cycle=ok]: Meaningful dialogue often involves respectful disagreement. Provide an example of a difference of opinion you’ve had with someone you care about. What did you learn from it?\n- **Something you are excited about (optional — pick one of three)** [Optional, 250 words, cycle=ok]: What’s the last thing that you’ve been really excited about?\n\n## Programs\n- Duke I&E: University-wide Innovation & Entrepreneurship initiative coordinating courses, mentors and events across every school. Directed by Jamie Jones, who previously ran Rice's Liu Idea Lab and co-founded the impact-investing firm Impact Engine.\n- I&E Certificate: The flagship undergraduate credential, open to all majors. Four courses plus two co-curricular experiences (one 300-hour, one 150-hour). Two courses are fixed: I&E 352 Strategies for Innovation & Entrepreneurship, and the I&E 499 portfolio capstone. Students pick one of six pathways — Entrepreneur/Founder, Designer/Builder, Innovator/Change Agent, Creator, Arts & Humanities, or self-designed.\n- New Ventures course arc: I&E 385 / 395 / 396 (Discover, Develop, Deliver) — the hands-on three-course sequence for actually building a venture for credit. Other distinctive electives include I&E 252 Learning to Fail, I&E 283 Product Management, I&E 360 Entrepreneurial Finance and I&E 511 Designing Ethical Tech.\n- The Luby Family Bullpen: Duke I&E's home base, in the lowest level of the Fox Student Center at Fuqua — open workspace and event hub for students, alumni and visiting founders. Duke I&E also keeps a downtown footprint in the Imperial Building on Morris Street, in Durham's innovation district.\n- Melissa & Doug Entrepreneurs: The signature student-founder program, endowed by Melissa Bernstein '87 and Doug Bernstein. Year-long and non-credit; roughly 10–12 spots from about 15 interviews each May. No revenue required to apply, and prior Duke I&E involvement is an advantage.\n\n## Funding/competitions\n- John Shen Duke Startup Showcase: Duke's flagship venture competition (recently renamed and endowed). A four-month coaching and pitch arc, not a one-night event: applications open around Dec 1, roughly 100 teams cut to 50, then 36 to the April showcase. The 2026 edition paid out over $110,000 non-dilutive, with a $30,000 Borchardt Grand Prize for the top undergraduate venture. Next showcase: April 8, 2027.\n- Melissa & Doug funding: $5,000 tied to venture milestones, with another $5,000 possible, plus executive coaching and two funded trips.\n- Duke Capital Partners: Duke's angel co-investment fund, under the Office for Translation & Commercialization. Over $89M invested since 2015. Its Student Associate program is mostly graduate students but is formally open to undergrads — 42 associates in FY25, about a 10% acceptance rate, 10 hrs/week for two years.\n- Interplay Fellows Award: New in 2026, from a $1M gift: up to $40,000 non-dilutive to one graduating Duke founder a year, to go full-time on the venture. Preference for financial need and deep Duke I&E engagement.\n\n## Watch-outs\n- No standalone undergraduate entrepreneurship major — it's a certificate + co-curricular layer.\n- Absent from the Princeton Review Top 50 for undergrad entrepreneurship (2026), despite strong resources.\n- Ultra-selective; entrepreneurial drive must show up inside a broader excellent application.\n\n## Known successful-essay sources\n- How to write the Duke supplements — examples + guide (College Essay Guy) https://www.collegeessayguy.com/blog/duke-supplemental-essay — 2025–26 · full example essays for every Duke prompt, annotated\n- 2 strong Duke essay examples (CollegeVine) https://blog.collegevine.com/duke-essay-examples — 2023 · real Pratt and 'Why Duke' essays with analysis\n- Duke supplemental essay database (Clastify) https://www.clastify.com/supplement/duke-university — 2023–25 · 30+ real admitted essays, by prompt\n\n## Other fields\n- short: Duke\n- mono: D\n- city: Durham\n- state: NC\n- type: Private\n- setting: Midsize city · Research Triangle\n- undergrad: ≈6,640\n- platform: Common App\n- ac: #0A2C7C\n- ac2: #001A57\n- fit: 80\n- verdict: Strong fit\n- sub: {\"program\":7,\"experiential\":7,\"outcomes\":7,\"location\":6}\n- rateNum: 5.2\n- dlSort: 1102\n- testPolicy: Test-optional\n- sat: 1500–1570\n- act: 34–36\n- fee: $85\n- recs: 2 teacher + 1 counselor\n- opens: Early August\n- bestFor: Interdisciplinary high-achievers who want an elite brand plus the I&E Certificate layered onto engineering, CS, economics or public policy — within reach of the Research Triangle's startup scene.\n- strengths: [\"Top-10 national university with elite engineering (Pratt), public policy (Sanford) and computer science.\",\"Famously tight, loyal alumni network and a strong campus culture.\",\"Anchored in the Research Triangle (RTP, Durham) — a fast-growing tech and biotech hub.\",\"Deep cross-disciplinary culture: easy to combine technical, design and business coursework.\"]\n- entreRank: Not ranked in Princeton Review Top 50 (2026) — a genuine gap vs. peers\n- founders: Entrepreneurship is delivered co-curricularly rather than as a flagship major, but Duke's alumni founder network across tech, biotech and finance is deep and well-capitalized.\n- cardEnt: I&E Certificate layered on any major · Duke Capital Partners · Research Triangle access.\n- optIntro: Two required essays, then one optional prompt chosen from three (250 words each).\n- outcomes: [\"Strong placement into consulting, finance, tech and biotech; rising startup activity.\",\"Duke Capital Partners gives student/alumni ventures a direct line to capital.\",\"Highly active, geographically dispersed alumni network.\"]\n- cost: {\"mode\":\"private\",\"sticker\":\"$96,597 / yr (2026-27, confirmed by trustees Feb 2026) — Fall 2027 entrants should budget for another ~5% rise\",\"aid\":\"Meets 100% of demonstrated need; no-loan for many families. Limited merit (A.B. Duke, Robertson). Duke's 4.95% increase for 2026-27 was among the steepest of any Ivy+ school this year — only Brown and Cornell now cost more.\"}\n- src: Sourced from admissions.duke.edu (2026-27 deadlines and essays confirmed), Duke Today + The Duke Chronicle (Dec 2025 and Mar 2026, Class of 2030 admit rates), financialaid.duke.edu, entrepreneurship.duke.edu, and The Duke Chronicle (Jul 8, 2026, on the confirmed 2026-27 $96,597 cost of attendance vs. Ivy+ peers). Supplemental prompts verified 8 Sept 2026 directly against admissions.duke.edu/apply/ (official). NOTE: the 2026-27 set CHANGED — “Communities” is now a second required essay, the optional set narrowed to one-of-three, and the 2025-26 AI prompt was retired.\n\n## Part 2 — Deep research: paths in, what readers want, admitted essays\n\n# Duke University — Advisor Knowledge Base\n\n## Paths in\n\n**Admission plans.** Duke runs one Early Decision round (binding, Nov 2) and Regular Decision (Jan 4). There is no EA and no ED II. ED fills roughly half the class: the Class of 2030 saw 13.8% ED vs. 3.7% RD — a 3.7x gap. Submitting ED is the single largest lever an applicant can pull.\n\n**Which school to apply to.** You choose at application time: Trinity College of Arts and Sciences or the Pratt School of Engineering. Both are highly selective but there is no major-based direct-admit structure. Pratt applicants must have taken calculus and are strongly encouraged to have physics. I&E and entrepreneurship work lives primarily in Trinity (though Pratt students use it heavily). There is no separate business school admit for undergrads.\n\n**Internal transfer.** Duke does allow internal transfer between Trinity and Pratt after enrollment, but it is competitive. Spring admission does not exist at Duke. There is no honors college with a separate application.\n\n**Interviews.** Duke alumni interviews are optional and invitation-based. Check the appropriate box on the supplement to be considered; you do not request one directly. Interviewers are Duke alumni volunteers who conduct virtual or phone conversations. They do not see your application or academic records and \"cannot predict or influence your chances of admission\" (admissions.duke.edu/alumni-interviews/). The office prioritizes interviews \"for those applicants where additional context is helpful,\" so not receiving one is not a signal of rejection. ED interviews run late October through early November; RD interviews run December through late February. If you do not receive an alumni interview, you may submit a Glimpse video (60–90 seconds, due Nov 9 for ED or Jan 11 for RD). International students can use InitialView instead.\n\n**Legacy.** As of the 2026-27 cycle, Duke has not eliminated legacy preference. Duke's president stated in a 2022 Academic Council address that banning legacy as a factor is \"troublesome,\" explicitly tying the practice to the university's founding family identity. In September 2024, Students for Fair Admissions accused Duke (in a letter to Duke's general counsel) of \"not eliminating legacy admissions,\" indicating legacy remained active for the Class of 2028 cycle. As of November 2025, a Duke Chronicle op-ed by a self-identified legacy student confirmed legacy preference was still in place, calling for its removal. No public announcement of elimination has been made. For Veer: if both parents attended Duke as undergraduates, he would qualify as a legacy applicant. That is a real, if modest, advantage — most credible estimates place the legacy boost at roughly 3–5 percentage points on admit rate, concentrated especially in ED. Legacy is listed as a factor in holistic review, not a guarantee. A weak application does not benefit from it. The strategic implication: legacy + ED + strong application is the strongest possible position.\n\n**Demonstrated interest.** Duke is test-optional and need-blind for domestic applicants. It does not track demonstrated interest in a formal way, but alumni interviews and Glimpse videos are read and do provide supplementary context. Visiting campus or attending info sessions is not tracked.\n\n**Out-of-state.** Duke is a private university; there is no in-state/out-of-state distinction.\n\nSources: admissions.duke.edu/alumni-interviews/ · dukechronicle.com (Nov 27, 2025) · universityherald.com (Sept 20, 2024)\n\n---\n\n## What readers want\n\nDuke's official admissions page describes a \"holistic approach\" considering \"both your academic and personal interests, what you've accomplished, and your unique experiences, perspectives, and background\" (admissions.duke.edu/apply/).\n\nThe key signal from the Duke admissions page and from prep advisors who have worked the process: Duke wants evidence that a student has done real research and can articulate a genuine fit with something specific Duke offers — not rank, not generalities about the Research Triangle. The Why Duke prompt literally tells you this: \"If there is something specific that attracts you to our academic offerings ... feel free to include that, too.\"\n\nWhat that means in practice:\n- Name programs you can actually explain. \"The I&E Certificate\" alone is generic. Explain which pathway (e.g., Entrepreneur/Founder) and why I&E 252 Learning to Fail or the Discover–Develop–Deliver arc lines up with something specific you've built or want to build.\n- The \"Duke family\" culture is real and readers value it. Students who speak to it authentically (not just by saying \"tight-knit\") tend to resonate. The alumni interview is part of this — readers can see whether a student engaged with it.\n- Pratt vs. Trinity is a real choice readers notice. If you say engineering in your application but apply to Trinity, that creates a disconnect. If you name Pratt programs but your coursework shows no calculus or physics, that also creates friction.\n- 250 words is tight. Readers expect students to make every word count. The Communities essay is brand-new for 2026-27 and readers are learning what to expect from it — which means there is no entrenched \"right answer\" yet, but also no mercy for vagueness.\n\nOn essays naming I&E courses: naming specific I&E courses in the Why Duke essay can help if done correctly and hurt if done as a list. The test is whether you can speak to *why* that specific course or program maps to something you've actually done or plan to do. An applicant who can say \"I ran a direct-to-consumer pilot for my product at 15 and want to use I&E 385 Discover alongside Pratt resources to build the hardware layer\" reads as credibly prepared. An applicant who lists I&E 352, the Bullpen, and the Duke Startup Challenge in three disconnected sentences reads as if they copied from a guide. The course names themselves are not magic; the connection to a real story is.\n\nSources: admissions.duke.edu/apply/ · blog.prepscholar.com/why-duke-essay\n\n---\n\n## Admitted essays: what they did\n\n**Note on sources.** The reference file points to College Essay Guy (collegeessayguy.com/blog/duke-supplemental-essay), CollegeVine (blog.collegevine.com/duke-essay-examples), and Clastify (clastify.com/supplement/duke-university). Full annotated examples are at those URLs. The Communities prompt is new for 2026-27 and no admitted examples for it exist in the research — that is noted as not found.\n\n**Why Duke — patterns from admitted essays (pre-2026 prompt wording):**\n\nEssay A (CollegeVine, annotated): A Pratt applicant wrote about a specific professor's computational fluid dynamics research, connected it to a high school robotics project that revealed the limits of intuition-only design, and explained how Pratt's cross-disciplinary labs and the I&E ecosystem would let her pursue both rigor and application. The essay used the first 80 words to establish the specific intellectual gap (fluid simulation in her robot's joint design) and spent the remaining words connecting it to what Duke offered. It avoided ranking, avoided adjectives like \"vibrant\" or \"collaborative,\" and ended with a concrete plan (\"I want to take MEMS 261 and bring that to the I&E 385 Discover track\").\n\nEssay B (College Essay Guy): A Trinity applicant centered entirely on one course he found through a professor's syllabi: a Global Health seminar. He made the connection to his own summer work with a rural health NGO. The essay was 220 words — well under the limit — and worked because it had one clear through-line rather than covering multiple programs.\n\nWhat both essays did: picked one or two things from Duke they could explain with specificity, connected to personal evidence, and did not use the word \"diverse\" or mention Duke's ranking.\n\n**Communities — admitted examples:**\n\nNot found. This is a brand-new 2026-27 prompt. No admitted examples are available in the public record yet. Based on analogous community essays at peer schools: strong versions tend to pick a small, specific community rather than a broad one (\"startup founders at my high school\" rather than \"the entrepreneurship community\"), describe a tension or something unexpected the applicant learned from it, and land on what they will bring to Duke rather than what Duke will do for them.\n\nSources: blog.collegevine.com/duke-essay-examples · collegeessayguy.com/blog/duke-supplemental-essay · clastify.com/supplement/duke-university\n\n---\n\n## What separates strong essays\n\n**Why Duke:**\n- One or two specific things, not a tour of the website.\n- Connection between what Duke offers and something the applicant has actually done, not just what they plan to do.\n- Evidence the applicant went looking — a professor's paper, a specific course syllabus, a conversation with a current student.\n- Personal voice. The 250-word limit forces tightness; readers notice when applicants pad with adjectives.\n\n**Communities:**\n- Specificity of community. Not \"my family\" or \"the Indian-American community.\" A smaller, more defined group tends to work better.\n- An honest observation about how the community shaped the applicant — including tension, friction, or a lesson that surprised them.\n- A forward-looking sentence about what they bring to Duke, not just what they got from the community.\n\n**Optional essays (pick one of three):** Readers use these to fill gaps or add dimensionality. The strongest optional essays either reveal a value or experience not visible elsewhere in the application, or go deeper on something mentioned in activities but not explained.\n\n---\n\n## Clichés and mistakes to avoid\n\n- Mentioning Duke's ranking, its position in the Research Triangle, or using the phrase \"world-class\" without specific content underneath.\n- Listing programs as a bucket list: \"I want to take advantage of the I&E Certificate, the Duke Startup Challenge, the Bullpen, and DukeEngage.\" This reads as a researched list, not a felt connection.\n- Using \"Why Duke\" space to repeat the personal statement.\n- Writing about Cameron Indoor Stadium, the Cameron Crazies, or Duke basketball as the main reason for wanting Duke. Campus culture is fine to mention but cannot substitute for academic fit.\n- Broad community claims in the Communities essay: \"My community is the immigrant experience\" or \"My community is athletes\" without specificity.\n- Name-dropping I&E courses without demonstrating what you know about them. If you haven't read the syllabus or talked to someone who's taken the course, your claim will be thin.\n- Treating the alumni interview as a formality. Alumni interviewers write a report. A student who comes with no questions and answers in monosyllables wastes the opportunity. The interview is conversational — asking real questions about the interviewer's Duke experience is part of the point.\n\n---\n\n## Specifics an applicant can name\n\nThese are tied to entrepreneurship and verifiable. Do not name them without being able to explain what you know about them.\n\n**Courses:**\n- I&E 352: Strategies for Innovation & Entrepreneurship — one of two fixed required I&E Certificate courses. Taught with case studies and guest founders.\n- I&E 252: Learning to Fail — explicit failure-forward design course, unusual and distinctive. Hard to fake familiarity with if you name it.\n- I&E 385 / 395 / 396: Discover, Develop, Deliver — the three-course venture-building sequence for credit. 385 is the entry point.\n- I&E 283: Product Management — useful for tech founders specifically.\n- I&E 360: Entrepreneurial Finance — valuation and fundraising frameworks.\n- I&E 499: The portfolio capstone — the required culminating course for the certificate.\n- I&E 511: Designing Ethical Tech — distinctive because it connects tech ethics to product design.\n\n**Programs and organizations:**\n- I&E Certificate: Available to all majors, six pathways including Entrepreneur/Founder and Designer/Builder.\n- Melissa & Doug Entrepreneurs: Year-long endowed student-founder program, ~10-12 spots, applications in May, requires Duke I&E engagement. Endowed by Melissa Bernstein '87 and Doug Bernstein.\n- The Luby Family Bullpen: I&E's home base in the Fox Student Center at Fuqua, open workspace.\n- John Shen Duke Startup Showcase: Duke's flagship competition (recently renamed/endowed), four-month arc, $30K Borchardt Grand Prize for top undergrad venture, over $110K distributed in 2026.\n- Duke Capital Partners: Angel co-investment fund under the Office for Translation & Commercialization, $89M+ invested since 2015. Student Associate program (~10% acceptance rate) is mostly graduate students but formally open to undergrads.\n- Interplay Fellows Award: New in 2026, $1M gift, up to $40K non-dilutive to one graduating Duke founder per year to go full-time on a venture.\n- Jamie Jones: Current director of Duke I&E; formerly led Rice's Liu Idea Lab and co-founded impact-investing firm Impact Engine — naming him signals real research.\n\n**Watch-outs:**\n- Duke is NOT in the Princeton Review Top 50 for undergraduate entrepreneurship (2026). Don't claim otherwise.\n- There is no standalone undergraduate entrepreneurship major. It is a certificate layered onto a degree.\n\nSources: entrepreneurship.duke.edu · ref/duke.md (verified 2026-27)\n\n---\n\n## Coaching questions\n\nThese are for Veer specifically, pushing past surface answers on the Duke essays.\n\n1. Your parents met at Duke. What do they actually say about it that isn't on the brochure? What has surprised them about how the school changed, or didn't? How does that shape what you want from Duke rather than what they had?\n\n2. If you're applying as a legacy, do you think it should help you? Why or why not — and how does your honest answer to that show up in how you talk about Duke?\n\n3. The Why Duke essay is 250 words. You have room for one or two specific things. What are the two things at Duke you would be most devastated to miss — not because they're impressive, but because they actually match something you've built or a failure you've had?\n\n4. I&E 252 is called \"Learning to Fail.\" Can you describe a real failure in something you've built — not a sports loss, a product or project that didn't work — and explain what you actually learned from it that changed how you built the next thing?\n\n5. The Communities prompt asks what you've learned from a community and what you'll bring to Duke. What's the most unexpected thing your community taught you — something that cut against what you expected?\n\n6. The Discover–Develop–Deliver sequence (I&E 385/395/396) is for building ventures for credit. If you walked into I&E 385 Day 1 with your current idea, what would you say when the instructor asks you what problem you're solving and who has that problem?\n\n7. Duke I&E is cross-campus, not a school. That means your degree still lives in Trinity or Pratt. Which major are you seriously considering and why — and how does the certificate connect to that major's coursework, not replace it?\n\n8. The alumni interview is conversational. What question would you ask a Duke alum who graduated 10 years ago that you genuinely don't know the answer to — not about academics, but about what the experience actually cost them or gave them?\n\n9. The optional essays give you one more shot. What is the most important thing about you that your other essays don't fully explain? Is that more important than using the optional essay for demonstrating fit?\n\n10. Duke is in Durham. The Research Triangle (RTP) is a 20-minute drive. How specifically do you plan to use that geography — internship, co-investment, a specific company, a professor who consults there? Or is it actually not a real part of your plan?\n\n11. You're from Lincolnshire, Illinois — not the South. What specifically draws you to Duke over the Midwestern schools that are geographically closer? What does Duke offer that Northwestern, UChicago, or Michigan don't?\n\n12. 250 words for Why Duke. Write the first sentence right now, out loud. Does it start with \"Duke is...\" or \"I\"? Try again — start with the moment you first realized something specific about Duke.\n\n---\n\n## Sources\n\n- admissions.duke.edu/alumni-interviews/ — official alumni interview page, Duke Undergraduate Admissions (retrieved 2026)\n- admissions.duke.edu/apply/ — application requirements, 2026-27 cycle (verified)\n- dukechronicle.com/article/111925-walley-20251127 — \"Duke, leave legacy behind,\" Harrison Walley, Nov 27, 2025\n- universityherald.com/articles/79294/20240920/duke-university-affirmative-action-supreme-court-violation-asian-students.htm — \"Duke Accused of Not Complying With Supreme Court's Affirmative Action Ruling,\" Sept 20, 2024\n- dukechronicle.com (Sept 6, 2024) — Class of 2028 diversity statistics, legacy and affirmative action context\n- blog.prepscholar.com/why-duke-essay — Why Duke essay tips, PrepScholar (updated 2024)\n- blog.collegevine.com/duke-essay-examples — CollegeVine annotated Duke essays (2023)\n- collegeessayguy.com/blog/duke-supplemental-essay — College Essay Guy Duke supplement guide with full examples\n- clastify.com/supplement/duke-university — Clastify admitted essay database, 30+ Duke essays (2023-25)\n- entrepreneurship.duke.edu — Duke I&E programs, courses, and funding\n- ref/duke.md — verified 2026-27 prompts, rates, and program data\n","nyu":"## Part 1 — Verified application facts (from the Founder's Compass dashboard)\n\n# New York University (id: nyu)\n\nPlatform: Common App | Test: Test-optional | Selectivity: {\"rate\":\"≈8%\",\"tier\":\"Most selective\",\"note\":\"Overall rate is approximate (most recent cycle); Stern admits at a far lower rate. You apply to a specific NYU school/college, not NYU at large.\",\"cycle\":\"prior\"}\n\nDeadlines: [{\"plan\":\"Early Decision I (binding)\",\"date\":\"Nov 1\",\"note\":\"Decisions Dec 15\",\"cycle\":\"ok\"},{\"plan\":\"Early Decision II (binding)\",\"date\":\"Jan 1\",\"note\":\"Decisions Feb 15\",\"cycle\":\"ok\"},{\"plan\":\"Regular Decision\",\"date\":\"Jan 5\",\"note\":\"Decisions Apr 1\",\"cycle\":\"ok\"}]\n\nApp note: NYU uses the Common App: the personal statement (250–650 words) plus one optional NYU supplement. You apply to a specific NYU school/college; there is no separate Stern essay.\n\nEntrepreneurship headline: NYU's edge is New York itself: the NYU Entrepreneurial Institute, Stern's Berkley Center and the Leslie eLab put you inside one of the world's deepest pools of startups, capital and talent.\n\nDegrees: Stern entrepreneurship concentration & Business and Political Economy; university-wide coursework; Tandon and Tisch/Gallatin interdisciplinary venture paths — no single 'entrepreneurship major.'\n\n## Supplements (verified 2026-27 where cycle=ok)\n\n- **The ‘bridge builder’ essay (optional)** [Optional, ≤250 words · answer one or more parts, cycle=ok]: We are looking for students who want to be bridge builders—students who can connect people, groups, and ideas to span divides, foster understanding, and promote collaboration within a dynamic, interconnected, and vibrant global academic community. We are eager for you to tell us how your experiences have helped you understand what qualities and efforts are needed to bridge divides so that people can better learn and work together. Consider one or more of: (1) a time you encountered a perspective different from your own — what did you learn? (2) an experience working with others of different backgrounds — what challenges did the group face and what role did you play? (3) someone you’ve observed who is especially good at helping people think or work together — how do they do it?\n\n## Programs\n- NYU Entrepreneurial Institute: University-wide startup engine — mentorship, the Summer Launchpad accelerator and the flagship Entrepreneurs Challenge.\n- Berkley Center (Stern): Stern's hub for entrepreneurship & innovation; undergraduate entrepreneurship coursework and a concentration.\n- Leslie eLab: Student incubator and maker / co-working space in the Village — open across schools.\n- Tandon Future Labs: Brooklyn-based startup incubators and hardware / engineering venture support.\n\n## Funding/competitions\n- NYU Entrepreneurs Challenge: University-wide competition with $200K+ in prizes across technology, social-venture and new-venture tracks.\n- Summer Launchpad: Cohort accelerator with stipends and a lean-startup curriculum, ending in an investor demo day.\n- eLab programming: Year-round prototyping resources, founder workshops and mentor office hours.\n\n## Watch-outs\n- High sticker price and historically uneven need-based aid — model your real net cost early.\n- Selectivity varies sharply by school: Stern is single-digit and far tougher than NYU overall.\n- No traditional campus — the city is the campus, which suits some students and not others.\n\n## Known successful-essay sources\n- NYU counselors tackle the supplement (official samples) (NYU · meet.nyu.edu) https://meet.nyu.edu/advice/application-tips/nyu-admissions-counselors-tackle-the-2023-24-supplemental-essay/ — 2023–24 · NYU's own counselors model the prompt\n- 3 strong NYU community essay examples (CollegeVine) https://blog.collegevine.com/nyu-diversity-essay-examples — 2023 · real student essays with critique\n- NYU essay examples (and why they worked) (CollegeAdvisor) https://www.collegeadvisor.com/essay-guides/nyu-essay-examples-and-why-they-worked/ — 2021–22 · admitted essays with ex-admissions-officer notes\n\n## Other fields\n- short: NYU\n- mono: N\n- city: New York\n- state: NY\n- type: Private\n- setting: Urban · Greenwich Village\n- undergrad: ≈29,000\n- platform: Common App\n- ac: #57068C\n- ac2: #330662\n- fit: 87\n- verdict: Excellent fit\n- sub: {\"program\":8,\"experiential\":7,\"outcomes\":8,\"location\":10}\n- rateNum: 8\n- dlSort: 1101\n- testPolicy: Test-optional\n- sat: ≈1480–1570\n- act: ≈33–35\n- fee: $85\n- recs: 1 counselor + 1 teacher\n- opens: August 1\n- bestFor: Founders who want to build in New York — plugging into Stern, the Leslie eLab and the NYU Entrepreneurial Institute alongside one of the densest startup, finance and media ecosystems on earth. Especially strong for fintech, consumer, media-tech and hardware (via Tandon).\n- strengths: [\"A global brand with top programs in business (Stern), the arts (Tisch) and a fast-rising engineering school (Tandon).\",\"Greenwich Village location wires students straight into NYC venture capital, startups and industry.\",\"Apply-by-school structure lets you target a focused community (Stern, Tandon, CAS, Gallatin).\",\"Strong international footprint — NYU Abu Dhabi, Shanghai and global academic centers.\"]\n- entreRank: Frequently cited among top entrepreneurship programs; exact 2026 national rank not confirmed\n- founders: An enormous NYC alumni base across fintech, media and consumer tech; the city itself acts as an extended network of investors, operators and internships.\n- optIntro: \n- cost: {\"mode\":\"private\",\"sticker\":\"≈$93,000–$95,000 / yr\",\"aid\":\"Aid has expanded recently (need-based commitments plus AccessNYU scholarships), but NYU is historically less generous than peer privates — run the net-price calculator and apply early for scholarships.\"}\n- cardEnt: NYC ecosystem · Entrepreneurial Institute + Stern Berkley Center + Leslie eLab.\n- src: Sourced from nyu.edu admissions (deadlines live for the upcoming cycle), the NYU Entrepreneurial Institute, Stern Berkley Center and Leslie eLab pages. Overall acceptance rate and score bands are approximate from the most recent cycle. Supplemental prompt confirmed 2026-27 on the official NYU Admissions blog (meet.nyu.edu, July 29, 2026): the bridge-builder prompt continues unchanged for this cycle.\n\n## Part 2 — Deep research: paths in, what readers want, admitted essays\n\n# NYU — Knowledge Base\n\n## Paths in\n\n### Overall selectivity\nNYU received 110,807 applications for Fall 2025 and admitted 10,232, a 9.2% overall rate (CDS 2024-25, https://www.collegedata.fyi/schools/nyu/2024-25). The ref file lists 8% for the prior cycle; the 9.2% figure is the most recent CDS number. The two are close enough that the real answer is: NYU sits in the same tier as Penn or Cornell.\n\n### By school — the gap is large\nNYU is not one pool. You apply to a specific college, and each runs its own admissions. According to College Transitions (https://www.collegetransitions.com/blog/nyu-inside-the-numbers/, June 2026), **three NYU colleges now admit fewer than 5% of applicants — the College of Arts and Science (CAS), the Stern School of Business, and Rory Meyers College of Nursing — a rate in the range of Yale or Princeton overall.** No exact per-school figures are published in the CDS; NYU discloses only the aggregate.\n\nPractical hierarchy for an entrepreneurship applicant:\n- **Stern School of Business** — most selective, sub-5%. If you are applying specifically for the undergraduate business degree and entrepreneurship concentration, this is the direct path but the hardest gate.\n- **College of Arts and Science (CAS)** — sub-5%. Large liberal arts college; feeds the Entrepreneurs Challenge and eLab. You can study economics, computer science, or interdisciplinary tracks and still access all university-wide entrepreneurship resources.\n- **Tandon School of Engineering (Brooklyn)** — separate campus in Brooklyn. Engineering-focused, more accessible than Stern or CAS. Has its own Future Labs incubators. A viable path for tech founders (product engineering, CS, data science) who want NYU's brand and startup ecosystem without competing against the Stern pool.\n- **Gallatin School of Individualized Study** — students design their own concentrations by combining courses across NYU. Admit rate is estimated as more accessible than Stern/CAS, though not published. Attracts interdisciplinary thinkers. No business-specific curriculum, but students regularly combine social entrepreneurship, policy, and tech.\n- **Global Liberal Studies (GLS / Liberal Studies Core)** — a two-year interdisciplinary core program. Students complete the core and then transition to a bachelor's program at NYU New York, Abu Dhabi, or Shanghai. Sometimes used by NYU as a \"soft redirect\" for applicants to more selective schools.\n\nKey point: **Gallatin and GLS admit at meaningfully higher rates than Stern or CAS.** Exact numbers are not confirmed by official sources — treat that as directional, not precise.\n\n### Early Decision I and Early Decision II\nNYU offers two binding early rounds:\n- **ED I**: deadline Nov 1, decisions Dec 15.\n- **ED II**: deadline Jan 1, decisions Feb 15.\n- **RD**: deadline Jan 5, decisions Apr 1.\n\nNYU is unusual: it does not disclose ED application or admit counts in its CDS. The fields exist in Section C21 but are left blank. There is no verified, official ED vs. RD rate gap to cite. Counselors widely believe ED carries an advantage (binding commitment raises yield), but no confirmed percentage is available. If a source claims an exact ED rate, treat it with caution — it is not from NYU's own disclosures.\n\nED II is a legitimate second shot at binding admission, useful for students who were not ready in November or who were deferred/not admitted EA elsewhere. The pool is smaller but still competitive.\n\n### Being admitted to a different school or campus\nNYU sometimes admits applicants to a program other than the one they named — most commonly:\n- **Liberal Studies Core** instead of CAS or Stern. A student hoping for CAS or Stern may receive an offer from Liberal Studies, which is a two-year program that then feeds into degree programs. Accepting this is not the same as being admitted to your first-choice school; it is a separate track with transition requirements.\n- **NYU Abu Dhabi or NYU Shanghai** instead of NYU New York. These are full-degree campuses with their own admissions pools, generous financial aid, and strong academics, but different experiences from Greenwich Village. Some applicants who applied to New York are offered admission to a global campus. This is not a backup or consolation — NYU treats these as distinct programs — but it is worth knowing the possibility exists.\n- Officially, NYU recommends students indicate their top-choice school and they are reviewed for that school. There is no formal cascading into lower-admit-rate schools.\n\n### Other paths\n- **Internal transfer** between NYU schools is possible but not guaranteed and varies by program. Moving from GLS or CAS to Stern, for example, requires meeting Stern's internal transfer requirements.\n- **Spring admission**: NYU does offer spring entry for some programs.\n- **No interviews**, no portfolio requirements for most schools (Tisch is an exception).\n- **Demonstrated interest**: NYU does not track demonstrated interest. Campus visits and info sessions do not move the needle.\n- **Legacy**: No published legacy preference. Unlike Duke, legacy is not a confirmed factor at NYU.\n- **Test-optional**: Confirmed test-optional. Middle 50% for submitters: SAT 1480-1550, ACT 34-35 (CDS 2024-25).\n\n---\n\n## What readers want\n\nNYU's official admissions blog (meet.nyu.edu) describes the bridge-builder prompt as looking for students who can \"connect people, groups, and ideas to span divides, foster understanding, and promote collaboration within a dynamic, interconnected, and vibrant global academic community.\" Source: https://meet.nyu.edu/advice/application-tips/nyu-admissions-counselors-tackle-the-2023-24-supplemental-essay/\n\nCollege Transitions (June 2026) reports that NYU has \"elevated essays, recommendations, and character in its admissions rubric while dropping class rank entirely.\" This tracks with the supplement's intent: NYU wants students who have something to give to a global community, not students who are just accomplished. Source: https://www.collegetransitions.com/blog/nyu-inside-the-numbers/\n\nFrom the CollegeAdvisor ex-admissions officer note on a Stern essay: \"These essays work best when the reader can feel the student's conviction and enthusiasm. Admissions officers appreciate when the reader can easily see the impact the student will have on the school community.\" Source: https://www.collegeadvisor.com/essay-guides/nyu-essay-examples-and-why-they-worked/\n\nThe supplement asks students to tie their bridge-building experience to NYU specifically. Admissions counselors' own model essays (meet.nyu.edu) show three valid approaches:\n1. Broad, values-driven (Jimmy's version: community engagement, sustainable design).\n2. Personal tension (Ayham's version: tradition vs. change, desire for growth).\n3. Specific moment of curiosity about another person (Bridget's version: sibling insight, curiosity extended to the world).\n\nAll three are acceptable framings. The counselors' own examples are not granular about specific NYU programs. That means the supplement is primarily about character and bridging, not a research essay. However, grounding the conclusion in a specific NYU resource (a lab, a program, an organization) still differentiates the essay.\n\n---\n\n## Admitted essays: what they did\n\n### Essay 1 — Stern applicant, actuarial science (CollegeAdvisor)\n**Angle**: Math-minded finance student who wants to combine actuarial science with a global business degree.\n**Structure**: Opens with origin story (calculating sales tax, probability as a child), narrows to career interest (actuary/risk management), then names exactly what Stern offers that no other school does for this path.\n**Specific details used**: Named STAT-UB 21 \"Introduction to Stochastic Processes\" and STAT-UB 15 \"Statistical Inference and Regression Analysis\" as the coursework that excited him. Named the International Business Exchange Program. Named the BS in Business Program's global perspective emphasis.\n**Why it worked** (ex-AO quote): \"It is clear that the student's declared interests are, in fact, in line with both the student's background and experiences, as well as in line with what the college has to offer.\" Conviction came through because the student named the exact curriculum, not just the school's reputation.\n**Source**: https://www.collegeadvisor.com/essay-guides/nyu-essay-examples-and-why-they-worked/\n\n### Essay 2 — CAS applicant, public health / social advocacy (CollegeAdvisor)\n**Angle**: Internship at the International Rescue Committee (Lower East Side, refugee youth program), centered on a specific child named Mahdi.\n**Structure**: Opens with the training and then the gap between the traumatic expectation and the actual bubbly child. Builds emotional stakes around the child's future in America. Ends by naming specific NYU resources that connect.\n**Specific details used**: CASSR (Center for Anti-Racism, Social Justice, and Public Health) research, courses named (Social Policy in Modern Societies; Race and Ethnicity), student organization doing Washington Square Park post-its activism. Location used concretely: transferred at West 4th from A to M train, walked around Washington Square Park.\n**Why it worked**: The essay made New York the setting, not the draw. By grounding the story in actual NYC geography and an actual internship, the applicant showed she already understood what NYU's city campus means. The NYU specifics she named came directly from the experience, not from a list.\n**Source**: https://www.collegeadvisor.com/essay-guides/nyu-essay-examples-and-why-they-worked/\n\n### Essay 3 — NYU counselor model essay (Bridget Halstead, NYU admissions staff)\n**Angle**: Growing up watching the same movies with siblings every Friday. Discovery that her younger sister was a full person with independent thoughts, not a \"mini-me.\"\n**Structure**: Opens with the Will Ferrell line (\"Everybody wave goodbye to Juice Box!\"), acknowledges the weirdness of the opening, then shows what the moment revealed about her assumptions. Extends the insight — everyone has a unique perspective worth discovering — into college.\n**Why it works as a model**: Shows that the supplement does not have to involve big multicultural experiences. A domestic, small-scale observation counts if it reveals a genuine habit of mind. The counselors are telling applicants that the bridge-building prompt is about orientation toward people, not about résumé items.\n**Source**: https://meet.nyu.edu/advice/application-tips/nyu-admissions-counselors-tackle-the-2023-24-supplemental-essay/\n\n### Essay 4 — Intercontinental debate organizer (submitted by applicant, essayforum.com)\n**Angle**: African debater who noticed that international debate circuits excluded students from underfunded countries. Organized a free, open, international Moot Court tournament.\n**Specific details used**: Youth participants from Kazakhstan, South Africa, Morocco, and the Maldives. Named NYU CEDA's partnerships with IPPF and Brooklyn Public Library's Bridging the Gap. Also mentioned wanting to add to Strawberry Fest.\n**Note from reviewer**: The baking festival mention was called out as disconnected and diluting the main narrative. Lesson: do not add a second NYU hook if it is not earned by the story.\n**Source**: https://essayforum.com/undergraduate/bridge-builder-high-school-word-limit-nyu-96770/\n\n---\n\n## What separates strong essays\n\n**Strong essays do these things:**\n- Pick one concrete moment or action and stay there. They do not survey a long career of bridge-building.\n- Name NYU by school. \"At NYU Stern\" or \"through the Leslie eLab\" is more credible than \"at NYU.\"\n- Use New York as a character, not a backdrop. The city is the campus; applicants who cite specific streets, subways, neighborhoods, or institutions show they understand what NYU actually is.\n- Connect bridge-building to the entrepreneurship work concretely. Building across groups — investors and communities, engineers and social workers, disciplines — is genuinely a founder skill. Make that explicit.\n- Write toward one or two parts of the multi-part prompt, not all four. Trying to answer every sub-question produces a list, not an essay.\n\n**Patterns from admitted essays:**\n- Highly specific academic coursework named (actual course numbers or names, not just \"business courses\").\n- A real experience outside the essay that the school can verify — internships, research, organized events.\n- Emotional stakes that arise from the story, not stated in the abstract.\n- The NYU conclusion is earned by the narrative, not appended at the end.\n\n---\n\n## Clichés and mistakes to avoid\n\n- **\"NYU's diverse community\"** with no specifics — every applicant says this.\n- **\"I love New York City\"** — treating NYC as a tourist attraction rather than showing you know how to use it.\n- **Listing rankings** — calling NYU \"a top-ranked school\" reads as filler and slightly wrong (NYU ranks outside the top 25 overall but top 5 in some programs).\n- **\"I founded a startup\"** résumé dump — the supplement is 250 words. It cannot hold a venture history. Choose one bridge-building act.\n- **Answering all four sub-questions** — the prompt says \"consider one or more.\" Answering all four produces a list.\n- **Generic \"bridge-building\" language** — \"I love connecting people\" tells the reader nothing without a specific scene.\n- **Applying to Stern as a backup to Wharton** — Stern is sub-5% and just as hard. It is not a safety from Penn.\n- **Misidentifying which school offers what**: Tandon is engineering (Brooklyn campus), Gallatin is individualized study, GLS is a core program that transitions into a degree. Do not conflate them.\n- **Assuming Liberal Studies is a consolation prize** — it is a real two-year program with a distinct curriculum. Accepting it blindly without understanding the transition requirements is a mistake.\n\n---\n\n## Specifics an applicant can name\n\n### For Stern / entrepreneurship focus\n- **Berkley Center for Entrepreneurship & Innovation** — Stern's hub; undergraduate entrepreneurship concentration and coursework.\n- **Leslie eLab** — university-wide student incubator in the Village, maker/co-working space.\n- **NYU Entrepreneurs Challenge** — university-wide competition, $200K+ in prizes across technology, social-venture, and new-venture tracks.\n- **Summer Launchpad** — cohort accelerator with stipends, lean-startup curriculum, investor demo day.\n- **STAT-UB 21** \"Introduction to Stochastic Processes\" and **STAT-UB 15** \"Statistical Inference and Regression Analysis\" — Stern quantitative courses.\n- **International Business Exchange Program** — Stern's global study opportunity.\n- **Business and Political Economy** (BPE) — Stern interdisciplinary program combining business with economics, politics, and law.\n\n### For CAS / interdisciplinary focus\n- **NYU Entrepreneurial Institute** — university-wide, runs Launchpad and Entrepreneurs Challenge.\n- Courses in Social Policy in Modern Societies; Race and Ethnicity.\n- **CASSR** (Center for Anti-Racism, Social Justice, and Public Health).\n- Washington Square Park as campus center — specific geography that admitted students name.\n\n### For Tandon (engineering / tech ventures)\n- **Tandon Future Labs** — Brooklyn-based startup incubators; hardware and engineering ventures.\n- Strong CS and data science programs with engineering venture support.\n\n### For Gallatin\n- Design your own concentration (specifically state the proposed combination).\n- Interdisciplinary seminars; senior colloquium project.\n- Collaborative across NYU schools.\n\n### Commonly misnamed\n- \"NYU's startup center\" — the correct name is the NYU Entrepreneurial Institute or the Leslie eLab.\n- \"Stern's incubator\" — the university-wide eLab is not Stern-only.\n- GLS and Liberal Studies are used interchangeably; officially the program is the Global Liberal Studies program.\n\n---\n\n## Coaching questions\n\n1. When you say you want to build ventures at NYU, which school are you actually applying to — Stern, CAS, Gallatin, or Tandon? What does each one give you that the others do not? Pick one and defend it.\n\n2. The supplement asks for a bridge-builder. Not a founder. Not a leader. A bridge-builder. What is the most specific single moment where you connected two groups or perspectives that were not connecting on their own?\n\n3. In the strong Stern essay, the applicant named actual course numbers. What specific NYU courses, programs, or labs would you name — and how did you find them? (Not from rankings pages — from the actual catalog or alumni.)\n\n4. NYU's campus is New York City. In your essay, does New York City appear as a specific place — a neighborhood, a subway line, an institution, a type of person — or as an abstract concept called \"New York\"? Push for a specific.\n\n5. You want to build ventures. How does building across groups — investors and community, tech and social sector, disciplines — connect to what you've already done? What is your actual bridge-building track record, not your aspirational one?\n\n6. Stern admits fewer than 5% of applicants. If you're applying to Stern, what is your case for being in that 5%? What does your quantitative background show? What have you built?\n\n7. NYU doesn't track demonstrated interest. So the only place to show you know NYU is the essay. What do you know about NYU that a student who just Googled it would not know?\n\n8. The supplement is optional. Why would you submit it? What does it add to your application that the personal statement does not already show?\n\n9. The Liberal Studies / GLS offer is a real possibility if you apply to Stern or CAS. Have you looked at what the transition requirements are? How would you feel about that outcome? (This tests whether they actually understand the school structure.)\n\n10. If you were admitted to NYU Shanghai instead of NYU New York, would you go? What does your answer say about why you want NYU specifically vs. a city-campus experience generally?\n\n11. The bridge-builder prompt gives four sub-questions. Which one actually fits your story, and which ones are you adding just to seem thorough? Cut everything that does not fit the one honest answer.\n\n12. What does your personal statement already cover? The supplement is 250 words — what is genuinely new information for a reader who just finished your main essay?\n\n---\n\n## Sources\n\n| Source | URL | Date | What it covers |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| NYU CDS 2024-25 via collegedata.fyi | https://www.collegedata.fyi/schools/nyu/2024-25 | Apr 2025 | Overall admit rate 9.2%, score bands, ED fields (blank) |\n| College Transitions — NYU inside the numbers | https://www.collegetransitions.com/blog/nyu-inside-the-numbers/ | Jun 2026 | Stern/CAS/Nursing sub-5% claim, trend data |\n| NYU admissions counselors tackle the supplement | https://meet.nyu.edu/advice/application-tips/nyu-admissions-counselors-tackle-the-2023-24-supplemental-essay/ | Dec 2023 | Official counselor model essays for bridge-builder prompt |\n| CollegeAdvisor — NYU essay examples and why they worked | https://www.collegeadvisor.com/essay-guides/nyu-essay-examples-and-why-they-worked/ | 2021-22 | Admitted essays with ex-AO commentary |\n| CollegeVine — 3 NYU diversity essay examples | https://blog.collegevine.com/nyu-diversity-essay-examples | 2023 | Admitted essays for older diversity prompt (different from current bridge-builder) |\n| Empowerly — NYU supplemental essays guide | https://empowerly.com/applications/nyu-supplemental-essays/ | Nov 2024 | Bridge-builder prompt text (2024-25 version), strategies |\n| EssayForum — bridge-builder draft | https://essayforum.com/undergraduate/bridge-builder-high-school-word-limit-nyu-96770/ | Nov 2024 | Draft essay with peer and consultant feedback |\n| Liberal Studies advising page | https://liberalstudies.nyu.edu/academic-advising/core-program-advising.html | — | Transition process from LS Core to bachelor's programs |\n| Clastify bridge-builder examples | https://www.clastify.com/supplement/nyu/bridge-builder | 2025 | Index of successful NYU bridge-builder essays |\n\n**Not found**: Official NYU per-school admit rates; confirmed ED I vs. ED II rate comparison (NYU does not publish these). Official legacy policy statement. Confirmed admit rate for Gallatin and GLS separately.\n","uchicago":"## Part 1 — Verified application facts (from the Founder's Compass dashboard)\n\n# University of Chicago (id: uchicago)\n\nPlatform: Common App / Coalition | Test: Test-optional | Selectivity: {\"rate\":\"4.5%\",\"tier\":\"Most selective\",\"note\":\"CDS 2024-25 (43,612 applicants, 1,955 admitted). Genuinely test-optional — only ~49% of enrolled students submitted scores.\",\"cycle\":\"prior\"}\n\nDeadlines: [{\"plan\":\"Early Action (non-binding)\",\"date\":\"Nov 2\",\"note\":\"Decisions mid-December; reply by May 1\",\"cycle\":\"ok\"},{\"plan\":\"Early Decision I (binding)\",\"date\":\"Nov 2\",\"note\":\"Decisions mid-December; reply mid-January\",\"cycle\":\"ok\"},{\"plan\":\"Early Decision II (binding)\",\"date\":\"Jan 4\",\"note\":\"Decisions mid-February; reply mid-March\",\"cycle\":\"ok\"},{\"plan\":\"Regular Decision\",\"date\":\"Jan 4\",\"note\":\"Decisions late March; reply by May 1\",\"cycle\":\"ok\"}]\n\nApp note: UChicago accepts the Common App or Coalition: the personal statement (250–650 words) plus two required UChicago essays — the ‘Why UChicago’ essay and one famously creative extended essay.\n\nEntrepreneurship headline: The Polsky Center pairs a Booth-taught entrepreneurship minor with the New Venture Challenge — a top-ranked accelerator that writes real equity checks to student teams, not just prize money.\n\nDegrees: Entrepreneurship minor (Booth-taught) · pairs naturally with Economics, Computer Science, Statistics or Molecular Engineering\n\n## Supplements (verified 2026-27 where cycle=ok)\nTwo required essays, officially confirmed for 2026-27 on Jun 30. The extended essay has no hard word limit (~500–700 words) — pick one prompt below.\n\n- **Why UChicago? (required)** [Required, ~300–600 words, cycle=ok]: How does the University of Chicago, as you know it now, satisfy your desire for a particular kind of learning, community, and future? Please address with some specificity your own wishes and how they relate to UChicago.\n- **Extended essay 1 — how do thoughts eat?** [Extended · pick 1, no hard limit, cycle=ok]: Food for thought: How do thoughts eat? (Inspired by Ernest Leong, Class of 2026.)\n- **Extended essay 2 — origami-inspired solutions** [Extended · pick 1, no hard limit, cycle=ok]: The James Webb Space Telescope, launched in 2021, utilized origami-inspired techniques to compactly store and deploy its sunshield in space. Choose an artistic practice and use its principles to propose an elegant solution to a problem. (Inspired by Akhil Korra, Class of 2028.)\n- **Extended essay 3 — mixed metaphors** [Extended · pick 1, no hard limit, cycle=ok]: Sometimes metaphors get mixed up. For one reason or another, one could say, “we'll burn that bridge when we get there” or “the world is your china shop.” Make up your own mixed metaphor. Explain how it could make sense, be understood, or even applied. (Inspired by Julia Nieberg, Class of 2029.)\n- **Extended essay 4 — mundane Olympics** [Extended · pick 1, no hard limit, cycle=ok]: The Olympics have long celebrated the pinnacle of human athletic achievement. But what if they expanded to honor the mundane? Imagine a new Olympic event built around an everyday activity like speed dishwashing or competitive grocery bagging. How is it scored, officiated, and judged? Why is it a worthy addition? (Inspired by Ewan Smith, Class of 2028.)\n- **Extended essay 5 — Mistaken Identity of Acronym** [Extended · pick 1, no hard limit, cycle=ok]: AI: Allen Iverson. NASA, or the North American Saxophone Alliance. Share a potentially confounding, comedic, or captivating example of MIA (Mistaken Identity of Acronym) and tell us its backstory. (Inspired by Jeeho Byun, Class of 2030.)\n- **Extended essay 6 — choose your own adventure** [Extended · pick 1, no hard limit, cycle=ok]: In the spirit of adventurous inquiry, choose one of our past prompts (or create a question of your own). Be original, creative, thought provoking. Draw on your best qualities as a writer, thinker, visionary, social critic, sage, citizen of the world, or future citizen of the University of Chicago; take a little risk, and have fun!\n\n## Programs\n- Polsky Center: UChicago's entrepreneurship & innovation hub spanning the College, Booth and the wider university.\n- Entrepreneurship minor: A College minor taught with Booth faculty — unusually deep access to a top-5 business school as an undergrad.\n- New Venture Challenge (NVC): Flagship accelerator with a dedicated College NVC track and a Social NVC — culminating in real investment.\n- Polsky Exchange: Co-working, incubation and a fabrication lab supporting early-stage student ventures.\n\n## Funding/competitions\n- NVC investment pool: $150,000+ invested in 2026 finalists — equity into student companies, not just prizes.\n- George Shultz Innovation Fund: Student-driven venture fund investing in university-affiliated deep-tech and science startups.\n- College NVC track: Undergraduate-specific New Venture Challenge pathway with mentorship and seed capital.\n\n## Watch-outs\n- Intense, theory-forward academic culture; the workload can crowd out building time.\n- The famously creative supplemental essays demand serious effort.\n- Strengths skew analytical/financial — less of a scrappy 'maker' vibe than some peers.\n\n## Known successful-essay sources\n- UChicago extended essays — guide + examples (College Essay Guy) https://www.collegeessayguy.com/blog/uchicago-extended-essay — full examples for the famously quirky extended essays\n- 2 great UChicago essay examples (CollegeVine) https://blog.collegevine.com/uchicago-essay-example — 2022 · real extended-essay responses, annotated\n- UChicago essay examples (and why they worked) (CollegeAdvisor) https://www.collegeadvisor.com/essay-guides/uchicago-essay-examples/ — 2023–24 · admitted essays with ex-AO commentary\n\n## Other fields\n- short: UChicago\n- mono: U\n- city: Chicago\n- state: IL\n- type: Private\n- setting: Urban · Hyde Park\n- undergrad: ≈7,560\n- platform: Common App / Coalition\n- ac: #7A0019\n- ac2: #530011\n- fit: 85\n- verdict: Excellent fit\n- sub: {\"program\":8,\"experiential\":8,\"outcomes\":8,\"location\":7}\n- rateNum: 4.5\n- dlSort: 1103\n- testPolicy: Test-optional\n- sat: 1510–1560\n- act: 34–35\n- fee: $75\n- recs: 1 counselor + 2 teachers\n- opens: August 1\n- bestFor: Analytically-minded founders — fintech, data, deep tech, econ-driven ventures — who want a real equity-investing accelerator (the New Venture Challenge) and a rare Booth-taught entrepreneurship minor on top of the most rigorous quantitative core in the country.\n- strengths: [\"World #1 economics, plus elite finance, mathematics, statistics and molecular engineering.\",\"The Core curriculum builds exceptional analytical range.\",\"Polsky's New Venture Challenge is one of the most successful university accelerators anywhere.\",\"Strong Chicago ecosystem and a powerful finance / consulting / tech placement pipeline.\"]\n- entreRank: Polsky's New Venture Challenge ranks among the top U.S. university accelerators\n- founders: NVC alumni include Grubhub (IPO) and Braintree/Venmo (acquired by PayPal) — a rare track record of billion-dollar outcomes for a university accelerator, plus a dense finance/quant founder network.\n- optIntro: Two required essays, officially confirmed for 2026-27 on Jun 30. The extended essay has no hard word limit (~500–700 words) — pick one prompt below.\n- cost: {\"mode\":\"private\",\"sticker\":\"≈$98,300 / yr (before aid)\",\"aid\":\"Meets full demonstrated need, no loans. Confirmed May 13, 2026: starting Autumn 2027 — exactly the Fall 2027 entry cycle this dashboard tracks — UChicago doubles its free-tuition income cap to $250,000/yr (with typical assets), one of the highest of any major private university; families under $125,000 also get free housing, meals and fees covered. FAFSA/CSS Profile still required.\"}\n- cardEnt: New Venture Challenge (real equity) · Booth-taught entrepreneurship minor.\n- src: Sourced from collegeadmissions.uchicago.edu — 2026-27 deadlines and essay prompts confirmed directly on the official site (verified Jul 2, 2026), CDS 2024-25 for admit-rate data, and polsky.uchicago.edu. The extended-essay prompts rotate yearly; five new ones were just posted for this cycle.\n\n## Part 2 — Deep research: paths in, what readers want, admitted essays\n\n# University of Chicago — Advisor Knowledge Base\n\n## Paths in\n\n**Admission plans.** UChicago offers four plans:\n- Early Decision I (binding): Nov 2 deadline, mid-December decisions, reply by mid-January.\n- Early Action (non-binding): Nov 2 deadline, mid-December decisions, reply by May 1.\n- Early Decision II (binding): Jan 4 deadline, mid-February decisions, reply by mid-March.\n- Regular Decision: Jan 4 deadline, late March decisions, reply by May 1.\n\n**The real advantage of applying early.** UChicago does not publish early-round admit rates. Independent advisors (Koppelman Group, May 2025) estimate combined early pools (ED I + EA + ED II) admit at 20–30%, compared to the overall 4.5% rate. The same source estimates that \"over two-thirds of the incoming class\" comes from early applicant pools, and reports a 99% admit rate among their own early applicants — a figure that reflects selective client pools but confirms the strategic weight of early application. UChicago's yield rate is approximately 86% (highest among top-50 schools tracked), which is why the school invests heavily in binding applications. Applying ED I is the strongest signal of commitment and carries the largest advantage. ED II offers a second binding option and likely a meaningful advantage over RD, though smaller than ED I.\n\n**No published ED II rate.** The rate for ED II specifically is not found in any official source. UChicago's own site says \"your application will go through the same careful review process no matter which of these plans you choose\" — a standard disclaimer. In practice, ED II applicants who are strong but missed the ED I deadline benefit from the binding commitment signal. For applicants who receive disappointing results from other schools in December, ED II is a legitimate strategic option.\n\n**Which school / program.** UChicago is a unified College — there is no separate school to apply to (unlike Stern/CAS or Haas). All undergraduates enter through the College. Entrepreneurship is delivered through the Polsky Center and a minor taught by Booth faculty. There is no direct-admit undergraduate business track. Majors tend to be in Economics, Computer Science, Statistics, Mathematics, or Molecular Engineering for analytically-oriented founders. The Entrepreneurship minor (Booth-taught) is the flagship credential and is unusually strong for an undergraduate program.\n\n**Internal transfer.** UChicago accepts internal transfer between majors easily; the structure is flexible. Transfer admission from other institutions is possible but rare. Spring admission is not offered for first-year students.\n\n**No traditional interview.** UChicago does not conduct admissions interviews. The optional Video Profile (described below under Optional Materials) is the substitute.\n\n**Demonstrated interest.** UChicago is test-optional with a genuine \"no-harm\" policy (only ~49% of enrolled students submitted scores). Demonstrated interest is not formally tracked, but applying ED or EA signals commitment, and the Video Profile is one more way to show it.\n\nSources: collegeadmissions.uchicago.edu/apply/ · koppelmangroup.com (May 2025) · ref/uchicago.md\n\n---\n\n## What readers want\n\nThe official UChicago admissions page states: \"We care about you and who you really are, not what you think we want to see in an application.\" (collegeadmissions.uchicago.edu/apply/) That line is not boilerplate — it describes a genuine institutional preference for applicants who present an authentic intellectual identity rather than a packaged version of what UChicago supposedly wants.\n\nPeter Wilson, UChicago's director of undergraduate admissions, explained the purpose of the extended essay: \"When students come to the College, they will invariably be met with a situation or problem they have never encountered before. This prompt gives Admissions counselors a way to evaluate how students will navigate those situations once they are at UChicago.\" (news.uchicago.edu, Aug 2021)\n\nGrace Chapin James, former eight-year chair of the essay committee: \"When we give people these creative questions, the goal is to open up their minds and see if they have a level of creativity or flexibility and exploration that will lend itself well to being in a UChicago classroom.\" (news.uchicago.edu, Aug 2021)\n\nDean of the College John Boyer: The extended essay invites prospective students \"to be creative in a serious but intellectually playful way in response to an open-ended challenge.\" It also \"show[s] our students something of the intellectually dynamic culture they will be joining.\" (news.uchicago.edu, Aug 2021)\n\nWhat this means operationally:\n- Readers are looking for genuine intellectual curiosity, not a performance of it.\n- The extended essay is specifically designed to reveal how a student thinks under ambiguity, not what they know.\n- The Why UChicago essay demands specific, personal language — not rankings, not \"the Core,\" not \"Hyde Park neighborhood.\" Readers want evidence a student has engaged with the actual intellectual culture of the school.\n- Originality in the extended essay is rewarded, but clarity is required. The goal is a memorable answer that is unambiguously the applicant's own.\n\nSources: collegeadmissions.uchicago.edu/apply/ · news.uchicago.edu/story/unique-uchicago-essay-questions-spark-students-creativity (Aug 2021)\n\n---\n\n## Admitted essays: what they did\n\n**Essay 1 — Xander Hastings (Economics/Astrophysics, Class of 2023, via AdmitRaven):**\n\nXander scrapped months of essay drafts one week before the ED deadline. He rewrote his extended essay in Spenserian sonnets — strict iambic pentameter — turning the prompt itself into both the subject and the structure of his answer. He was \"scared to scrap so many months of progress\" but found the sonnets felt more authentic: \"as I began coming up with rhymes and doing syllable scansion, I completely came alive. It was so much more me.\" His only rule was clarity: \"as long as people could understand it, that's all I cared about.\" He was not trying to impress for its own sake; the form was a signal of who he is. The Why UChicago essay used a Pac-Man metaphor to describe his campus visit: \"The UChicago campus had become a game of Pac-Man for me, and all the white dots that I had to gobble up were irresistibly intriguing traditions, powerful resources, and intellectual stimulations to fall in love with.\" He also described a physics professor stopping him on the street to discuss local politics, then asking what year he was — \"and I lit up like a firecracker.\" The essay showed the experience of already belonging, not just wanting to belong. Xander's advice: \"Surprise them. Entertain them. Teach them something you've learned. Don't be afraid to go over the top — embrace the weird within you. Your voice is the only thing that can't be replicated.\" Source: admitraven.com/en/case-studies/cdd76171-e4ae-44be-bd7f-c3fb7d747074 (May 2025)\n\n**What made it work:** The sonnet form was audacious but executed at a high level — clarity was maintained. The Why UChicago essay was specific down to sensory detail (the Pac-Man metaphor, the professor encounter) and demonstrated prior genuine campus engagement. Neither essay summarized his résumé. Both showed how he thinks, not just what he's done.\n\n**Essay 2 — Noah Adams (via noahnomad.com, enrolled UChicago, 2017):**\n\nNoah picked the prompt \"What is square one and can you go back to it?\" He wrote about returning home to Pittsburgh after a year studying abroad in Germany. The structure was unexpected: Germany — on paper the unfamiliar place — became his \"square one,\" while Pittsburgh — his literal home — did not. He used three specific moments of adaptation in Germany (public transit, a failed exam, a misunderstanding with his host family about a closed bedroom door) to argue that discomfort is where he finds himself most authentically. He ended not with a generic \"UChicago will challenge me\" statement, but with: \"I am looking forward to the discomfort I will find as an undergraduate and welcome the opportunity to prove to myself that I can overcome it. I want to get back to square one.\" Source: noahnomad.com/blog/my-uchicago-extended-essay (2017)\n\n**What made it work:** The essay inverted the expected answer (home = square one). It used three very specific personal examples rather than abstract claims. The connection to UChicago came from the internal logic of the essay, not an appended paragraph about the school.\n\n**Pattern from admitted UChicago extended essays generally (from uchicago.edu/apply/essay/ and essay committee sources):**\n- Students who succeeded picked one of the six options that gave them the most to say — not the one that sounded most impressive.\n- Past prompts used by admitted students via the \"choose your own adventure\" option include highly philosophical, absurdist, or field-specific angles: ship of Theseus, color-infused expressions, vestigial structures, portals.\n- The essays that worked took a genuine intellectual risk — a non-obvious angle, a formal experiment (like sonnets), or a contrarian argument — and followed it to a surprising conclusion.\n- Essays that read as \"I did research about UChicago and I'm smart\" failed even when well-written. The prompts exist precisely because that kind of essay is the default.\n\nSources: admitraven.com · noahnomad.com · news.uchicago.edu\n\n---\n\n## What separates strong essays\n\n**Extended essay:**\n- A specific, unusual, or unexpected angle on the prompt. Not the first answer that comes to mind.\n- Clarity maintained throughout — however experimental the form, the argument or narrative is intelligible.\n- Evidence of how the applicant thinks, not just what they know. Readers look for intellectual flexibility and a willingness to follow an idea past the comfortable point.\n- Originality that comes from genuine interest rather than strategic positioning. Essays that are \"trying to seem quirky\" are distinct and unfavorable compared to essays that \"can't help but be original because this is genuinely how I think.\"\n- The \"choose your own adventure\" option (option 6) is a genuine choice, not a fallback. Some of the strongest essays use past prompts or invent their own. The key is that the prompt and answer are equally interesting.\n\n**Why UChicago:**\n- Personal and specific. Name a specific intellectual encounter — a text, an idea, a professor's work, a course's framing — and explain the actual connection to your own intellectual history.\n- Avoid generic Core curriculum praise. Every applicant mentions the Core. The essays that stand out use the Core's specific structure (primary texts, interdisciplinary sequences) and connect it to something the applicant has already grappled with.\n- Show desire for a specific kind of thinking environment, not just \"rigor.\" UChicago's culture is not just hard — it's analytically precise, philosophically serious, and sometimes contrarian. Essays that get this feel distinct.\n\n---\n\n## Clichés and mistakes to avoid\n\n- Writing the extended essay as a standard \"here is what I've done\" response to an abstract prompt.\n- Mentioning UChicago's economics ranking, Booth's ranking, or the school's Nobel count as reasons for wanting to attend. Readers read this as surface-level research.\n- Using the extended essay to demonstrate domain knowledge rather than intellectual character. A correct and thorough answer to \"How do thoughts eat?\" that reads like a neuroscience paper is weaker than an unexpected but vivid and argued answer from an applicant with no neuroscience background.\n- Forcing a connection to entrepreneurship or the Polsky Center in the Why UChicago essay when the fit is weak. If the essay reads as \"I want to go here because of the New Venture Challenge,\" it will not distinguish — everyone who knows UChicago's entrepreneurship offerings knows the NVC.\n- Submitting a video profile that recaps the essays in spoken form. The video profile exists to add new information, not to perform the application.\n- Trying to write a \"UChicago-style\" essay rather than your own essay. Admissions readers have read decades of quirky essays. Forced quirkiness is immediately recognizable.\n- Submitting the \"choose your own adventure\" option (6) with a prompt that is neither original nor interesting. The freedom is only valuable if you use it well.\n\n---\n\n## Specifics an applicant can name\n\nThese are tied to entrepreneurship and verifiable.\n\n**Programs:**\n- Polsky Center for Entrepreneurship and Innovation: The main hub, spanning the College, Booth, and broader university. Located in the Harper Center.\n- Entrepreneurship minor: Taught by Booth faculty. Unusually strong undergraduate access to a top-5 business school. Requires application and has prerequisites; it is not automatic.\n- New Venture Challenge (NVC): UChicago's flagship accelerator. The College NVC track is the undergraduate-specific pathway. In 2026, $150,000+ was invested in finalists — as equity, not prize money. NVC alumni include Grubhub (IPO) and Braintree/Venmo (acquired by PayPal).\n- Social NVC: A parallel track focused on social impact ventures.\n- Polsky Exchange: Co-working space and incubator with a fabrication lab for early-stage student ventures.\n- George Shultz Innovation Fund: Student-driven fund investing in university-affiliated deep-tech and science startups.\n\n**Professors and intellectual touchstones an applicant can credibly name:**\n- The Becker Friedman Institute (economics research hub) — useful for applicants interested in econ-driven ventures.\n- UChicago's market design and mechanism design faculty (e.g., research connected to Nobel laureates Jean Tirole-adjacent work) — for fintech or marketplace founders.\n- Note: Do not name individual professors unless you have actually read their work and can explain what interested you. Name-dropping without substance is a common error.\n\n**Traditions and culture applicants can reference authentically:**\n- Scav Hunt: UChicago's legendary four-day scavenger hunt, considered the world's largest. Directly connected to the culture of creative, rule-bending problem-solving the extended essays embody.\n- The Core Curriculum: Specifically, that it uses primary texts rather than textbooks, and that students from different majors share the same intellectual vocabulary. This is the mechanism, not just the brand.\n- The extended essay tradition itself: Prompts since 1984, student-generated since 2000. Applicants who reference this history show they've engaged with the school's culture seriously.\n\n**Caution:** UChicago's academic culture is genuinely analytical and theoretically rigorous. Applicants who present as primarily action-oriented builders without also demonstrating intellectual depth are a weaker fit. The strongest applicant at UChicago is both — a builder who can also go deep on the underlying theory. That is the profile of an NVC finalist.\n\nSources: polsky.uchicago.edu · collegeadmissions.uchicago.edu · news.uchicago.edu · ref/uchicago.md\n\n---\n\n## The extended essay: culture and how admitted students took risks\n\nThe UChicago extended essay has been part of the application since at least 1984. Since 2000, the five rotating prompts are all inspired by current students and alumni — the school emails the community each spring, receives roughly 1,000 submissions, and a small group of admissions counselors selects the final five. The sixth option (choose your own adventure) is always available and lets applicants use any past prompt or create their own.\n\nThis means two things for applicants:\n1. The prompt you answer was literally written by an undergraduate because they found it interesting. The best way to answer it is to find the question genuinely interesting yourself.\n2. If none of the five current prompts give you something to say, the past-prompt archive is enormous and open. Choosing an older prompt and explaining why you found it irresistible can itself be part of the answer.\n\nThe risks that paid off for admitted students:\n\n- **Formal risk** (Xander Hastings, 2023): Rewrote his entire supplement as Spenserian sonnets in iambic pentameter one week before the deadline. The risk was that it would read as gimmicky. It worked because the execution was high-quality and the form genuinely matched his identity as a literary thinker.\n- **Structural risk** (Noah Adams, enrolled): Inverted the expected answer — argued that a foreign country was \"home\" and his literal home was not. The risk was seeming contrarian without substance. It worked because the specific details (a closed bedroom door causing a family misunderstanding) made the argument real.\n- **Intellectual risk** (multiple): Past admitted essays have argued counterintuitive positions — that a \"vestigial structure\" is not vestigial, that a mixed metaphor reveals something true about the concepts it conflates, that a spurious correlation is actually causal. The risk is being wrong or seeming foolish. It works when the argument is genuinely followed to its logical end.\n\nThe risk that did not pay off: safe, competent, well-organized responses that answer the prompt correctly but are forgettable. Admissions readers see thousands of essays. An applicant who \"plays it safe\" on the extended essay is leaving the most important window into their intellectual character largely closed.\n\nSources: news.uchicago.edu/story/unique-uchicago-essay-questions-spark-students-creativity · admitraven.com · noahnomad.com\n\n---\n\n## ED II option\n\nED II at UChicago has a Jan 4 deadline, mid-February decisions, and a binding commitment to enroll if admitted.\n\n**Who should consider ED II:**\n- Applicants who applied EA or RD to UChicago in the first round but have since decided UChicago is their clear top choice, and who are still considering other schools with Feb/March decisions.\n- Applicants who missed the Nov 2 deadline for genuine logistical reasons.\n- Applicants who received a December deferral from another binding ED school and are now targeting UChicago — though this is a narrow scenario.\n\n**Strategic note.** UChicago explicitly states that all four application plans go through the same review process. The binding commitment of ED II provides a yield-rate signal that the school values. Applying ED II over RD is likely advantageous, though the magnitude of that advantage over RD is unknown — no official rate is published. Applying ED I (Nov 2, binding) remains the strongest early signal.\n\n**For Veer specifically:** He lives in Lincolnshire, IL — roughly an hour from Hyde Park. That is a legitimate geographic fit he can speak to. His Nov 1 deadline target is for the EA or ED I round. If he applies to UChicago EA (non-binding, also Nov 2) and is not admitted in December, he could escalate to an ED II commitment in January as a deliberate strategy, provided UChicago is his clear first choice by that point.\n\nSources: collegeadmissions.uchicago.edu/apply/ · koppelmangroup.com\n\n---\n\n## Optional video profile\n\nUChicago does not conduct traditional admissions interviews. The recommended video profile is the substitute.\n\n**Format.** Two options:\n1. Upload directly to the UChicago Account: up to 2 minutes.\n2. Glimpse by InitialView: 60–90 seconds, available to U.S. high school students, shareable with multiple colleges.\n\nRecommended submission deadlines (not hard deadlines): Nov 4 for EA/ED I; Jan 7 for RD/ED II. Submitting by these dates ensures the video is available during initial review. Late submissions may not be seen before a decision is made.\n\n**What UChicago says about it** (official optional materials page, collegeadmissions.uchicago.edu/apply/application/optional-materials/): \"If you would like to add your voice to your application, you have the option to submit a two-minute video introduction in lieu of the traditional college interview... Your recording does not need to be extensively rehearsed or polished, and the video does not need to be edited.\" The purpose: \"If there is any important information relevant to your candidacy you were unable to address elsewhere in the application, please share that information here.\"\n\n**What the video should and should not do:**\n- Should: Add new context — an intellectual habit, a project, a personal story, a perspective — not found or developed in the written essays.\n- Should not: Repeat the essays, activities list, or personal statement in spoken form. That wastes the opportunity.\n- Should: Sound like a thoughtful conversation. A loose outline, not a memorized script. A shaky phone recording with genuine content is rated higher than a polished video that says nothing new.\n- Should not: Try to perform \"UChicago student.\" Admissions readers are experienced with applicants performing intellectual quirk. Forced eccentricity is easily recognized.\n\n**Content approach.** Choose one idea, question, or story that says something specific about how you think. Not two, not three — one. Common strong topics: a question you keep returning to, a project that failed and what you actually learned, a habit of observation or collection (one applicant described keeping a notebook of overheard questions from strangers), a moment of recognition that changed how you see something. The topic does not need to be impressive. It needs to be specific and personal enough that a reader would remember it after watching 50 other videos.\n\n**Example of weak vs. strong approach** (from myls.ai, July 2026):\n- Weak: \"I'm applying to UChicago because it has a great reputation and strong academics.\" — Generic, applies to any school.\n- Strong: \"I spent last summer trying to prove a probability puzzle wrong just to understand why it worked, and I ended up filling an entire notebook before I accepted I was the one who was wrong. That kind of stubborn curiosity is exactly why UChicago's reputation for rigor over convenience appeals to me.\" — Concrete, personal, connected to a distinctive attribute of UChicago culture.\n\n**Is it worth submitting?** UChicago \"actively recommends\" submitting one. It is not required, but skipping it without thought is a strategic error. Applicants to a 4.5% school who leave any opportunity to add genuine context on the table are making a choice with real costs. Submit it if — and only if — you have something genuinely new to say.\n\nSources: collegeadmissions.uchicago.edu/apply/application/optional-materials/ · myls.ai/blog/university-of-chicago-video-profile-complete-guide/ (July 2026) · collegevine.com/faq/15699/uchicago-video-supplement-tips\n\n---\n\n## Coaching questions\n\nThese are for Veer specifically, pushing past surface answers on the UChicago essays.\n\n1. You're from Lincolnshire — roughly an hour from Hyde Park. You could visit UChicago tomorrow. Have you? What did you actually notice when you were there, or what specific thing are you saving that visit for? What question would you only know to ask after being there?\n\n2. The extended essay has six options. Which one gave you the most to say — not the one that sounded most interesting, but the one where you had an answer already forming before you finished reading the prompt? Describe your answer in two sentences.\n\n3. The \"choose your own adventure\" option lets you use any past prompt or invent your own. Is there a past UChicago prompt you wish you'd seen first? What would you write for it?\n\n4. The NVC invests real equity in student ventures. That's different from a prize competition. If you pitched your current idea to the NVC, what would they say is the biggest hole in your market logic — and can you answer it?\n\n5. UChicago's Entrepreneurship minor is taught by Booth faculty. That means you're taking graduate business school courses as an undergrad. Are you prepared for a Booth-style case discussion, where the faculty expects you to defend your analysis against sharp pushback? What specific analytical gap would you most need to close to be ready?\n\n6. The extended essay readers have said the goal is to see \"creativity, flexibility, and exploration that will lend itself well to being in a UChicago classroom.\" What's the most creative argument you've made about something you built — not a pitch, an argument, with a counterargument and a response?\n\n7. The Why UChicago essay asks how the school \"satisfies your desire for a particular kind of learning, community, and future.\" What specific kind of learning do you want that you can't find elsewhere? Not \"rigor\" — what specific intellectual approach or environment?\n\n8. Xander Hastings scrapped months of essay drafts one week before the deadline and wrote in iambic pentameter. He said \"it was so much more me.\" What would it mean for your essay to be \"so much more you\"? What are you holding back in the draft you have now?\n\n9. The video profile should add new context. What is the most important thing about you that your written essays don't capture — and can you say it, out loud, in two minutes, in a way that feels like a conversation rather than a prepared speech?\n\n10. UChicago has a 4.5% overall admit rate. Applying EA (non-binding) and getting deferred in December, then switching to ED II in January, is a real strategy — it preserves your options but signals commitment at the right moment. Is UChicago actually your first choice, or are you still deciding? Because ED I (binding Nov 2) is the right move if it is, and EA is the right move if it isn't. Which is it?\n\n11. The Core Curriculum puts every student through the same primary texts regardless of major. That means your future econ-major classmate and your future CS-major classmate will both have read Plato's Republic. What text — from any subject — do you think every founder should have to read, and why?\n\n12. UChicago's culture is analytical and sometimes contrarian. The school has more Nobel laureates in economics than any other institution. If you built a marketplace business and an economist at UChicago challenged your pricing model on theoretical grounds, could you defend it? Or would you update it? What does your answer say about how you want to think?\n\n---\n\n## Sources\n\n- collegeadmissions.uchicago.edu/apply/ — official application page (verified July 2026)\n- collegeadmissions.uchicago.edu/apply/essay/ — 2026-27 essay prompts (verified July 2026)\n- collegeadmissions.uchicago.edu/apply/application/optional-materials/ — official optional materials page including video profile\n- news.uchicago.edu/story/unique-uchicago-essay-questions-spark-students-creativity — Aug 2021, extended essay history, Peter Wilson and Grace Chapin James quotes\n- admitraven.com/en/case-studies/cdd76171-e4ae-44be-bd7f-c3fb7d747074 — Xander Hastings admitted essay case study (May 2025)\n- noahnomad.com/blog/my-uchicago-extended-essay — Noah Adams, published admitted essay (2017)\n- koppelmangroup.com/blog/2025/5/2/early-decision-strategy-for-uchicago-2025-2026 — ED/EA strategy analysis, Koppelman Group (May 2025)\n- myls.ai/blog/university-of-chicago-video-profile-complete-guide/ — Video profile complete guide (July 2026)\n- collegevine.com/faq/15699/uchicago-video-supplement-tips — CollegeVine video supplement tips\n- polsky.uchicago.edu — Polsky Center programs and NVC\n- ref/uchicago.md — verified 2026-27 prompts, rates, and program data (verified July 2026)\n","northwestern":"## Part 1 — Verified application facts (from the Founder's Compass dashboard)\n\n# Northwestern University (id: northwestern)\n\nPlatform: Common App / Coalition | Test: Test-optional | Selectivity: {\"rate\":\"≈7.7%\",\"tier\":\"Most selective\",\"note\":\"Calculated from CDS 2024-25 (49,474 applicants, 3,806 admitted). Early Decision is a meaningful lever.\",\"cycle\":\"prior\"}\n\nDeadlines: [{\"plan\":\"Early Decision (binding)\",\"date\":\"Nov 1\",\"note\":\"Decisions mid-December\",\"cycle\":\"ok\"},{\"plan\":\"Regular Decision\",\"date\":\"Jan 4\",\"note\":\"Decisions late March\",\"cycle\":\"ok\"}]\n\nApp note: Northwestern uses the Common App / Coalition. It no longer requires the personal statement (you may include one). One required background essay plus up to two optional short prompts.\n\nEntrepreneurship headline: The Garage is the heart of it — a buzzing on-campus incubator open to any student — backed by the Farley Center's curriculum and Northwestern's signature cross-disciplinary 'NUvention' venture courses.\n\nDegrees: Farley undergraduate Certificate in Entrepreneurship (and minor) · pairs with McCormick engineering, Medill journalism, design and economics\n\n## Supplements (verified 2026-27 where cycle=ok)\nOne required essay (≤300 words), then answer up to two of five optional prompts (under 200 words each).\n\n- **Background & contribution (required)** [Required, ≤300 words, cycle=ok]: We want to be sure we're considering your application in the context of your personal experiences: What aspects of your background (your identity, your school setting, your community, your household, etc.) have most shaped how you see yourself engaging in Northwestern's community, be it academically, extracurricularly, culturally, politically, socially, or otherwise?\n- **Paint The Rock (optional)** [Optional, <200 words, cycle=ok]: Painting “The Rock” is a tradition at Northwestern that invites all forms of expression—students promote campus events or extracurricular groups, support social or activist causes, show their Wildcat spirit (what we call “Purple Pride”), celebrate their culture, and more. What would you paint on The Rock, and why?\n- **Dream up something interdisciplinary (optional)** [Optional, <200 words, cycle=ok]: Northwestern fosters a distinctively interdisciplinary culture. If you could dream up an undergraduate class, research project, or creative effort (a start-up, a design prototype, a performance, etc.), what would it be? Who might be some ideal classmates or collaborators?\n- **Community & belonging (optional)** [Optional, <200 words, cycle=ok]: Community and belonging matter at Northwestern. Tell us about one or more communities, networks, or student groups you see yourself connecting with on campus.\n- **Our location (optional)** [Optional, <200 words, cycle=ok]: Northwestern's location is special: on the shore of Lake Michigan, steps from downtown Evanston, just a few miles from Chicago. What aspects of our location are most compelling to you, and why?\n- **Diversity of perspectives (optional)** [Optional, <200 words, cycle=ok]: Northwestern is a place where people with diverse backgrounds from all over the world can study, live, and talk with one another. This range of experiences and viewpoints immeasurably enriches learning. How might your individual background contribute to this diversity of perspectives in Northwestern's classrooms and around our campus?\n\n## Programs\n- The Garage: Northwestern's student startup incubator — build space, the Wildfire pre-accelerator, Jumpstart and founder residencies.\n- Farley Center: Center for Entrepreneurship & Innovation (McCormick) running the undergraduate entrepreneurship certificate / minor.\n- NUvention: Signature cross-disciplinary courses where students build real ventures (web/media, medical, energy, social).\n- Segal Design Institute: Human-centered design and prototyping that feeds product-minded founders.\n\n## Funding/competitions\n- VentureCat: Northwestern's flagship startup competition with substantial cash prizes across categories.\n- Wildfire & Jumpstart: The Garage's pre-accelerator and idea-stage programs with mentorship, milestones and small grants.\n- Garage residencies: Selective residencies giving teams space, coaching and investor connections to keep building.\n\n## Watch-outs\n- Entrepreneurship is a certificate + co-curricular layer, not a standalone major.\n- The quarter system is fast and intense — protect time for building.\n- Ultra-selective; ED materially improves odds but is binding.\n\n## Known successful-essay sources\n- How to write the Northwestern supplement — examples + guide (College Essay Guy) https://www.collegeessayguy.com/blog/northwestern-supplemental-essay — 2025–26 · full examples for the current prompts\n- 3 inspiring Northwestern essay examples (CollegeVine) https://blog.collegevine.com/northwestern-essay-examples — 2023 · real 'Why Northwestern' essays with critique\n- A Medill 'Why Northwestern' essay that worked (Essays That Worked) https://essaysthatworked.com/essay-library/112 — 2022 · full admitted essay + why it worked\n\n## Other fields\n- short: Northwestern\n- mono: N\n- city: Evanston\n- state: IL\n- type: Private\n- setting: Suburban lakefront · 12 mi from Chicago\n- undergrad: ≈8,850\n- platform: Common App / Coalition\n- ac: #4E2A84\n- ac2: #341c59\n- fit: 84\n- verdict: Strong fit\n- sub: {\"program\":8,\"experiential\":8,\"outcomes\":7,\"location\":7}\n- rateNum: 7.7\n- dlSort: 1101\n- testPolicy: Test-optional\n- sat: 1500–1560\n- act: 34–35\n- fee: $75\n- recs: 1 counselor + 1 teacher\n- opens: August 1\n- bestFor: Cross-disciplinary builders — media × tech, design × engineering, journalism × product — who want a genuinely student-run incubator (The Garage) and a structured entrepreneurship curriculum, all 12 miles from Chicago.\n- strengths: [\"Top-10 national university strong in journalism (Medill), engineering (McCormick), communication and theatre.\",\"Deeply collaborative, interdisciplinary culture across its schools.\",\"The Garage is one of the best on-campus student incubators in the country.\",\"Lakefront Evanston campus with fast access to Chicago's startup and corporate scene.\"]\n- entreRank: The Garage is widely regarded among the strongest university startup incubators\n- founders: Garage alumni have launched venture-backed startups across consumer, media and software; a strong Chicago and national alumni network with notable media/tech crossover.\n- optIntro: One required essay (≤300 words), then answer up to two of five optional prompts (under 200 words each).\n- cost: {\"mode\":\"private\",\"sticker\":\"≈$99,400 / yr\",\"aid\":\"Meets 100% of demonstrated need; no-loan financial aid for many families.\"}\n- cardEnt: The Garage incubator (open to all) · Farley certificate · NUvention.\n- src: Sourced from admissions.northwestern.edu (ED Nov 1 / RD Jan 4 confirmed 2026-27), CDS 2024-25, thegarage.northwestern.edu and farley.northwestern.edu. Supplemental prompts confirmed 2026-27 directly via Northwestern's official Writing Supplements FAQ (checked Jul 13, 2026), which explicitly confirms the required-short-answer + optional-personal-essay structure 'for the 2026–27 admissions cycle'; prompt text itself is unchanged from 2025-26.\n\n## Part 2 — Deep research: paths in, what readers want, admitted essays\n\n# Northwestern University — Knowledge Base\n\n## Paths in\n\n### Overall selectivity\nNorthwestern received 53,676 applications for Fall 2025 and admitted 3,933, a 7.3% overall rate. The prior year (Fall 2024): 49,474 applied, 3,806 admitted, 7.7%. Source: Northwestern Office of Institutional Research Data Book (https://www.northwestern.edu/provost/about/ir/data-book/v58/t2.01-undergraduate-admissions-statistics.pdf, 2026).\n\n### Admission plans\n- **Early Decision (binding)**: deadline Nov 1, decisions mid-December. This is the only early round. Northwestern has no EA or REA.\n- **Regular Decision**: deadline Jan 4, decisions late March.\n\nED is a meaningful lever. Northwestern does not publish a separate ED vs. RD rate, but binding commitment pools at highly selective schools typically admit at 2-3x the RD rate. The ref file confirms the CDS overall is 7.7% (prior year). No confirmed NU-specific ED advantage rate is available from official sources.\n\n### Which school to apply to\nThis is the most consequential decision in the Northwestern application. You apply to one of six undergraduate schools:\n\n| School | Best for | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Weinberg College of Arts and Sciences** | Economics, data science, social sciences, pre-professional tracks | Largest school; most flexible curriculum; MMSS (see below) is housed here; most common entry point for students pursuing the Kellogg Certificate |\n| **McCormick School of Engineering** | Tech founders, CS, engineering + entrepreneurship | Access to NUvention courses through Farley; Integrated Science Program (ISP) is McCormick-adjacent; strongest if your venture is tech-first |\n| **Medill School of Journalism, Media, Integrated Marketing Communications** | Media × tech founders; journalism entrepreneurs; content/platform businesses | Uniquely strong for media-product intersection; NUvention: Media is cross-listed with journalism and CS |\n| **School of Communication** | Theater, film, performance, media adjacent | Smaller; useful if your venture involves creative industry; McCormick+Comm dual-degree path exists |\n| **Bienen School of Music** | Music and creative industries | Only if music is the venture focus |\n| **School of Education and Social Policy (SESP)** | EdTech, social entrepreneurship, policy + tech | Smaller but distinctive; undergraduate learning sciences and human development majors |\n\n**Northwestern does not publish school-by-school admit rates.** No confirmed selectivity differences between schools are available from official sources. Counselors and admissions forums suggest McCormick may admit at a slightly lower rate than Weinberg due to engineering volume elsewhere, but this is not verified. Do not assume one school is an easier path in.\n\n**Internal transfer** between schools is possible but not guaranteed and is treated as a separate process. Do not count on switching after admission.\n\nFor an entrepreneurship-focused applicant like Veer:\n- **Weinberg + Economics** is the most common path to MMSS and the Kellogg Certificate pipeline.\n- **McCormick + CS or Industrial Engineering** is strongest for a product/tech venture with a technical co-founder identity.\n- **Medill** is worth considering if the venture involves media, news, or content — the McCormick+Medill combination is explicitly described as a path Northwestern supports.\n- Applying to McCormick when your interest is more business/social than technical reads as a mismatch; apply to Weinberg or SESP instead.\n\n### Interview — now discontinued\nNorthwestern **discontinued alumni interviews in October 2024**. According to The Daily Northwestern (https://dailynorthwestern.com/2024/10/04/campus/northwestern-discontinues-alumni-interviews-for-undergraduate-applicants/), \"Demand for interviews increasingly outpaces alumni capacity\" — the Office of Undergraduate Admission wrote this in an email to the Alumni Admission Council in July 2024.\n\nWhat replaced it:\n1. **Alumni 'Cat Chats** (also called \"Alumni Conversations\"): Voluntary, optional meetings with alumni volunteers — explicitly NOT evaluative. The admissions office says: \"conversations are meant to recruit prospective students rather than evaluate applicants under review.\" These do not go into your file. Source: https://admissions.northwestern.edu/visit/experience/alumni-conversations.html\n2. **Glimpse Video** (optional, 60-90 seconds): Filmed through the Glimpse platform with no ability to edit. Less than 10% of Class of 2029 applicants submitted one. Dean of Admissions Liz Kinsley: \"Glimpse videos shouldn't be seen as a replacement for the discontinued alumni interviews.\" Source: The Daily Northwestern, Oct 2025 (https://dailynorthwestern.com/2025/10/10/campus/a-glimpse-of-personality-class-of-2029-used-glimpse-videos-to-add-a-spark-to-their-applications/).\n\n**Practical implication**: There is no evaluative interview for NU. The 'Cat Chat is a networking opportunity, not an admissions conversation. If you want to pursue one, it gives you authentic intelligence about NU life — use it to ask specific questions about The Garage, NUvention, or MMSS, then name those insights in your essays.\n\n### Optional materials and demonstrated interest\n- **Personal statement (Common App essay)**: Northwestern no longer requires it but accepts it. Official language: \"We welcome — but by no means expect — your submission of a personal essay.\" Most competitive applicants include it.\n- **Demonstrated interest**: Northwestern does not use demonstrated interest as an admission factor per its stated policy.\n- **Legacy**: Not a confirmed advantage at Northwestern. Unlike Duke, no legacy preference is stated.\n- **Test-optional**: Confirmed. Middle 50% for submitters: SAT 1500-1560, ACT 34-35.\n\n---\n\n## MMSS, Kellogg Certificate, and The Garage\n\n### MMSS (Mathematical Methods in the Social Sciences)\nMMSS is a **selective, honors-level major** within Weinberg combining rigorous quantitative methods (math, statistics, formal modeling) with the social sciences. Founded in 1978.\n\nKey facts (source: https://mmss.northwestern.edu/about/ and https://mmss.northwestern.edu/about/mmss-faq-2025.html):\n- Students take 12 core courses together as a cohort — this creates an unusually tight academic community.\n- Requires a senior thesis (year-long research project, MMSS 398-1,2,3).\n- You must apply for MMSS separately — either as a high school senior (on the application) or as a current NU first-year applying for sophomore admission.\n- Not a business major: \"MMSS is not a business major. It's not based on case studies, and it doesn't teach from a script or a cookbook.\"\n- Where graduates go: consulting, tech, finance, grad school, investment banking — and \"even projects like improving police department operations or launching their own startups.\"\n- From the FAQ: \"MMSS also provides ideal preparation for students planning to enroll in one of the Kellogg Undergraduate Certificate Programs.\"\n- **For Veer**: MMSS is the strongest academic foundation for a quant-literate entrepreneur who wants to use data to build. It is not entrepreneurship training per se — it is analytical training that happens to feed startup work.\n\n### Kellogg Certificate for Undergraduates\nThe Kellogg School offers **two undergraduate certificates** open to any Northwestern student: Financial Economics (FE) and Managerial Analytics (MA). Source: https://catalogs.northwestern.edu/undergraduate/additional-baccalaureate-options/certificates/kellogg-certificates/ and https://www.kellogg.northwestern.edu/programs/certificate/academics/prerequisites/.\n\nKey facts:\n- **About 100 students per year** accepted total (roughly 50 per certificate).\n- Competitive application during sophomore or junior year (sophomore preferred for scheduling flexibility).\n- Requires completion of advanced prerequisites: calculus through multivariable, microeconomics, then corequisites in probability and econometrics/statistics.\n- Middle 50% GPA in prerequisites: **3.2 to 3.9**.\n- Four Kellogg courses taught at graduate level.\n- Students get a **dedicated career development specialist** for internships and full-time placement.\n- You do NOT need to be in business school — any school can participate. MMSS students are explicitly mentioned as well-positioned for this path.\n- **Cannot apply to both certificates** — choose FE (finance, economic analysis) or MA (data science, analytics).\n\n**For Veer**: The Kellogg Certificate is a credential, not a business degree. It signals quant strength and gives Kellogg connections. FE is better for fintech/finance ventures; MA is better for data-driven product companies. Neither replaces an MBA — they are undergraduate credentials that signal rigor.\n\n### The Garage\nThe Garage is Northwestern's **student startup incubator — open to all full-time, degree-seeking NU students from any school**. 11,000 sq ft of space including co-working, an AR/VR lab, and a Makerspace with rapid prototyping equipment. More than 1,500 student-founded startups have incubated there. Source: https://www.thegarage.northwestern.edu/programs and https://admissions.northwestern.edu/visit/programs/start-up.html.\n\nPrograms (from official site):\n- **Tinker Program**: Entry-level, self-paced, no time commitment. The door-opener for any student curious about entrepreneurship.\n- **Startup Matchmaking**: Quarterly event for teams to recruit members from the broader NU student body.\n- **Residency Program**: Core incubation — mentorship, weekly family dinners, 24/7 space access. For teams beyond the idea stage.\n- **Jumpstart Program**: 10-week summer accelerator, $10,000, structured programming. The most intensive pre-seed support.\n- **VentureCat**: University-wide startup competition with a **$175,000 prize pool**. Open to all students.\n- **Northwestern Problem Summit**: Connects students with real-world challenges across disciplines to inspire new ventures.\n- **Little Joe Ventures Fellowship**: Highly competitive, financial awards and transformational experiences, for **sophomore students** specifically.\n- **X-Factor Program**: Leadership development for founders (self-awareness, management).\n- **Luminate**: Entry-level program for first-generation/low-income (FGLI) students.\n- **Opportunity Fund**: Up to $1,000 for SES-eligible Residents or Tinkerers.\n- **Summer Startup Internship Program**: Work at early-stage startups founded by Garage alumni.\n\n**Key fact for essay writers**: The Garage is widely named as a reason to apply to Northwestern, but most essays name it without knowing its programs. \"I want to work in The Garage\" is generic. \"I want to pitch VentureCat with a team from McCormick and Medill, building on a NUvention: Media project\" shows actual knowledge. Name the specific program you would enter first.\n\n### NUvention\nNUvention is Farley Center's flagship project-based course series. Six tracks, each one quarter. Source: https://farley.northwestern.edu/academics-resources/nuvention/\n\nTracks:\n- **NUvention: Arts & Entertainment** — creative-industry venture building; open to Comm and Medill students.\n- **NUvention: Bio** — collaboration with Center for Synthetic Biology (Danielle Tullman-Ercek).\n- **NUvention: Energy** — cross-listed with Trienens Institute for Sustainability and Energy. Has produced the most named, funded ventures: Hazel Technologies, NUMiX Materials, blip energy, NuMat Technologies, Lilac Solutions.\n- **NUvention: Media** — co-taught by Jeremy Gilbert (former Washington Post) and Larry Birnbaum (CS, Narrative Science co-founder). Cross-listed with journalism, CS, and entrepreneurship.\n- **NUvention: Medical** — Feinberg, Kellogg, and McCormick students together; based on Stanford BioDesign methodology. Notable spinoff: Hubly Surgical (neurosurgical drilling system).\n- **NUvention: Launch** — for teams with an existing business model from a prior NUvention or Garage experience; prep for VentureCat and Rice Business Plan Competition.\n\n**Why this matters for Veer**: NUvention is where NU entrepreneurship is done, not just discussed. NUvention: Media is the right track for a founder interested in fintech, content, or consumer tech with a media angle. NUvention: Energy is for deep-tech. NUvention: Medical is for health/med. Real-world spinoffs from these courses are verifiable and worth naming by name in essays.\n\n---\n\n## What readers want\n\nFrom College Essay Guy's guide to the Northwestern supplement (https://www.collegeessayguy.com/blog/northwestern-supplemental-essay, updated Oct 2026):\n- \"Northwestern requires one main 'Why Northwestern?' supplemental essay (300 words)... this school's culture, quarter system, and interdisciplinary programs are genuinely distinctive enough that readers can tell who did the research.\"\n- \"A common mistake is focusing on the school's prestige or location in Evanston rather than connecting specific academic or extracurricular opportunities to the student's own interests and goals.\"\n\nFrom EduAvenues Northwestern supplement guide (https://www.eduavenues.com/blog/northwestern-supplemental-essays-guide):\n- \"The strongest 'Why NU' essays sound like a plan, not a love letter. They name programs by name, connect them to the student's specific interests, and make Chicago feel like part of the academic experience rather than a tourism bullet.\"\n- The four things NU readers signal they value: (1) interdisciplinary thinking, (2) specific knowledge of NU, (3) action-oriented goals, (4) a real reason for Chicago.\n\nNote: Northwestern's essays are the background/contribution essay (required, 300 words) plus up to two optional short prompts (under 200 words each). There is no formal \"Why Northwestern\" prompt in the 2026-27 cycle. The background essay asks how your experiences have shaped how you see yourself engaging in Northwestern's community — it is effectively the \"why NU\" in disguise. The optional prompts (Paint the Rock, interdisciplinary dream, community and belonging, location, diversity) give applicants more surface area to show fit.\n\n---\n\n## Admitted essays: what they did\n\n### Essay 1 — The Garage + Chicago specificity (EduAvenues model)\n**Pattern** (paraphrased from the guide, not a full student essay):\nThe essay that works names a specific Garage program or NUvention track by exact name, connects it to something the applicant already built, and ends with a concrete use of Chicago — not \"I love Chicago\" but \"Chicago's startup scene through 1871 and The Garage would let me test my ed-tech ideas with real users while still in school.\"\n**Why it works**: Every sentence is load-bearing. An essay this short cannot carry generic praise.\n**Source**: https://www.eduavenues.com/blog/northwestern-supplemental-essays-guide\n\n### Essay 2 — Interdisciplinary combination that only exists at NU (EduAvenues)\n**Pattern**: Student with interests in two fields (e.g., engineering and journalism) uses the essay to argue that only NU's school structure allows the combination. Names both schools, names actual courses or joint programs, then names what they would build.\n**Key quote from guide**: \"A McCormick and Communication combination, for example, lets a student build technical theater design that does not exist as a single major elsewhere. Make the combination concrete: name the schools, name the courses, name what you would build.\"\n**Why it works**: Makes the case for NU distinctively — this essay cannot be sent to Penn or Duke unchanged.\n**Source**: https://www.eduavenues.com/blog/northwestern-supplemental-essays-guide\n\n### Essay 3 — Medill applicant, Essays That Worked\nA Medill \"Why Northwestern\" essay (2022, pre-current prompts) that worked named Jeremy Gilbert specifically, referenced the Washington Post background, connected it to the applicant's interest in journalism + technology, and showed how Medill's specific curriculum path would let them pursue that intersection.\n**Source**: https://essaysthatworked.com/essay-library/112 (noted in the ref file as a known successful-essay source)\n\n### Essay 4 — CollegeVine Northwestern examples\nCollegeVine's \"3 Inspiring Northwestern Essay Examples\" covers current prompts with real student essays and critique. The pattern from CollegeVine: essays that worked for Northwestern were specific about NU programs, showed genuine fit with NU's collaborative culture, and avoided \"I've always wanted to go to Northwestern\" openings.\n**Source**: https://blog.collegevine.com/northwestern-essay-examples\n\n---\n\n## What separates strong essays\n\n**For the background/contribution essay (required, 300 words):**\n- Strong essays show a specific background element — geographic, cultural, family, school setting — and connect it directly to how the student will engage in NU's community.\n- The essay is about context, not achievement. A student from a small town naming rural constraints that shaped their problem-solving is more memorable than a student recapping their startup.\n- **Avoid**: Turning this into a list of extracurriculars or a second activity description.\n\n**For the optional prompts (under 200 words):**\n- Students who submit two earn more surface area but must keep each one tight. Weak students pad; strong students cut.\n- The interdisciplinary dream prompt is the natural home for an entrepreneur. A strong answer names a specific course, research project, or venture idea — and names ideal classmates from different schools at NU by what they study, not just that they're \"diverse.\"\n- The location prompt is a trap. \"Chicago is close\" is the answer everyone gives. A strong answer names a specific Chicago institution (1871, MATTER, a specific neighborhood accelerator, a lab at UI Chicago) and ties it to something the applicant would actually do.\n- Paint the Rock is a personality test, not an essay. The strongest answers are specific and reveal something unexpected about the applicant.\n\n**Interdisciplinary is not optional at NU:**\nFrom the EduAvenues guide: \"A single-track narrative reads as a poor fit, even if the track is strong.\" NU is built for students who connect fields. An essay that says \"I want to study economics at Weinberg\" without naming any cross-school connection tells the reader the applicant does not understand NU.\n\n---\n\n## Clichés and mistakes to avoid\n\n- **Generic \"The Garage\" reference** — Every applicant with an entrepreneurship interest mentions The Garage. Name the specific program (Jumpstart, VentureCat, Little Joe Fellowship) you would enter and why.\n- **Rankings praise** — \"Northwestern is a top 10 school\" is filler.\n- **Replacing the school name** — An essay that would work for Penn or Duke with a word swap is not done.\n- **Single-focus narrative** — One major, one goal, no cross-school connection reads as a mismatch.\n- **Sports enthusiasm only** — \"I want to watch Big Ten football\" is not why Northwestern.\n- **\"I love that Chicago is nearby\"** — This specific phrasing appeared in the EduAvenues guide as the canonical bad line. Use Chicago concretely or not at all.\n- **Naming MMSS without understanding it** — MMSS is a selective application, not a course sequence anyone can take. Do not name it as something you'll \"sign up for\" on day one.\n- **Assuming the Kellogg Certificate is just business classes** — It requires advanced math prerequisites and competitive application during sophomore/junior year. An applicant who mentions it should also mention the quantitative coursework they've done that prepares them.\n- **Treating the interview as an evaluative step** — There is no interview. The Glimpse video is optional and most applicants skip it.\n\n---\n\n## Specifics an applicant can name\n\n### The Garage programs (name the right one)\n- **Tinker Program**: Entry-level, for explorers. Name this if you have ideas but haven't built yet.\n- **Jumpstart**: 10-week summer accelerator, $10K. Name this if you have a team and idea.\n- **VentureCat**: $175K competition. Name this as a goal, not a guarantee.\n- **Little Joe Ventures Fellowship**: Specifically for sophomores. A distinctive name that shows research depth.\n- **Residency**: Core incubation for teams beyond the idea stage.\n\n### NUvention tracks (name the right one for your venture type)\n- **NUvention: Media** — for content, platform, journalism-tech founders. Named faculty: Jeremy Gilbert, Larry Birnbaum.\n- **NUvention: Energy** — for sustainability/cleantech. Known spinoffs: Hazel Technologies, NuMat Technologies, Lilac Solutions.\n- **NUvention: Medical** — for health-tech. Known spinoff: Hubly Surgical.\n- **NUvention: Launch** — for students continuing an existing venture from coursework or Garage.\n\n### Academic programs and courses\n- **MMSS** — Mathematical Methods in Social Sciences. Requires separate application; cohort of 12 core courses; senior thesis. Explicitly feeds Kellogg Certificate pipeline.\n- **Kellogg Certificate** — Financial Economics or Managerial Analytics. ~100 students/year; applied sophomore/junior year; requires calculus, micro, probability, econometrics.\n- **Farley Center** (full name: Farley Center for Entrepreneurship & Innovation, in Ford Building, Room G-302) — runs NUvention and the Undergraduate Certificate in Entrepreneurship (minor also available).\n- **ENTREP 495** — NUvention: Launch course number; signals you looked up the catalog.\n- **CAESAR** — NU's course catalog. Named by EduAvenues as where serious applicants do their research.\n\n### Chicago-specific\n- **1871** — Chicago's flagship tech startup hub (named explicitly in a known good example).\n- **Chicago's venture community** — connect it to whatever sector Veer actually builds in.\n\n### Culture and student life\n- **The Daily Northwestern** — student newspaper; EduAvenues recommends reading it for a week to understand actual campus culture.\n- **Dance Marathon** — one of the country's largest student philanthropies; mentioned as a named NU activity.\n- **WildcatConnection** — NU's student org platform; mentioned by EduAvenues as research source.\n- **Purple Pride** — NU's term for school spirit; used correctly in the Paint the Rock prompt.\n\n### Professors — not found\nNo specific faculty in entrepreneurship/innovation could be confirmed at the level of a named research lab or course the advisor could recommend by name without risk of error. Use the Farley Center faculty page (farley.northwestern.edu) to find a current mentor; faculty changes fast.\n\n---\n\n## Coaching questions\n\n1. You are applying to one NU school. Which one, and why — not \"it's flexible\" but what specific major, concentration, or track you want, and why Weinberg gives it to you when McCormick, Medill, or SESP would not?\n\n2. The required essay asks what in your background has shaped how you see yourself engaging at Northwestern. What is the most specific, concrete thing from your life in Lincolnshire — your school, your family, your community — that shaped you as a builder? Not a values statement. A scene.\n\n3. You want to use The Garage. Which program specifically — Tinker, Residency, Jumpstart, or VentureCat? What stage is your venture at right now, and which program matches that stage?\n\n4. NUvention has six tracks. Which one fits your venture idea, and what would your team look like — which schools would the other students come from, and why do you need them?\n\n5. The Kellogg Certificate admits about 100 students. What is your quant preparation — what math and economics have you taken, and what grade? Have you done the prerequisite coursework? If not, do not name the Certificate without a credible plan.\n\n6. MMSS is a separate application for a selective honors major. Is your interest in MMSS genuine — do you actually like abstract mathematical modeling of social systems? Or are you naming it because it sounds impressive?\n\n7. What does NU give you that UChicago does not? They are 12 miles apart, similarly ranked, with similar quant programs. Write me 3 sentences that distinguish NU for your specific goals.\n\n8. Chicago. You live in Lincolnshire. You are already 30 miles from The Loop. What would you do in Chicago as a Northwestern student that you cannot already do from Lincolnshire? Name the specific institution or neighborhood.\n\n9. The \"Why Northwestern\" essay is 300 words. Read yours with the school name removed. Does it fit Penn? Duke? Cornell? If yes, rewrite it.\n\n10. The interdisciplinary dream prompt is optional — but it is your best chance to show a cross-school vision. What course, research project, or venture would you dream up at NU? Name at least two NU schools, at least one real course from the catalog, and at least one ideal collaborator by what they study.\n\n11. Northwestern no longer has alumni interviews. The optional Glimpse video is 60-90 seconds, unedited, and fewer than 10% of applicants submit it. Would you submit one? What would you say, and why would it add something the essays do not show?\n\n12. The bridge between NU academics and Chicago industry is real — but only if you use it. After graduation, where do you want to be? What does the Garage + NUvention + Kellogg Certificate pipeline set you up for that you could not get at, say, Stern + NYU Entrepreneurs Challenge?\n\n---\n\n## Sources\n\n| Source | URL | Date | What it covers |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Northwestern Undergraduate Admissions History (IR Data Book) | https://www.northwestern.edu/provost/about/ir/data-book/v58/t2.01-undergraduate-admissions-statistics.pdf | Jul 2026 | Historical admit rates 2005-2025, 7.3% for 2025 |\n| Northwestern alumni interviews discontinued | https://dailynorthwestern.com/2024/10/04/campus/northwestern-discontinues-alumni-interviews-for-undergraduate-applicants/ | Oct 2024 | Interview elimination, replacement by Cat Chats |\n| Glimpse videos, Class of 2029 | https://dailynorthwestern.com/2025/10/10/campus/a-glimpse-of-personality-class-of-2029-used-glimpse-videos-to-add-a-spark-to-their-applications/ | Oct 2025 | <10% submit Glimpse, Kinsley quotes, admitted student examples |\n| Alumni Cat Chats (official) | https://admissions.northwestern.edu/visit/experience/alumni-conversations.html | — | Format, not evaluative, affinity groups |\n| MMSS About page | https://mmss.northwestern.edu/about/ | — | Program overview, Kellogg Certificate connection |\n| MMSS FAQ 2025 | https://mmss.northwestern.edu/about/mmss-faq-2025.html | 2025 | What MMSS is, alumni outcomes, culture |\n| Kellogg Certificate catalog | https://catalogs.northwestern.edu/undergraduate/additional-baccalaureate-options/certificates/kellogg-certificates/ | — | FE and MA certificate overview, ~100 students |\n| Kellogg Certificate prerequisites | https://www.kellogg.northwestern.edu/programs/certificate/academics/prerequisites/ | Jun 2026 | Prereqs, coreqs, GPA range 3.2-3.9 |\n| The Garage programs | https://www.thegarage.northwestern.edu/programs | — | All programs, VentureCat $175K, Jumpstart $10K, 1,500+ startups |\n| NUvention overview | https://farley.northwestern.edu/academics-resources/nuvention/ | — | 6 tracks, named spinoffs, faculty |\n| NUvention Launch | https://farley.northwestern.edu/academics-resources/nuvention/nuvention-launch.html | — | ENTREP 495, prerequisites, Spring 2026 |\n| EduAvenues supplement guide | https://www.eduavenues.com/blog/northwestern-supplemental-essays-guide | Dec 2025 | \"Why NU\" structure, clichés, named bad lines |\n| College Essay Guy supplement guide | https://www.collegeessayguy.com/blog/northwestern-supplemental-essay | Updated Oct 2026 | Essay examples, current prompts, key takeaways |\n| CollegeVine — Northwestern essay examples | https://blog.collegevine.com/northwestern-essay-examples | 2023 | Admitted essays with critique |\n| Essays That Worked — Medill essay | https://essaysthatworked.com/essay-library/112 | 2022 | Full admitted Medill essay + analysis |\n| START-UP program (admissions + Garage) | https://admissions.northwestern.edu/visit/programs/start-up.html | 2026 | Garage context, 11,000 sq ft, for admissions awareness |\n\n**Not found**: Official school-by-school admit rate breakdown for Weinberg vs. McCormick vs. Medill. Confirmed NU ED vs. RD admit rate gap with a primary source. Specific named professors in entrepreneurship that can be cited with confidence as current.\n","unc":"## Part 1 — Verified application facts (from the Founder's Compass dashboard)\n\n# UNC Chapel Hill (id: unc)\n\nPlatform: Common App | Test: Test required* | Selectivity: {\"rate\":\"≈17%\",\"tier\":\"Highly selective\",\"note\":\"Out-of-state first-years are capped near 18% by state law — far more competitive and ~$70K/yr. In-state is one of the best values in the country.\",\"cycle\":\"prior\"}\n\nDeadlines: [{\"plan\":\"Early Action (non-binding)\",\"date\":\"Oct 15\",\"note\":\"Also the Morehead-Cain / Robertson scholarship deadline\",\"cycle\":\"ok\"},{\"plan\":\"Regular Decision\",\"date\":\"Jan 15\",\"note\":\"Decisions by late March\",\"cycle\":\"ok\"}]\n\nApp note: UNC uses the Common App. New for 2026-27: UNC removed its two short-answer supplements — only the Common App personal statement is required (plus activities and additional information).\n\nEntrepreneurship headline: UNC delivers entrepreneurship through the cross-campus Shuford Program — a track-based minor open to any major — plus Launch Chapel Hill and Kenan-Flagler: elite-public substance at in-state value.\n\nDegrees: Minor in Entrepreneurship (Shuford, track-based) · Kenan-Flagler BSBA · pairs with computer science, biology and the social sciences\n\n## Supplements (verified 2026-27 where cycle=ok)\nThe two prior short-answer prompts below are shown for reference only — they are not part of the 2026-27 application.\n\n- **Personal quality & community impact (removed for 2026-27)** [Removed 26-27, was 250 words, cycle=ok]: Discuss one of your personal qualities and share a story, anecdote, or memory of how it helped you make a positive impact on a community. This could be your current community or another community you have engaged.\n- **An academic topic you're excited to explore (removed for 2026-27)** [Removed 26-27, was 250 words, cycle=ok]: Discuss an academic topic that you're excited to explore and learn more about in college. Why does this topic interest you? Topics could be a specific course of study, research interests, or any other area related to your academic experience in college.\n\n## Programs\n- Shuford Program in Entrepreneurship: Campus-wide Minor in Entrepreneurship with tracks — commercial, social, scientific, artistic — open to all majors.\n- Launch Chapel Hill: Accelerator at Innovate Carolina Junction; a new KPMG-powered track helps deep-tech/AI startups land their first customers.\n- Kenan-Flagler Business School: Top undergraduate business program with an assured first-year business-admit pathway and entrepreneurship concentration.\n- 1789 Student Venture Lab: Downtown student venture hub plus the 1789 Student Venture Fund providing early seed dollars.\n\n## Funding/competitions\n- Carolina Challenge: Campus pitch competition / Makeathon awarding cash to student ventures.\n- 1789 Student Venture Fund: Seed grants for earliest-stage student ventures (37 teams competed for spring-2026 awards).\n- Morehead-Cain & Robertson: Full-ride merit scholarships with enrichment funding that can bankroll ventures and summers.\n\n## Watch-outs\n- Out-of-state admission is brutal (≈18% statutory cap) and costs ~$70K/yr — treat OOS as a high reach.\n- Test scores are required again for most applicants — confirm your waiver eligibility.\n- Funding and VC density trail the coastal peers on this list, though the Triangle is rising fast.\n\n## Known successful-essay sources\n- 2 strong UNC Chapel Hill essay examples (CollegeVine) https://blog.collegevine.com/unc-chapel-hill-essay-examples — 2024 · real essays with annotated analysis\n- 12 UNC Chapel Hill essays that worked (Essays That Worked) https://essaysthatworked.com/unc-chapel-hill — 2022–26 · admitted essays across UNC prompts\n- UNC 'academic interests' essay database (Clastify) https://www.clastify.com/supplement/unc-chapel-hill/academic-interests — 2024–25 · real student essays, by prompt\n\n## Other fields\n- short: UNC\n- mono: U\n- city: Chapel Hill\n- state: NC\n- type: Public\n- setting: College town · Research Triangle\n- undergrad: ≈20,200\n- platform: Common App\n- ac: #4B9CD3\n- ac2: #13294B\n- fit: 76\n- verdict: Solid fit\n- sub: {\"program\":7,\"experiential\":6,\"outcomes\":6,\"location\":5}\n- rateNum: 17\n- dlSort: 1015\n- testPolicy: Test required*\n- sat: ≈1390–1530\n- act: ≈31–34\n- fee: $85\n- recs: 1 counselor + 1 teacher\n- opens: August 1\n- bestFor: In-state students above all — an elite public at unbeatable value, with the Shuford entrepreneurship minor (pick a track: commercial, social, scientific or artistic), Launch Chapel Hill, and Kenan-Flagler's business pipeline, in the Research Triangle.\n- strengths: [\"A top-5 U.S. public university and a perennial best-value pick.\",\"Kenan-Flagler Business School with a first-year business-admit pathway.\",\"Vibrant campus, big-time athletics and deep school spirit.\",\"Research Triangle location — Durham/Raleigh's fast-growing tech and biotech corridor.\"]\n- entreRank: Shuford minor widely respected; no single national undergrad rank confirmed\n- founders: A deep, loyal Tar Heel alumni network; the Research Triangle keeps a growing share of UNC startups (and capital) close to campus.\n- optIntro: The two prior short-answer prompts below are shown for reference only — they are not part of the 2026-27 application.\n- cost: {\"mode\":\"public\",\"inState\":\"≈$24,000–$29,000\",\"outState\":\"≈$70,000\",\"aid\":\"Meets 100% of demonstrated need; the Carolina Covenant is no-loan for low-income students. Morehead-Cain and Robertson offer full rides. Out-of-state aid is limited.\"}\n- cardEnt: Shuford track-based minor · Launch Chapel Hill · Kenan-Flagler.\n- src: Sourced from admissions.unc.edu (2026-27 supplement removal confirmed Jun 19, 2026; Oct 15 EA / Jan 15 RD deadlines re-verified live on the site's 2026-27 Apply hub Jul 15, 2026), UNC's own IPEDS Fall 2025 admissions filing (oira.unc.edu, 76,247 applicants / 17% admitted — refreshed Jul 21, 2026 from a stale older-cycle figure), innovate.unc.edu and Kenan-Flagler. In/out-of-state rates and test-waiver rules should be verified for your situation.\n\n## Part 2 — Deep research: paths in, what readers want, admitted essays\n\n# UNC Chapel Hill — Advisor Knowledge Base\n\n_Applicant context: Veer, from Lincolnshire IL (out-of-state). Entrepreneurship focus. Early Action deadline Oct 15._\n\n---\n\n## Paths in\n\n### Admission plans\n\nUNC offers Early Action (non-binding) and Regular Decision. There is no Early Decision and no binding option.\n\n| Plan | Deadline | Decision | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Early Action | Oct 15 | In-state: Dec 18–20; **OOS: Feb 10** | Non-binding. Also the Morehead-Cain and Robertson scholarship deadline. |\n| Regular Decision | Jan 15 | March 31 | No binding commit until May 1. |\n\nSource: UNC admissions site and Dec 18, 2025 news release. https://www.unc.edu/posts/2025/12/18/carolina-delivers-first-nc-admissions-decisions-a-month-sooner/\n\n### Overall admit rate\n\n**Fall 2025 (most recent):** 76,247 applicants, 12,751 admitted = **17% overall.**\nSource: CDS 2025-26. https://oira.unc.edu/wp-content/uploads/sites/297/2026/06/CDS_UNCCH_2025-26_20260615.pdf\n\n**Fall 2024 (prior cycle, for in-state / out-of-state detail):**\n- In-state: 6,289 admitted of 16,553 applied = **38%**\n- Out-of-state domestic: 2,792 admitted of 42,085 applied = **6.6%**\n- International: 1,128 of 7,897 applied = ~14%\n\nSource: CDS 2024-25 and CollegeNumbers aggregator. https://collegenumbers.com/school/university-of-north-carolina-at-chapel-hill\n\nThe OOS rate for Fall 2025 was 4,685 admitted of 48,645 OOS applicants = **9.6%** — a significant improvement over 6.6%, but state law caps non-residents at roughly 18% of first-year enrollment, so annual variation exists. Treat OOS admission as a high reach.\n\n### EA vs. RD for out-of-state students\n\nUNC does not publish separate EA and RD admit rates. What is confirmed:\n\n- OOS early action applicants get decisions by Feb 10 — later than in-state (Dec 18).\n- EA is the pathway required for Kenan-Flagler Assured Enrollment consideration. If Veer wants business assured admission, he must apply EA.\n- The Morehead-Cain scholarship is EA-only (Oct 15 deadline).\n- No published data on whether EA improves OOS odds vs. RD. Counselors generally advise EA for OOS students anyway: you learn earlier, and the Assured Enrollment and scholarship deadlines require it.\n\n### What now carries the application (no supplements in 2026-27)\n\nUNC dropped both 250-word supplemental prompts for the 2026-27 cycle. Official announcement: June 19, 2026. https://admissions.unc.edu/short-essays-removed-from-carolinas-2026-2027-application/\n\nDirector of Admissions Jared Rosenberg stated: \"Recognizing that completing a college application is a significant investment of time, requiring applicants to complete multiple application components while balancing academics, extracurricular activities, work and family responsibilities, UNC is removing the supplemental essays.\" Source: Daily Tar Heel, Oct 6, 2026. https://dailytarheel.com/270360/university/university-removes-supplemental-essays-2026-2027-application/\n\nWhat now carries the application:\n\n1. **Common App personal statement** (250–650 words) — the sole required essay. Seven Common App prompts; UNC lists them all. https://admissions.unc.edu/application-prompts-for-2025-2026/\n2. **Activities list** (10 slots in Common App)\n3. **Additional Information** section (650 characters) — UNC specifically calls this out as a place to share voice.\n4. **Recommendations** (1 counselor + 1 teacher required)\n5. **Assured Enrollment supplemental essay** — if opting in to Kenan-Flagler pathway (see below).\n\nA Chapel Hill-area counselor quoted in DTH: \"Some students' strongest qualities come through reflection, resilience, perspective, or compelling life experiences. They now have one fewer place to share those qualities.\" Admissions now has to find that in fewer documents.\n\n### Kenan-Flagler First-Year Admission: Assured Enrollment (Future Business Admits)\n\nUNC Kenan-Flagler admits approximately two-thirds of the Undergraduate Business Program (UBP) through the Assured Enrollment (FBA) pathway. This is not a separate application to a different school — it is a pathway checked in the Special Opportunities section of the UNC Common App.\n\n**How to apply:**\n1. Apply to UNC by the **Early Action deadline (Oct 15)** — Assured Enrollment is EA-only.\n2. Select \"Assured Enrollment to UNC Kenan-Flagler\" in Special Opportunities.\n3. Submit a short supplemental essay explaining interest in business and goals.\n4. List Business Administration as one of top three academic interests.\n\nSource: admissions.unc.edu/apply/special-opportunities/business/ and kenan-flagler.unc.edu/programs/undergraduate-business/admissions/high-school-students/\n\n**Selection criteria (holistic, no separate stats published):**\n- Exemplary high school academic record.\n- Character, integrity, resilience, responsibility.\n- Well-rounded extracurricular engagement including community involvement or work.\n- Demonstrated curiosity about business through academic choices, personal initiatives, or co-curricular experience.\n\n**Once admitted as FBA:**\n- Guaranteed business major spot as a sophomore — one semester earlier than non-FBA intra-transfer students.\n- Takes BUSI 100 (1.5 credits) and BUSI 200 (3 credits) in first year — FBA-only courses.\n- Must complete Calculus, Statistics, Economics, and BUSI 100/200 with a grade of C or better (not C-) by end of first year.\n- Minimum cumulative GPA of 3.25 after year one to maintain status.\n\nFall 2026 EA round: over 300 business assured admission offers made to in-state early action admits. Source: UNC Dec 2025 release.\n\n**If not offered Assured Enrollment:** Apply to UBP through the intra-university transfer process after first year. That process is competitive and requires an application and interview.\n\n### Shuford Program in Entrepreneurship\n\nCampus-wide minor open to any major. Not just for business students — CS, journalism, public policy, biology students all enroll.\n\n**Five courses required, plus a 320-hour internship:**\n\n| Course | Title |\n|---|---|\n| ECON 125 | Introduction to Entrepreneurship (gateway; open to all undergrads) |\n| ECON 101 or 111 | Intro to Economics or Economics of Entrepreneurship & Innovation |\n| ECON 325 | Entrepreneurship: Principles, Concepts, Frameworks, and Fluency (core) |\n| ECON 327 or equivalent | Venture-Creation Workshop (track course) OR approved course from another dept |\n| ECON 393 | Practicum in Entrepreneurship (capstone) |\n\nSource: shuford.unc.edu/curriculum and UNC catalog. https://catalog.unc.edu/undergraduate/programs-study/entrepreneurship-minor/\n\n**Important sequence:** ECON 325 must be completed before the internship, and ECON 325 must be done by end of junior year. The minor requires at least 3 full semesters plus a summer.\n\n**ECON 327 track sections include:** Commercial, Scientific, Arts, Sports, Media, and now a Startup Sales Funnel-Building section. Students work in teams to launch an actual venture concept. Source: shuford.unc.edu/trackcourses\n\n**ECON 390 — The Shuford Founder Accelerator:** Restricted to current student founders. Must have an active venture. Application required; contact Tom Scott (tws@unc.edu). Usually offered spring semester. This is for students who are already founding a company — relevant to Veer if he arrives at UNC with a live venture.\n\n**Students must apply to be admitted to the minor.** ECON 125 and ECON 111 are the only courses open to all before admission.\n\n### Other paths\n\n**Morehead-Cain Scholarship:** Full ride (tuition, room, board, fees, plus enrichment funding for summers including ventures). Nomination required from high school. Deadline Oct 15. https://www.moreheadcain.org\n\n**Robertson Scholars Leadership Program:** Full ride at UNC and Duke, with cross-enrollment. Strong program for service-oriented students. Oct 15 deadline.\n\n**Transfer:** ~900 transfer students admitted annually, deadline Feb 15 for fall.\n\n**Waitlist:** 4,084 students accepted waitlist spots for fall 2024; 295 admitted off waitlist.\n\n**Interviews:** None. UNC does not conduct interviews.\n\n**Legacy:** About 15% of enrolled first-year students are legacies. Legacy is considered but listed as a \"considered\" (not \"important\") factor in the CDS.\n\n**Demonstrated interest:** UNC does not consider it. Source: College Transitions analysis.\n\n---\n\n## What readers want\n\nUNC admissions has stated publicly — in their own words on the admissions site — that their favorites share common themes:\n\n> \"They're smart. Motivated. Service-oriented. Curious. Creative. Courageous. The kind of people who will not only flourish in but actively enhance our community while they're here.\"\n\nSource: admissions.unc.edu (quoted in College Transitions, collegetransitions.com/blog/how-to-get-into-unc-chapel-hill-admissions-data-strategies/)\n\nDirector Rosenberg's statement on the essay removal implies that admissions now leans more on activities, recommendations, and the personal statement to find those qualities. \"Rigor of high school course load\" is the only factor UNC rates \"very important.\" The next tier (\"important\") includes essays, recs, extracurriculars, character/personal qualities, and state residency.\n\nFor Kenan-Flagler Assured Enrollment, the school specifically looks for \"a demonstrated curiosity about business and related fields, reflected through academic choices, personal initiatives or co-curricular experiences.\" This is the thing that separates the FBA admits from everyone else who lists business as an interest.\n\n---\n\n## Admitted essays: what they did\n\nNote: UNC removed its supplements for 2026-27. All examples below are from prior cycles (2022-25) and addressed the old prompts. They are still useful because (a) the personal statement carries all the weight now, and (b) the qualities that worked in those essays still matter.\n\n**Example 1 — Founder of NAIL club (Substack, 2024, admitted):**\nPrompt: Personal quality that made a positive community impact (250 words).\nThis student described founding Networking and Apprenticeship for Imminent Leaders (NAIL) after an internship exposed her to cross-disciplinary researchers. The essay moved from curiosity (lab experience) → initiative (founding the club) → concrete impact (bringing in a bioinformatics professor who taught how to get research internships). Short narrative arc, specific detail, community outcome. Admitted with assured enrollment in two separate programs. Source: insaeng.substack.com/p/unc-chapel-hill-supplemental-essays\n\n**Example 2 — Music access gap (Essays That Worked, 2022, admitted):**\nPrompt: If you could change one thing to better your community.\nStudent was a musician who named a specific UNC student (Evan Linnett) running Musical Empowerment, a campus organization — visited campus, had a real conversation, came back with a specific idea to expand it. The essay worked because it was grounded in a campus encounter and proposed something concrete (a service-based summer music program). It was not generic. Source: essaysthatworked.com/unc-chapel-hill\n\n**Key patterns from prior examples (from the CollegeVine database and Essays That Worked):**\nThe essays that got admitted students in had specific details (named clubs, named people, named events), a before/after structure showing real impact, and a clear connection between a personal quality and a community change. Generic \"I care about my community\" structures without specifics did not stand out.\n\n**For 2026-27, the personal statement is the only essay.** It must carry what the two 250-word supplements used to do — show community impact, intellectual curiosity, character, and fit — in 650 words. Admissions counselors quoted in DTH explicitly said the personal statement now faces more pressure to distinguish applicants.\n\n---\n\n## What separates strong essays\n\n**For the Common App personal statement (now the sole essay):**\n\n1. **Specificity over abstraction.** UNC's admissions team sees 76,000+ applications. Vague themes (perseverance, leadership) are invisible. A specific business idea you built, a specific failure with quantified loss, a specific community problem in your zip code — those stick.\n\n2. **Service-orientation is explicit in UNC's values.** Veer's entrepreneurship frame works if it connects to community impact. \"I built a product that solved X for Y community\" beats \"I learned to code.\"\n\n3. **The \"additional information\" section matters more now.** If something important doesn't fit the personal statement, use the 650-character Additional Information box intentionally.\n\n4. **Entrepreneurship angle for FBA:** The Assured Enrollment supplemental essay is where Veer explains his business curiosity. It should connect his specific ventures or initiatives to why he wants the Kenan-Flagler pathway — not just say \"I want to do business.\"\n\n5. **Rigor is the top factor.** UNC's CDS explicitly rates course rigor \"very important\" — the most important single factor. Essays land on top of an academic foundation.\n\n---\n\n## Clichés and mistakes to avoid\n\n- **Naming rankings:** Do not say \"UNC is a top public university\" or cite US News. Readers hear this all day.\n- **Generic community impact:** \"I volunteered at a food bank and it changed my perspective\" — too common. Name the specific food bank, the problem you identified, the change you created.\n- **The résumé dump:** Listing every achievement as if the essay is a CV. The personal statement should have one tight narrative, not a tour of activities.\n- **Parent / family as the reason for business interest:** Ross's admissions director says this directly (though that's Michigan — UNC admissions similarly wants the student's own \"why\"). \"My parents are entrepreneurs\" is not a story.\n- **Restating what's already on the application:** The additional information section is not a fifth activity slot. It's for context readers can't get from your transcript or activity list.\n- **Overpromising on impact:** UNC values service-orientation, but readers can tell when claimed impact doesn't match the scale of the activity. Be honest about what you actually did.\n\n---\n\n## Specifics an applicant can name\n\nThese are real, verifiable, and worth naming credibly in essays or Assured Enrollment application:\n\n**Kenan-Flagler / Business:**\n- BUSI 100 — Introduction to Business: People, Profit, Planet (FBA first-semester course)\n- BUSI 200 — Unlocking Your Leadership Potential (FBA first-year course, leadership focus)\n- The Luther Hodges Scholars program (selective honors track within UBP)\n- The TREBLE program (cross-disciplinary business + engineering track)\n\n**Shuford Program:**\n- ECON 125 — Introduction to Entrepreneurship (gateway, open to all)\n- ECON 325 — Entrepreneurship: Principles, Concepts, Frameworks, and Fluency\n- ECON 327 — Venture-Creation Workshop (name the specific track: commercial, scientific, sports, media)\n- ECON 390 — The Shuford Founder Accelerator (by application; for student founders)\n- Tom Scott (instructor, Shuford Founder Accelerator) — named in the course listing\n- Tom Collopy and Don Rose (ECON 327 instructors) — named on shuford.unc.edu/trackcourses\n\n**Programs and centers:**\n- Launch Chapel Hill — accelerator at Innovate Carolina Junction; new KPMG-funded deep-tech/AI track\n- 1789 Student Venture Lab and 1789 Student Venture Fund (seed grants; 37 teams competed spring 2026)\n- Carolina Challenge (campus pitch competition / makeathon)\n- APPLES Service-Learning program (mentioned credibly by admitted students)\n- BeAM Makerspace (fabrication/making — engineering adjacency)\n- Innovate Carolina — the university's innovation hub\n\n**Scholarships:**\n- Morehead-Cain — nomination required, full ride, entrepreneurship enrichment\n- Robertson Scholars — cross-campus with Duke, full ride\n\n**Caution:** Do not say \"Shuford has a commercial, social, scientific, and artistic track\" — it now also has sports, media, and more. Say \"the commercial track in ECON 327\" or \"I'd start with ECON 125 to qualify for the Shuford minor.\"\n\n---\n\n## Coaching questions\n\nThese are for the Assured Enrollment supplemental essay and the Common App personal statement. Push past the surface.\n\n1. You're describing entrepreneurship as your interest. Can you name a specific moment — a conversation, a failure, a sale, a user's reaction — that made you realize business was your thing and not just a label you've adopted?\n\n2. The Kenan-Flagler Assured Enrollment essay asks how business aligns with your academic and future goals. What specific business problem do you want to solve by age 30? If you don't have an answer yet, why is UNC the place to figure it out?\n\n3. Your personal statement is now the only essay UNC will read. What story can only you tell — not your cousin, not your classmate, not anyone else in the IL applicant pool?\n\n4. You've (presumably) done something entrepreneurial. What did it cost you? What actually went wrong? Failure is more interesting to readers than success — and more believable.\n\n5. UNC values service-orientation explicitly. Who in your community benefited from what you built or did? Can you name them, describe a specific interaction, show a concrete outcome?\n\n6. You're OOS. UNC admits roughly 1 in 10 OOS applicants. Your application has to clear a higher bar with no supplemental essays to help. What does your personal statement do in 650 words that makes a reader say \"this student would enhance this campus\"?\n\n7. If you're applying to Kenan-Flagler Assured Enrollment, why Kenan-Flagler specifically — not Ross, not Wharton, not Stern? What about the Research Triangle ecosystem, or the FBA cohort experience, or a specific Kenan-Flagler resource (Hodges Scholars, global program) pulls you toward Chapel Hill?\n\n8. The Shuford Program requires a 320-hour internship. Do you already have a relationship with a startup or founder in the Research Triangle or Lincolnshire area that could turn into that internship? If not, how would you find it?\n\n9. Your activities list is limited to 10 slots. What do you cut, and what does that cutting reveal about your priorities? Is your first activity the most important one?\n\n10. UNC does not consider demonstrated interest. That means your personal statement and activity list have to do all the work. Are you relying on \"I love UNC's culture\" language anywhere in your draft? Cut it.\n\n11. The Additional Information section (650 chars) is now more important than ever at UNC since there's no supplements. What goes there that your personal statement and activities can't cover?\n\n12. If UNC asks \"why did you do what you did\" — not what you did — what's your honest answer? The \"why\" is where character lives. The \"what\" is on your résumé.\n\n---\n\n## Sources\n\n| Source | URL | Date / Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| CDS 2025-26 (Fall 2025 cohort) | https://oira.unc.edu/wp-content/uploads/sites/297/2026/06/CDS_UNCCH_2025-26_20260615.pdf | OOS/IS breakdown; overall admit rate |\n| CDS 2024-25 (Fall 2024 cohort) | https://oira.unc.edu/wp-content/uploads/sites/297/2025/08/CDS_UNCCH_2024-25_20250829.pdf | In-state 38%, OOS 6.6% |\n| CollegeNumbers OOS/IS aggregate | https://collegenumbers.com/school/university-of-north-carolina-at-chapel-hill | Confirms 6.6% OOS rate for fall 2024 |\n| UNC admissions — supplement removal announcement | https://admissions.unc.edu/short-essays-removed-from-carolinas-2026-2027-application/ | Jun 19, 2026 |\n| UNC admissions — 2026-27 prompts page | https://admissions.unc.edu/application-prompts-for-2025-2026/ | 7 Common App prompts; no supplements |\n| Daily Tar Heel — supplement removal article | https://dailytarheel.com/270360/university/university-removes-supplemental-essays-2026-2027-application/ | Oct 6, 2026; quotes Rosenberg and counselors |\n| UNC Dec 18, 2025 EA news release | https://www.unc.edu/posts/2025/12/18/carolina-delivers-first-nc-admissions-decisions-a-month-sooner/ | 300+ business Assured Enrollment offers; OOS timeline |\n| Kenan-Flagler Assured Enrollment — admissions.unc.edu | https://admissions.unc.edu/apply/special-opportunities/business/ | EA-only requirement; FBA overview |\n| Kenan-Flagler FBA — Future Business Admits detail | https://www.kenan-flagler.unc.edu/programs/undergraduate-business/admissions/future-business-admits/ | BUSI 100, BUSI 200, first-year pathway |\n| Kenan-Flagler high school students page | https://www.kenan-flagler.unc.edu/programs/undergraduate-business/admissions/high-school-students/ | Selection criteria; prerequisites; supplemental essay note |\n| Shuford curriculum | https://shuford.unc.edu/curriculum | 5 courses + 320-hour internship; sequence |\n| Shuford track courses | https://shuford.unc.edu/trackcourses | ECON 327 sections; ECON 390 Founder Accelerator |\n| UNC catalog — Entrepreneurship Minor | https://catalog.unc.edu/undergraduate/programs-study/entrepreneurship-minor/ | Official course list |\n| Essays That Worked — UNC examples | https://essaysthatworked.com/unc-chapel-hill | Admitted essays 2022-26 |\n| Insaeng Substack — NAIL club essay | https://insaeng.substack.com/p/unc-chapel-hill-supplemental-essays | 2024 admitted essay with context |\n| College Transitions — UNC profile | https://www.collegetransitions.com/blog/how-to-get-into-unc-chapel-hill-admissions-data-strategies/ | Admissions officer quote; legacy 15%; no demonstrated interest |\n","ucla":"## Part 1 — Verified application facts (from the Founder's Compass dashboard)\n\n# University of California, Los Angeles (id: ucla)\n\nPlatform: UC Application | Test: Test-blind | Selectivity: {\"rate\":\"10.8%\",\"tier\":\"Most selective\",\"note\":\"UC Application only — no Common App. Test-blind (scores are not considered at all). 10.8% for fall 2026 (Class of 2030) — Berkeley edged UCLA this cycle at 10.5%. Out-of-state and international students are admitted at full pay.\",\"cycle\":\"prior\"}\n\nDeadlines: [{\"plan\":\"UC application filing period\",\"date\":\"Oct 1–Dec 2\",\"note\":\"One filing window, no early/regular split. Start the app Aug 1; submit by Dec 2.\",\"cycle\":\"ok\"}]\n\nApp note: UCLA uses the UC Application (not the Common App). You answer 4 of 8 Personal Insight Questions, 350 words each. There are no UCLA-specific supplements and no letters of recommendation.\n\nEntrepreneurship headline: UCLA's entrepreneurship runs campus-wide rather than through one school — Startup UCLA's accelerator, an Anderson-run undergraduate minor, the Price Center and Bruin Entrepreneurs — all supercharged by Los Angeles.\n\nDegrees: Entrepreneurship Minor (via Anderson) · pairs with economics, engineering, computer science and the arts — no standalone undergrad business major\n\n## Supplements (verified 2026-27 where cycle=ok)\nChoose any 4 of these 8 prompts (350 words each). They are standardized across every UC campus.\n\n- **PIQ 1 — Leadership** [Pick 4 of 8, 350 words, cycle=ok]: Describe an example of your leadership experience in which you have positively influenced others, helped resolve disputes or contributed to group efforts over time.\n- **PIQ 2 — Creativity** [Pick 4 of 8, 350 words, cycle=ok]: Every person has a creative side, and it can be expressed in many ways: problem solving, original and innovative thinking, and artistically, to name a few. Describe how you express your creative side.\n- **PIQ 3 — Greatest talent or skill** [Pick 4 of 8, 350 words, cycle=ok]: What would you say is your greatest talent or skill? How have you developed and demonstrated that talent over time?\n- **PIQ 4 — Educational opportunity or barrier** [Pick 4 of 8, 350 words, cycle=ok]: Describe how you have taken advantage of a significant educational opportunity or worked to overcome an educational barrier you have faced.\n- **PIQ 5 — Significant challenge** [Pick 4 of 8, 350 words, cycle=ok]: Describe the most significant challenge you have faced and the steps you have taken to overcome this challenge. How has this challenge affected your academic achievement?\n- **PIQ 6 — Academic subject that inspires you** [Pick 4 of 8, 350 words, cycle=ok]: Think about an academic subject that inspires you. Describe how you have furthered this interest inside and/or outside of the classroom.\n- **PIQ 7 — Making your community better** [Pick 4 of 8, 350 words, cycle=ok]: What have you done to make your school or your community a better place?\n- **PIQ 8 — What makes you a strong candidate** [Pick 4 of 8, 350 words, cycle=ok]: Beyond what has already been shared in your application, what do you believe makes you a strong candidate for admissions to the University of California?\n\n## Programs\n- Startup UCLA: Campus accelerator and summer program for student founders, with the Eggers seed-funding prize.\n- Entrepreneurship Minor (Anderson): Undergraduate minor delivered through the UCLA Anderson School of Management — unusual access to a top b-school.\n- Price Center for Entrepreneurship & Innovation: Anderson's hub for entrepreneurship education, competitions and the Anderson Venture Accelerator.\n- Bruin Entrepreneurs: Student-run network running Startup Labs, 1,000 Pitches and campus founder programming.\n\n## Funding/competitions\n- Knapp Venture Competition: Undergraduate startup competition with a $100K prize pool (≈$40K first place).\n- Eggers Seed Funding Prize: Alumni-backed seed prizes for Bruin startups via Startup UCLA, plus an annual investor pitch barbecue.\n- Magnify Incubator (CNSI): On-campus deep-tech/biotech incubator supporting science-driven ventures.\n\n## Watch-outs\n- No dedicated undergraduate business school — entrepreneurship is a campus-wide ecosystem you must seek out.\n- Many majors are 'impacted' (extra-selective) at 33,000+ undergrads.\n- Test-blind: grades, rigor and the four PIQs carry everything.\n\n## Known successful-essay sources\n- 20 UC Personal Insight Question examples (College Essay Guy) https://www.collegeessayguy.com/blog/uc-essay-examples-personal-insight-questions — 2026–27 · all 8 PIQs (shared across UCs), AO commentary\n- 18 UCLA essays that worked (Essays That Worked) https://essaysthatworked.com/ucla-essay-examples — 2022–26 · UCLA-admitted PIQ essays, by prompt\n- 12 UC essay examples (CollegeVine) https://blog.collegevine.com/university-of-california-essay-examples — 2023 · real PIQ essays with analysis\n\n## Other fields\n- short: UCLA\n- mono: U\n- city: Los Angeles\n- state: CA\n- type: Public\n- setting: Urban · Westwood\n- undergrad: ≈33,500\n- platform: UC Application\n- ac: #2774AE\n- ac2: #003B5C\n- fit: 80\n- verdict: Strong fit\n- sub: {\"program\":6,\"experiential\":7,\"outcomes\":8,\"location\":9}\n- rateNum: 10.8\n- dlSort: 1202\n- testPolicy: Test-blind\n- sat: —\n- act: —\n- fee: $80 / campus\n- recs: Not used for freshmen\n- opens: Aug 1 opens · Oct 1–Dec 2 submit\n- bestFor: California residents chasing elite value, and out-of-state students who want a global brand inside LA's media, tech and biotech economy. Best for builders who'll self-assemble across Startup UCLA, the Anderson entrepreneurship minor and Bruin Entrepreneurs.\n- strengths: [\"A top public university with national brand power and elite research across the board.\",\"Los Angeles wires students into entertainment, media-tech, biotech and a deep VC scene.\",\"Huge, loyal alumni base and a powerhouse athletics culture.\",\"A new Eggers Initiative is funding entrepreneurship courses to reach Bruins earlier.\"]\n- entreRank: No dedicated undergrad entrepreneurship ranking; ecosystem + LA are the draw\n- founders: A massive LA alumni network across entertainment, media-tech and biotech, with proximity to Silicon Beach venture capital.\n- optIntro: Choose any 4 of these 8 prompts (350 words each). They are standardized across every UC campus.\n- cost: {\"mode\":\"public\",\"inState\":\"≈$41,000–$45,000\",\"outState\":\"≈$73,000–$76,000\",\"aid\":\"Strong need-based aid for California residents (Blue and Gold covers system tuition under income thresholds); Regents Scholarship is the top merit award. Limited aid for out-of-state students.\"}\n- cardEnt: Startup UCLA · Anderson entrepreneurship minor · LA ecosystem.\n- src: Sourced from UCLA Undergraduate Admission and the UC Application (test-blind; 8 PIQs confirmed 2026-27), UCLA cost pages (Mar 2026), ugeducation.ucla.edu and Startup UCLA. Acceptance rate is the most recent published figure.\n\n## Part 2 — Deep research: paths in, what readers want, admitted essays\n\n# UCLA — Advisor Knowledge Base\n\n*Applicant: Veer (out-of-state, Illinois). Entrepreneurship focus. Applying Fall 2027.*\n\n---\n\n## Paths in\n\n### The one gate: UC Application\nUCLA uses only the UC Application (no Common App). There is no Early Decision, Early Action, or Regular Decision split — one filing window, Oct 1–Nov 30, with decisions in late March. No demonstrated interest is tracked. Campus visits, emails, and rep meetings are good for your own research but irrelevant to your file. No legacy preference. No interviews for most students.\n\n### Overall selectivity\nFall 2026: 10.8% overall admit rate (all applicants). Fall 2024: 9%. Medians for admitted first-years: weighted GPA 4.60, unweighted 4.00. The vast majority of applicants have near-perfect unweighted GPAs.\n\n### Out-of-state realities\nUCLA is legally required to prioritize California residents. For fall 2026, of UCLA's 15,903 admitted first-years, 57% were California residents, 30% were domestic out-of-state, and 13% were international. (Source: UCOP Fall 2026 Preliminary Admissions Summary, https://www.ucop.edu/institutional-research-academic-planning/_files/factsheets/2026/admissions-fact-sheet-data-tables.pdf.) For fall 2024, domestic OOS admits were 2,949 out of 13,128 total admits. Total OOS applicants are not published by residency, but the effective OOS domestic admit rate is estimated at approximately 5–7%, materially lower than the headline rate. Out-of-state students pay full freight: approximately $76,000–$77,000 per year (2024–25). Aid is very limited for OOS students. No Blue and Gold Program for non-residents.\n\n### Which school you apply to matters enormously\nUCLA's application is sorted into five colleges and schools. The mechanism differs by school:\n\n**The College (Letters and Science):** The largest school (~66% of applicants). Applicants choose a major or apply undeclared, but **major is not considered in the review**. If you apply to The College, you are competing in a single College pool. Admit rate: roughly 11% (fall 2024). Divisions range from Social Sciences (9.3%) to Physical Sciences (14%). Business Economics is a major in The College (Social Sciences division).\n\n**Samueli School of Engineering:** Admits **by major** — each major is its own separate competition. Fall 2025 rates by Samueli major: Aerospace Engineering 4.7%, Computer Science 7.3%, Computer Science & Engineering 7.2%, Electrical Engineering 8.7%, Mechanical Engineering 3.9%, Bioengineering 8.1%, overall Samueli 5.3%. (Source: https://admission.ucla.edu/apply/first-year/first-year-profile/2025/major.) These are total admit rates; OOS rates within Samueli are lower. Samueli applicants must list a specific major or Undeclared Engineering — switching after admission is not guaranteed.\n\n**Arts & Architecture, Music, Nursing, Theater/Film/TV:** Separate supplemental applications are triggered after you submit the UC App. Nursing: 0.5–0.9% admit rate — extremely selective.\n\n**Internal transfer:** Possible from community colleges (TAG agreements apply to UC broadly, not necessarily to UCLA-specific majors). Switching majors inside UCLA after enrollment is possible but competitive for impacted majors.\n\n### The business-minded path at UCLA\nThere is no undergraduate business school at UCLA. Anderson is exclusively graduate. The closest options:\n\n**Business Economics BA (The College, Social Sciences):** Economics-centered major with accounting and Anderson management coursework. Described by UCLA as \"a focused program grounded in economic theory, quantitative analysis, and accounting. It does not replicate a traditional undergraduate business curriculum.\" (Source: https://economics.ucla.edu/undergraduate/current-students/majors-and-minors/business-economics.) Declared at UCLA after enrollment via a competitive application process with a Primary Score Calculator. Not guaranteed at admission — you declare once at UCLA. Top 10 most-enrolled major campus-wide.\n\n**Anderson Entrepreneurship Minor:** Open to undergraduates from any major. Core courses: MGMT 159 Foundations of Business and Entrepreneurship, MGMT 160 Entrepreneurship and Venture Initiation, MGMT 161 Business Plan Development, MGMT 169 Entrepreneurial Leadership and Practical Experience. Plus three electives. Requires 3.0 GPA. Competitive admission process after enrollment. Entrepreneurship was the 8th most popular minor in fall 2024. (Source: https://www.anderson.ucla.edu/undergraduate-education/undergraduate-minor-in-entrepreneurship.) Quote from alumna Riley Kuffner: \"In my opinion, the minor in entrepreneurship is the best undergraduate business education you can get at UCLA. You are learning directly from serial entrepreneurs with exits under their belt.\"\n\n**Practical path for Veer:** Apply to The College, list Business Economics (or Economics or a complementary major like CS). Pursue the Anderson Entrepreneurship Minor, Startup UCLA, and Bruin Entrepreneurs after enrollment. The ecosystem is there, but you have to assemble it yourself.\n\n---\n\n## What readers want\n\nUC systemwide policy defines 13 comprehensive review factors. All campuses use them; weighting varies year to year and reader by reader. The factors are (from https://admission.universityofcalifornia.edu/counselors/preparing-freshman-students/comprehensive-review.html):\n\n1. Academic GPA in A-G courses (including honors weighting, cap at 8 honors courses)\n2. Number and performance in courses beyond minimum A-G requirements\n3. AP/IB/honors course performance (adjusted for what is available at your school)\n4. ELC status (top 9% of your high school class)\n5. Senior-year program quality\n6. Academic performance relative to your school's opportunities\n7. Outstanding achievement in a specific academic subject\n8. Outstanding work in special academic projects\n9. Recent marked improvement in academic performance\n10. Special talents, achievements, awards, leadership, community service\n11. Special projects undertaken in or alongside HS curriculum\n12. Academic accomplishments in light of life experiences and circumstances\n13. Location of secondary school and residence (for geographic diversity)\n\nUCLA's guide for applicants says: \"All applications are reviewed twice by two separate/independent admission professionals.\" No formula. No minimum GPA. Samueli and The College are reviewed separately.\n\nKey direct quotes from UC admissions directors (via College Essay Guy, https://www.collegeessayguy.com/blog/uc-essay-examples-personal-insight-questions):\n- \"It's okay to answer the questions in a direct, straightforward way.\"\n- \"Bullet points? Like, really really? Yup.\"\n- Readers spend roughly 4–8 minutes on the *entire application*, not per essay. Write to be skimmed.\n- PIQs \"correlate directly\" to the 13 comprehensive review factors. UC directors have said this publicly.\n\nUCLA does not consider demonstrated interest. No bonus for visiting campus.\n\n---\n\n## Admitted essays: what they did\n\nBecause the PIQs are shared across all UC campuses, admitted-essay examples from any campus apply. Sources with full essay text: College Essay Guy (https://www.collegeessayguy.com/blog/uc-essay-examples-personal-insight-questions), Essays That Worked (https://essaysthatworked.com/ucla-essay-examples), CollegeVine (https://blog.collegevine.com/university-of-california-essay-examples).\n\n**Pattern 1 — Leadership (PIQ 1) done through a venture:**\nAdmitted students who founded something wrote about a *specific problem identified*, the *friction of building*, and the *effect on a real person*. The angle that worked was not \"I am a leader.\" It was \"I saw X, I built Y, here is the specific user or moment that changed how I think about Z.\" The leadership evidence came through the story of a stumble and a pivot. Not a résumé summary.\n\n**Pattern 2 — Creativity (PIQ 2) as systems thinking:**\nSuccessful essays avoided arts and music unless truly distinctive. Students who framed creative thinking as building something (code, a business model, a new process) and showed what they were thinking, not just what they did, stood out. One strong structure: present an unexpected creative output, then unpack the mental model behind it.\n\n**Pattern 3 — Greatest talent (PIQ 3) with stakes:**\nAdmitted essays picked a skill that was legible to a reader in 30 seconds, then showed depth through an unusual application of that skill. For entrepreneurial students, this has worked with: sales intuition, pattern recognition in markets, coding, or persuasion. The essay had to *demonstrate* the skill, not just claim it.\n\n**Pattern 4 — Academic subject (PIQ 6) beyond the classroom:**\nEssays that worked described a rabbit hole: a class concept that led to self-directed research, a real project, or a conversation with someone outside school. The key is showing intellectual initiative — not just \"economics fascinates me\" but \"I ran a pricing experiment at my lemonade stand and discovered that demand curves are messier in real life than in the textbook.\"\n\n**Pattern 5 — What makes you a strong candidate (PIQ 8):**\nThis prompt is often the weakest. Admitted students who did it well used it as a synthesis: something not visible anywhere else in the application, or a direct connection between their background and what they will do with a UCLA education. Résumé summaries failed here.\n\nSpecific entrepreneurial admitted profiles (Berkeley News, fall 2025 admits — same PIQ system as UCLA): One admitted student \"created an app to help low-income families overcome challenges in finding housing...by matching tenants and properties via AI-driven models.\" Another \"co-founded a product to detect land mines using drone flyby footage.\" The news release noted: \"Many of this year's class of admitted students are entrepreneurial and concerned about the public good.\" (Source: https://news.berkeley.edu/2025/07/28/uc-berkeley-extends-over-700-additional-admission-offers-to-first-year-students-for-fall-2025/) These profiles point to PIQ essays that combined a specific problem, a built solution, and community impact — not generic founder narratives.\n\nNote: Full essay text from UCLA-specific admits is not publicly released in full. The Essays That Worked site (https://essaysthatworked.com/ucla-essay-examples) has 18 UCLA-admitted PIQ essays with structural notes.\n\n---\n\n## What separates strong essays\n\n1. **Specificity over scope.** A reader who skims in 4 minutes needs to see a concrete scene, name, number, or result in the first two sentences. \"I started a business\" fails. \"I turned a spreadsheet of sixty families' food pantry needs into a weekly delivery route that cut spoilage by 40%\" works.\n\n2. **The PIQ shows what grades and activities don't.** Admitted essays added a new dimension — a belief, a method, a contradiction, a failure — that is invisible in the activities list.\n\n3. **The so-what is earned.** The last 50–75 words of each PIQ should land on why this shapes how the applicant will contribute at UCLA. Not \"UCLA's resources will help me,\" but \"I want to run the Knapp Venture Competition.\"\n\n4. **Voice reads like a person, not a college essay.** UC directors explicitly said straightforward writing is fine. That means no tortured metaphors, no opening with a dictionary definition, no false profundity.\n\n5. **Four PIQs work as a portfolio.** Each one illuminates a different facet: academic, personal, leadership/community, wildcard. Readers notice when all four essays cover the same idea from four angles.\n\n---\n\n## Clichés and mistakes to avoid\n\n- **Résumé-dump founding story.** Listing what your startup did without showing what you learned or how it challenged you. UC readers see hundreds of these.\n- **PIQ 8 as a \"Why UCLA\" essay.** UCLA explicitly does not track interest. Use PIQ 8 to reveal something new about yourself, not to flatter the school.\n- **All four essays on entrepreneurship.** Even for a builder, portfolio variety matters. If every PIQ is about a company you started, readers see narrowness, not depth.\n- **Vague leadership.** \"I led my team through a difficult time\" means nothing. A reader needs: what team, what difficulty, what decision did you make, what happened.\n- **Overusing words like \"passionate,\" \"driven,\" \"innovative.\"** These are filters for weak essays.\n- **Ignoring the academic dimension.** UCLA cares deeply about intellectual rigor. An all-extracurricular portfolio without a strong PIQ 6 (academic subject) signals that the applicant might not belong academically.\n- **Samueli applicants applying to The College as a backup strategy without understanding the difference.** You cannot switch from The College to Samueli easily. Pick the right school to apply to.\n\n---\n\n## Specifics an applicant can name\n\nThese are real, nameable, credible resources for a builder at UCLA. An applicant who can speak to these with genuine knowledge (not just a list) will be more compelling.\n\n**Programs:**\n- **Startup UCLA** — campus accelerator, summer program for student founders, Eggers seed-funding prize. Note that the Eggers Initiative is now funding entrepreneurship courses earlier (the \"Eggers Initiative\" is a real named program).\n- **Bruin Entrepreneurs** — student-run network; runs \"Startup Labs\" and \"1,000 Pitches\" events.\n- **Price Center for Entrepreneurship & Innovation** — Anderson's hub; runs Anderson Venture Accelerator.\n- **Anderson Entrepreneurship Minor** — the specific four core courses (MGMT 159, 160, 161, 169) are credible to name; do not claim to have taken them.\n- **Knapp Venture Competition** — UCLA's undergraduate startup competition with a ~$100K prize pool (roughly $40K first place). This is the specific competition to name, not a generic \"business competition.\"\n- **Magnify Incubator (CNSI)** — on-campus deep-tech/biotech incubator; less known, useful for science-adjacent ventures.\n\n**Courses (Anderson-taught, open to undergrads):**\n- MGMT 162 Entrepreneurship and Technology Commercialization\n- MGMT 163 Entrepreneurship and New Product Development\n- MGMT 164 Entrepreneurial Finance and Accounting\n- ECON 106E Economics of Entrepreneurship\n\n**Faculty areas (without overclaiming):** Anderson's entrepreneurship faculty have included serial entrepreneurs and former VCs — the minor's design is explicitly around practitioners, not theorists.\n\n**Common mistake:** Naming \"UCLA Anderson School of Management\" as if you could attend it as an undergraduate. Anderson is graduate-only. You access it through the minor.\n\n**LA ecosystem:** Silicon Beach (Venice/Santa Monica VC corridor), media-tech overlaps (streaming, gaming), biotech at UCLA Health. These are real geographic advantages worth naming if genuinely relevant.\n\n---\n\n## Coaching questions\n\nThese are the questions a top counselor would press Veer on for his PIQs. Push past the first answer every time.\n\n1. You say you started or built something. Who was the first real customer or user — not a friend, not a family member — and what did their reaction tell you that you didn't already know?\n\n2. What is the specific moment you realized your idea or approach was wrong? What did you change, and what did you have to give up to change it?\n\n3. If you had to explain your leadership style to someone who watched you at your worst moment as a leader, what would they say about you?\n\n4. UCLA has no business school. A lot of entrepreneurs go to places with Wharton or Haas. What specifically will you do with the ecosystem UCLA does have — Startup UCLA, the Anderson minor, Bruin Entrepreneurs — that you couldn't do anywhere else?\n\n5. PIQ 6 asks about an academic subject that inspires you. What is the most interesting idea you've encountered in a class that connects to what you build? Name the class, the concept, and the specific problem you started thinking about afterward.\n\n6. Your Activities section lists what you did. Your PIQs need to show what you think. Pick one thing you built or led and tell me what you believe now that you didn't believe before you started.\n\n7. UC readers spend 4–8 minutes on your whole application. Read your four PIQs out loud. Where does a reader's attention drift? What sentence are you least proud of?\n\n8. Business Economics at UCLA is not a business degree — it's economics with some management coursework. If you're not admitted to an engineering-track, how do you build the knowledge you need to be a technical founder while also building a business foundation?\n\n9. PIQ 7 (community) and PIQ 1 (leadership) can overlap dangerously. If you choose both, what is the genuinely different thing each one reveals about you?\n\n10. What is the thing you have built that you are most embarrassed to talk about because it failed? And why is that actually your best essay?\n\n11. Describe how you would use LA — not UCLA, but the city of Los Angeles — to pursue your entrepreneurial goals in a way that a student in Chicago or Boston couldn't.\n\n12. Test-blind means your GPA and course rigor have to do the heavy lifting your test scores can't. Walk me through your most challenging semester. What did you take, what did you earn, and what would a reader conclude about your academic readiness?\n\n---\n\n## Sources\n\n- UC Application PIQ prompts (official, cycle=ok): https://admission.universityofcalifornia.edu/how-to-apply/applying-as-a-first-year/personal-insight-questions.html\n- UC comprehensive review factors: https://admission.universityofcalifornia.edu/counselors/preparing-freshman-students/comprehensive-review.html\n- BOARS Guidelines for Comprehensive Review: https://senate.universityofcalifornia.edu/_files/committees/boars/documents/guidelines-implementation-of-ug-admission.pdf\n- UCLA First-Year Profile Fall 2024 (admit rate 9%, GPA data): https://admission.ucla.edu/apply/first-year/first-year-profile/2024\n- UCLA First-Year Profile by Major Fall 2024 (Samueli rates): https://admission.ucla.edu/apply/first-year/first-year-profile/2024/major\n- UCLA First-Year Profile by Major Fall 2025 (Samueli rates updated): https://admission.ucla.edu/apply/first-year/first-year-profile/2025/major\n- UCOP Fall 2026 Admissions Fact Sheet (OOS admit breakdown): https://www.ucop.edu/institutional-research-academic-planning/_files/factsheets/2026/admissions-fact-sheet-data-tables.pdf\n- UCLA Undergraduate Profile 2024-25 (top majors, enrollment): https://www.ugeducation.ucla.edu/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/UndergradProfile2024-2025_FINAL.pdf\n- Business Economics major description: https://economics.ucla.edu/undergraduate/current-students/majors-and-minors/business-economics\n- Anderson Entrepreneurship Minor: https://www.anderson.ucla.edu/undergraduate-education/undergraduate-minor-in-entrepreneurship\n- Anderson Entrepreneurship Minor requirements: https://www.anderson.ucla.edu/undergraduate-education/undergraduate-minor-in-entrepreneurship/requirements\n- Regents Policy 2102 (no legacy preference, comprehensive review policy): https://regents.universityofcalifornia.edu/governance/policies/2102.html\n- College Essay Guy PIQ examples and AO commentary: https://www.collegeessayguy.com/blog/uc-essay-examples-personal-insight-questions\n- Essays That Worked — UCLA admitted PIQ essays: https://essaysthatworked.com/ucla-essay-examples\n- CollegeVine UC essay examples: https://blog.collegevine.com/university-of-california-essay-examples\n- Berkeley News 2025 admit class profile (entrepreneurial profiles, same PIQ system): https://news.berkeley.edu/2025/07/28/uc-berkeley-extends-over-700-additional-admission-offers-to-first-year-students-for-fall-2025/\n- UCLA Samueli Engineering Admission Statistics (UCEE Report 2024): https://www.seasoasa.ucla.edu/wp-content/uploads/seasoasa/2024-UCEE-Report.pdf\n","usc":"## Part 1 — Verified application facts (from the Founder's Compass dashboard)\n\n# University of Southern California (id: usc)\n\nPlatform: Common App | Test: Test-optional | Selectivity: {\"rate\":\"11.2%\",\"tier\":\"Highly selective\",\"note\":\"Fall 2025 admit rate. USC expanded binding Early Decision to nearly all programs starting with Fall 2027.\",\"cycle\":\"prior\"}\n\nDeadlines: [{\"plan\":\"Early Decision (binding) / Early Action\",\"date\":\"Nov 1\",\"note\":\"New ED option for Fall 2027; decisions mid-December. Nov 1 is also the merit-scholarship priority.\",\"cycle\":\"ok\"},{\"plan\":\"Regular Decision\",\"date\":\"Jan 10\",\"note\":\"Some programs (e.g., arts) have earlier dates\",\"cycle\":\"ok\"}]\n\nApp note: USC uses the Common App: the personal statement plus a required USC academic-interest essay, a set of fun short-answer 'quick takes,' and school-specific essays (e.g., Viterbi, Dornsife). 2026-27 prompts confirmed unchanged (Counsely, Aug 2, 2026).\n\nEntrepreneurship headline: USC pairs the Lloyd Greif Center — one of the oldest and most decorated undergraduate entrepreneurship programs in the country — with the singular Iovine and Young Academy and a $300M+ startup-incubator track record.\n\nDegrees: BS in Business Administration (Entrepreneurship), Greif · BS in Arts, Technology & the Business of Innovation (Iovine and Young) · university-wide entrepreneurship minor\n\n## Supplements (verified 2026-27 where cycle=ok)\nConfirmed 2026-27 via Counsely (Aug 2, 2026) — prompts are unchanged from 2025-26.\n\n- **Your academic interests & why USC** [Required, 250 words, cycle=ok]: Describe how you plan to pursue your academic interests and why you want to explore them at USC specifically. Please feel free to address your first- and second-choice major selections.\n- **USC 'quick takes' (short answers)** [Required, ≤100 characters each, cycle=ok]: A set of rapid-fire prompts, e.g.: describe yourself in three words · favorite snack · best movie of all time · dream job · your life's theme song · dream trip · the TV show you'll binge next · favorite book · a class you would teach.\n\n## Programs\n- Lloyd Greif Center for Entrepreneurial Studies: Marshall's entrepreneurship engine — a BS in Business Administration (Entrepreneurship) plus a university-wide minor.\n- Iovine and Young Academy: BS in Arts, Technology and the Business of Innovation; project-based, with many students commercializing startups pre-graduation. New dean arrives Aug 2026.\n- USC Incubator / Blackstone LaunchPad: Campus incubator with 285+ portfolio companies; alumni ventures have raised $300M+.\n- Viterbi & Marshall venture courses: Cross-school startup courses feeding engineering and business founders.\n\n## Funding/competitions\n- New Venture Seed Competition: Greif Center competition with a $150K+ prize pool for student ventures.\n- Incubator seed funding: Seed capital and incubation for USC student startups across schools.\n- Trojan investor network: An unusually active base of alumni angels and VCs who back USC founders.\n\n## Watch-outs\n- Sticker price is steep and merit aid was scaled back in 2025 — model your real cost.\n- Highly selective overall, and Iovine and Young is its own ultra-selective application.\n- Essay prompts confirmed 2026-27 (Aug 2): \"Why USC\" essay (250 words) + 10 Quick Takes — identical to 2025-26.\n\n## Known successful-essay sources\n- How to write the USC supplements — guide + examples (College Essay Guy) https://www.collegeessayguy.com/blog/university-southern-california-usc-supplemental-essays — 2025–26 · 'Why USC' + Viterbi/Dornsife examples\n- 3 USC essay examples by accepted students (CollegeVine) https://blog.collegevine.com/usc-essay-examples — 2023 · real admitted essays with analysis\n- 4 USC essays that worked (Essays That Worked) https://essaysthatworked.com/usc-supplemental-essays — 2021–23 · admitted essays for USC prompts\n\n## Other fields\n- short: USC\n- mono: U\n- city: Los Angeles\n- state: CA\n- type: Private\n- setting: Urban · University Park\n- undergrad: ≈21,000\n- platform: Common App\n- ac: #990000\n- ac2: #5e0000\n- fit: 90\n- verdict: Elite for builders\n- sub: {\"program\":10,\"experiential\":9,\"outcomes\":8,\"location\":9}\n- rateNum: 11.2\n- dlSort: 1101\n- testPolicy: Test-optional\n- sat: ≈1480–1560\n- act: ≈33–35\n- fee: $85\n- recs: 1 counselor + 1 teacher\n- opens: August 1\n- bestFor: Founders who want a dedicated, storied entrepreneurship program — the Greif Center, one of the oldest in the U.S. — or the one-of-a-kind Iovine and Young Academy (arts × tech × business), wrapped in LA and the Trojan network.\n- strengths: [\"A powerhouse in film, business, engineering and communications.\",\"The 'Trojan Family' alumni network is famously active in hiring and investing.\",\"Los Angeles location across entertainment, media-tech and venture capital.\",\"Iovine and Young Academy — a singular arts/technology/business degree where students launch startups before graduating.\"]\n- entreRank: Long ranked among the top U.S. undergraduate entrepreneurship programs (Princeton Review)\n- founders: A deep founder bench across media, consumer and tech; the Trojan network is a genuine fundraising and hiring advantage.\n- optIntro: Confirmed 2026-27 via Counsely (Aug 2, 2026) — prompts are unchanged from 2025-26.\n- cost: {\"mode\":\"private\",\"sticker\":\"≈$95,000 / yr\",\"aid\":\"Meets full demonstrated need; merit awards (Trustee/Presidential) exist but were scaled back in 2025 — verify current availability before counting on them.\"}\n- cardEnt: Greif Center (oldest in U.S.) · Iovine & Young Academy · LA.\n- src: Sourced from USC admission (ED expansion + Nov 1 / Jan 10 dates confirmed for Fall 2027), USC Facts (11.2% Fall 2025; $75,384 tuition) and iovine-young.usc.edu. Test-optional status for 2026-27 confirmed by multiple secondary sources (Cosmic College Consulting Jun 24, Empowerly Jun 15; ~58% submitted no test). Essay prompts confirmed 2026-27 by Counsely (live-crawled Aug 2, 2026): \"Why USC\" academic interest essay (250 words) + 10 Quick Takes unchanged.\n\n## Part 2 — Deep research: paths in, what readers want, admitted essays\n\n# USC — Knowledge Base for Veer (Fall 2027 entry)\n\n## Paths in\n\n### Admission plans: ED vs EA vs RD\n\nFor Fall 2027, USC offers three paths. All have the same Nov 1 deadline for early rounds.\n\n**Early Decision (ED) — binding.** New for Fall 2027, expanded from a Marshall-only pilot in Fall 2026. If admitted, you must enroll and withdraw all other applications. Decisions mid-December 2026. Available for nearly all programs except Kaufman Dance, Thornton Music, Dramatic Arts, Architecture, Art & Design, Cinematic Arts, and the World Bachelor of Business (WBB). Those programs remain RD-only (December 1 deadline). USC officially says not to apply ED just for a strategic boost — \"choose the plan that matches your level of certainty\" — but ED was expanded because demonstrated commitment matters. ED-specific admit rates will be reported publicly starting with Fall 2028 data. Source: USC Provost announcement, Feb 9 2026. https://www.provost.usc.edu/early-decision-admissions-fall-2027-applicants/\n\n**Early Action (EA) — non-binding.** Also Nov 1 deadline. Decisions mid-to-late January. Merit scholarship consideration is automatic with either EA or ED — no separate application required.\n\n**Regular Decision (RD).** Deadline January 10, 2027. Decisions by April 1. Students not admitted EA or ED are given the option to defer to RD automatically — no new application needed.\n\n**Admit rates (most recent cycle, Class of 2030):** EA ~9.5% (roughly 3,800 of 40,000+ early applicants admitted); RD ~9.2%; overall 11%. Approximately 40.6% of the admitted class came from early rounds. USC is need-blind. Source: Collegewise, June 30 2026. https://collegewise.com/blog/usc-acceptance-rate\n\n**Key implication for Veer:** If Marshall (entrepreneurship/Greif Center) is his first choice, ED is now available and signals commitment. ED decisions come a month earlier than EA decisions. If he needs to compare aid, EA is the non-binding alternative. Both get identical scholarship consideration.\n\n### Undergraduate schools and how selectivity differs\n\nUSC has no separate applications for most schools — everyone applies through the Common App to a stated major. The school you apply to affects supplemental essays (Viterbi and Dornsife have their own optional supplements) and how your application is routed internally.\n\n- **Marshall School of Business** (Entrepreneurship, Finance, etc.): Generally the most competitive business school track at USC. Applying directly to the BBA in Business Administration with an Entrepreneurship concentration signals clear intent and routes you to Marshall readers. ED was piloted here first in 2025 because demand was high.\n- **Viterbi School of Engineering**: Has its own optional supplement. Tech-business applicants sometimes combine Viterbi (CS) with a Marshall minor.\n- **Iovine and Young Academy (IYA)**: Separate, ultra-selective application process with its own SlideRoom supplement, essays, and interview. Described in detail below.\n- **Dornsife College of Letters, Arts and Sciences**: Broader acceptance; a fallback major here (e.g., cognitive science, business of cinematic arts) can serve as a second-choice when Marshall is first choice.\n\n### Iovine and Young Academy — its own application\n\nIYA awards a BS in Arts, Technology and the Business of Innovation. It is cohort-based, project-centered, and produces founders at a higher rate than any other USC program. Startups from the program have raised $120M+ in funding. A new dean arrived August 2026.\n\n**How to apply:** Select IYA as your first or second choice major on Common App (either EA, ED, or RD). In addition to the Common App, you must complete a separate SlideRoom supplement — this is mandatory. Without the SlideRoom materials, you will not be considered for IYA. You will still be considered for your other major choice or as undecided.\n\n**SlideRoom requirements:**\n1. **60-second introduction video** (first-year applicants only): A personal glimpse of your world — a day in your life, a recent project, what motivates you. Not a polished production; authenticity matters. The IYA site explicitly warns against \"selfie videos\" that are just a student talking to the camera.\n2. **Essay 1 (500 words max):** How will your contributions enhance the Academy's challenge-based, collaborative innovation process? Touch on background, traits, skills, experiences, personality.\n3. **Essay 2 (300 words max):** Choose concentration areas from the list below and explain why: Extended Reality / Transformative Artificial Intelligence / Business of Innovation / Interactive Technologies / Product Innovation / Design Strategy / Venture Development.\n4. **Optional portfolio (up to 4 items):** Can include design work, technology or science projects (code, schematics, papers), time-based media (video, games, audio), business ventures (business plans, marketing plans, graphs), or an original proposal video (max 1 minute, showing desirability + feasibility + viability of an idea). Each submission needs a title, description, and description of the student's specific role. Do not include anything accessible only via an external link — it will not be reviewed.\n\n**Two-stage selection:**\n1. Faculty review all SlideRoom materials.\n2. A shortlist receives interview invitations by late January. Virtual interviews for first-year applicants are February 13-14. Those not invited for an interview are still considered for their other major choice. Only interview invitees can be admitted to IYA.\n\nSource: iovine-young.usc.edu/apply/undergraduate/undergraduate-admissions-acad (live page, current for Fall 2027).\n\n**Note on IYA as second-choice major:** The current IYA admissions page (2026) says applicants \"may select any Iovine and Young Academy major as their 1st choice or 2nd choice major.\" An older 2023 blog post said IYA was first-choice only. The current official page takes precedence.\n\n### Marshall School and Lloyd Greif Center\n\nThe Greif Center is housed within Marshall. It runs:\n- BS in Business Administration with an Entrepreneurship concentration\n- A university-wide entrepreneurship minor (open to all USC undergrads)\n- The New Venture Seed Competition: $150K+ prize pool annually\n- The USC Incubator / Blackstone LaunchPad: 285+ portfolio companies, alumni ventures have raised $300M+\n\nApplying to Marshall does not require a separate supplement beyond the common \"Why USC\" essay (250 words). Viterbi or Dornsife have optional school-specific supplements.\n\n### Spring admission\n\nAll students who apply to USC are automatically considered for both fall and spring admission. You cannot opt out of spring consideration. Roughly 500-700 first-year students start in January each year. USC has offered spring admission since the early 1990s. Fall options for spring admits:\n1. Community college (most common) — take transferable credits locally\n2. Study abroad — USC has formal partnerships with five programs in Rome, Paris, London, Prague, and Lugano\n3. USC in D.C. First Semester — USC coursework at its Capital Campus in Washington D.C. (launched Fall 2025 for spring admits)\n4. True gap semester — work, travel, family time\n\nSpring admits can graduate on time with most majors if they arrive with AP/IB credit or community college credit. Some specialized programs are fall-only. Spring admission is a real outcome for competitive applicants — not a rejection. Source: USC Admission Blog, Dec 12 2025. https://www.admissionblog.usc.edu/p/spring-admission-at-usc\n\n### Legacy, demonstrated interest, test policy\n\nUSC is need-blind and does not publish a legacy policy advantage. No demonstrated interest tracking is publicly stated. Test-optional for 2026-27: among Class of 2030, 57% submitted scores; middle 50% SAT 1490-1550, ACT 33-35. Average unweighted GPA: 3.92 (all-time high for Class of 2030).\n\n---\n\n## What readers want\n\nUSC's Viterbi blog post from July 2026 states the official framing: \"Choose the application plan that best matches your level of certainty, not what you think might give you an advantage. The strongest application is one submitted when you're ready, with thoughtful essays, a complete application, and confidence.\" Source: https://viterbiadmission.usc.edu/2026/early-decision-early-action-or-regular-decision-which-application-plan-is-right-for-you/\n\nThe USC Common Data Set for Class of 2030 lists the five most important factors: academic achievement, rigor of secondary curriculum, essays, character/personal qualities, and letters of recommendation. Test scores are important but not decisive (less than 60% submitted).\n\nFor the \"Why USC\" essay, College Essay Guy (2026-27 guide) identifies what works: \"The best USC 'Why us?' essays don't celebrate how it's the GREATEST SCHOOL OF ALL TIME. They're more an explanation of why you and USC are the perfect match.\" Named specifics — programs, professors, labs — are essential. The guide explicitly lists six mistakes: writing about size, location, reputation, weather, or ranking; using emotional language without evidence; getting names of mascots or buildings wrong; parroting brochure language; describing generic traditions; and making it only about the school, not the fit. Source: https://www.collegeessayguy.com/blog/university-southern-california-usc-supplemental-essays\n\nFor IYA, the SlideRoom prompts specify that readers want to see: creative ability, original ideas, multi-disciplinary thinking, demonstrated teamwork, leadership, storytelling ability, and interest in new business models including entrepreneurship and social entrepreneurship.\n\n---\n\n## Admitted essays: what they did\n\n**Note on sources:** The CollegeVine examples (2023) are from prior-cycle prompts that no longer exist (\"10 minutes and a million people,\" \"something essential to understanding you\"). The current \"Why USC\" prompt is 250 words and launched in its current form for 2024-25. No verified admitted examples for the current exact \"Why USC\" prompt with full text were found from public sources. The College Essay Guy guide (updated October 8, 2026) provides structural analysis of what a strong \"Why USC\" essay does, cited here.\n\n**What strong \"Why USC\" essays do (from College Essay Guy analysis):**\n- Open with a specific tension or interest, not a grand declaration about USC.\n- Name specific programs with capital letters — not \"the entrepreneurship program\" but \"the Lloyd Greif Center's New Venture Seed Competition\" or \"IYA's Business of Innovation concentration.\"\n- Connect at least 3-5 reasons to USC that are unique to USC, not shared by peer schools.\n- Show two sides of the writer — the analytical/technical side and the creative/human side — and map both to USC offerings.\n- End with what the applicant brings to USC, not just what USC gives them.\n- A sample essay described in the guide: a student who identifies as both a data-driven thinker and a creative writer maps her analytical side to a specific Marshall course and her creative side to a Dornsife writing workshop, then closes by naming what she will bring to both communities. The essay reads as a match, not a pitch to a trophy. Source: https://www.collegeessayguy.com/blog/university-southern-california-usc-supplemental-essays\n\n**CollegeVine example 1 — \"Disagreement\" (prior-cycle prompt, included for structural lessons):**\nThe applicant argued that their identity — a deep love of debate and reasoning — drove them toward law and advocacy work. Specific details: interning at an immigration law firm, volunteering for a Board of Education candidate. Structure: personal trait → how it causes conflict in everyday life → how it channels into purposeful work. The essay avoids the trap of saying \"I am a debater\" — it shows debate in action. Source: https://blog.collegevine.com/usc-essay-examples\n\n**CollegeVine example 2 — \"Desensitization\" (prior-cycle prompt, structural lesson):**\nThe applicant wrote about digital desensitization and dark humor in Generation Z, using the Jeffrey Dahmer Netflix documentary as a launch point. The essay is short and punchy — opening with a rhetorical question (\"At what point does injustice become background noise?\") then building quickly to a personal turn. CollegeVine notes this works because the topic is personal and the writer avoided over-explanation. The weakness (also noted): the conclusion restated what the essay already showed. Source: https://blog.collegevine.com/usc-essay-examples\n\n**Key pattern for Veer:** Admitted essays at USC for the current \"Why USC\" prompt work when they are built around one specific intellectual through-line — a problem the applicant is obsessed with solving — and then show how three to five named USC resources feed that exact obsession. Generic praise of the Trojan network or the LA location is not the work.\n\n---\n\n## What separates strong essays\n\n**For the \"Why USC\" 250-word essay:**\n- Strong: names a specific course number or professor in a Marshall program (e.g., BUAD 307 Entrepreneurial Selling, taught by practitioners, not just \"entrepreneurship courses\"); references the Greif Center's Seed Competition with a specific detail (the $150K+ prize pool, the mentoring structure); names the Trojan network in terms of a specific alumni or sector (consumer tech, entertainment, media) that connects to the applicant's stated venture.\n- Strong: covers both first- and second-choice major and explains the relationship between them (e.g., \"My first choice is Entrepreneurship in Marshall because X; my second choice is Business Administration-General because it gives me flexibility if I want to double into IYA courses\").\n- Weak: says \"USC is the perfect place for me to grow.\" Uses words like \"passionate,\" \"world-class,\" \"diverse,\" \"vibrant campus.\"\n- Weak: focuses exclusively on IYA in the \"Why USC\" essay if IYA is only the second choice — readers route those to the wrong program.\n\n**For the Quick Takes:**\n- These 10 prompts at 100 characters each look like fun but reveal character at speed. Readers use them to see if the person they're imagining in the \"Why USC\" essay is the same person in the Quick Takes. Inconsistency between tone in the essay and the Quick Takes is a red flag.\n- Strong Quick Takes are specific and unexpected: \"dream job\" of \"General Counsel, SpaceX\" is more memorable than \"entrepreneur.\" A \"theme song\" of \"Empire State of Mind (Jay-Z)\" is less revealing than something that connects to a real moment in the applicant's life.\n- The \"describe yourself in three words\" (25 characters each) should avoid any word that every applicant uses: \"driven,\" \"passionate,\" \"curious.\" They should be specific enough that only a handful of people in the applicant pool would choose that combination.\n- The \"class you'd teach\" prompt is the one most relevant to Veer: it should name a real, specific course that reflects his entrepreneurship lens (e.g., \"Pitch Mechanics: How to Raise a Pre-Seed Round\" not \"How to Start a Business\").\n\n**For IYA essays:**\n- Essay 2 is a trap if the applicant just lists concentration areas without explaining the connection between them. The best responses pick two areas max, explain a specific project or interest that spans them, and show how the combination is the point — not the individual parts.\n- The optional portfolio: one strong business venture document beats four mediocre submissions. Quality and curation are stated criteria.\n\n---\n\n## Clichés and mistakes to avoid\n\n- **Naming USC's ranking or prestige** in the \"Why USC\" essay. Readers know where they work.\n- **The \"Trojan Family\" paragraph.** Everyone writes one. It says nothing specific.\n- **Getting the Greif Center's name wrong.** It is the Lloyd Greif Center for Entrepreneurial Studies (within Marshall). Do not call it \"the Marshall entrepreneurship program\" or \"the USC Entrepreneurship Center.\"\n- **IYA: submitting a portfolio link** that points to an external website or Google Drive. It will not be reviewed.\n- **IYA: the \"selfie video\" intro.** The IYA admissions page explicitly calls this out. A student sitting in front of a camera talking is the weakest possible submission.\n- **Quick Takes: using any word the reader has already seen 800 times.** \"Passionate,\" \"driven,\" \"innovative.\"\n- **Applying ED without financial clarity.** USC's sticker price is ~$95,000/year. Merit scholarships were scaled back in 2025. Applying ED means committing before seeing a financial aid offer. USC does not allow ED students to break the commitment for financial reasons unless the aid package is demonstrably inadequate — verify the policy.\n- **Treating spring admission as a failure outcome.** Applicants who plan their fall semester productively (community college, study abroad, DC program) often arrive at USC ahead of their fall-starting peers in credits.\n\n---\n\n## Specifics an applicant can name\n\n**Lloyd Greif Center — confirmed programs and competitions:**\n- BUAD 307 (Entrepreneurial Selling) — practitioner-taught\n- BUAD 499 (Opportunity Recognition and New Venture Creation) — capstone course\n- New Venture Seed Competition — annual, $150K+ prize pool, open to all USC students\n- Greif Practitioner Mentor Program — connects students to LA-based founders\n- The entrepreneurship minor is open to all USC undergrads across all schools\n\n**USC Incubator / Blackstone LaunchPad:**\n- 285+ portfolio companies\n- Seed capital and incubation\n- Alumni ventures have raised $300M+\n\n**IYA concentration areas (exact names matter in Essay 2):**\nExtended Reality | Transformative Artificial Intelligence | Business of Innovation | Interactive Technologies | Product Innovation | Design Strategy | Venture Development\n\n**IYA signature programs:**\n- Impact and Industry Labs (real-world challenges with for-profit and non-profit companies)\n- IYA Venture Showcase (annual pitch event to industry panelists — ran its first edition in 2023)\n- Career Edge (industry placement program; alumni startups selected for Y Combinator)\n\n**Other programs an applicant can name:**\n- USC Viterbi and Marshall cross-school startup courses (for tech-business founders)\n- The Trojan investor network (alumni angels and VCs who back USC founders — cite specific sectors, e.g., entertainment tech, consumer hardware)\n- Blackstone LaunchPad at USC (startups, micro-grants, incubation)\n\n**What to avoid naming wrongly:**\n- Do not call the Coliseum \"the stadium\" without its name. USC's football stadium is the Los Angeles Memorial Coliseum.\n- Do not conflate the USC Incubator with the Blackstone LaunchPad — they are connected but distinct entities.\n- \"Iovine and Young Academy\" not \"the Iovine Young Academy\" or \"the IYA program at Marshall.\" IYA is its own school, not inside Marshall.\n\n---\n\n## Coaching questions\n\nThese are for the \"Why USC\" essay and for IYA supplements specifically.\n\n1. You want to build ventures. Name one specific course in the Greif Center's curriculum that you have actually looked up — its title, who teaches it, and what problem you would bring to that class that you cannot bring to a class at any other school.\n\n2. The \"Why USC\" essay is 250 words about why USC fits you academically. You have two major choices. Walk me through what you wrote for each and how they connect to each other — not just \"business plus X\" but why the combination is specifically your combination.\n\n3. The Quick Takes ask for your life's theme song. What did you write — and why would a reader who knows nothing else about you read that answer and think \"that is the person who built [X]\"?\n\n4. IYA Essay 2 asks you to pick concentration areas. You have seven options. Which two did you choose? Now tell me what specific project or idea you are working on that genuinely spans both — not conceptually, but in actual work you have done or want to do.\n\n5. The IYA intro video is 60 seconds. You cannot just talk to a camera — they say so explicitly. What would you show? Walk me through what the viewer sees in the first five seconds.\n\n6. Your optional IYA portfolio can include business ventures. What would you put in — and what exactly would the description say about your specific role (not the team's role) in building it?\n\n7. Spring admission is a real outcome. If USC offers you spring instead of fall, what would you do with the fall semester that would make you a stronger founder when you arrive in January? Give me a specific plan, not a generic one.\n\n8. USC markets itself heavily on the Trojan network. Name one specific person in the Trojan alumni network — not just \"an entrepreneur\" — whose career you have actually looked up and whose path connects to something you want to build. If you cannot name one, what does that tell you about your \"Why USC\" essay?\n\n9. The New Venture Seed Competition has a $150K+ prize pool. If you were a junior at USC and entered that competition, what would your venture be and what would you be pitching? This is not a hypothetical — it tells me whether you are applying to USC for a credential or for the room.\n\n10. The \"class you'd teach\" Quick Take: what is yours, and would a reader who read it then read your \"Why USC\" essay and think they are the same person? Read both out loud to me.\n\n11. You are applying as a high school senior who lives in Illinois. What is one thing you know about Los Angeles — not from a brochure but from actual research — that changes how you think about building a company there versus in Chicago or New York?\n\n12. If USC offers you ED and you commit, you cannot see aid packages from other schools before deciding. What is your family's financial situation and what is the number that would make USC unaffordable? Have you run the Net Price Calculator?\n\n---\n\n## Sources\n\n- USC Provost announcement — ED expansion for Fall 2027: https://www.provost.usc.edu/early-decision-admissions-fall-2027-applicants/ (Feb 9 2026)\n- USC Viterbi — ED/EA/RD breakdown for Fall 2027: https://viterbiadmission.usc.edu/2026/fall-2027-early-decision-early-action-or-regular-decision/ (Feb 12 2026)\n- USC Viterbi — Which plan is right for you?: https://viterbiadmission.usc.edu/2026/early-decision-early-action-or-regular-decision-which-application-plan-is-right-for-you/ (Jul 27 2026)\n- USC Admission Blog — EA/ED Marshall FAQ for Fall 2026 (describes pilot year): https://www.admissionblog.usc.edu/p/early-action-and-early-decision-usc (Oct 13 2025)\n- Collegewise — USC Acceptance Rate 2026: https://collegewise.com/blog/usc-acceptance-rate (Jun 30 2026)\n- IYA Undergraduate Admissions (official, current): https://iovine-young.usc.edu/apply/undergraduate/undergraduate-admissions-acad\n- USC Admission Blog — Spring Admission at USC: https://www.admissionblog.usc.edu/p/spring-admission-at-usc (Dec 12 2025)\n- USC Admission Blog — Fall Options for Spring Admits: https://www.admissionblog.usc.edu/p/fall-options-for-spring-admits (Mar 12 2026)\n- USC Admission Blog — Student Perspectives: Being a Spring Admit: https://www.admissionblog.usc.edu/p/student-perspectives-being-a-spring (Jan 19 2026)\n- College Essay Guy — USC Supplemental Essays 2026/2027: https://www.collegeessayguy.com/blog/university-southern-california-usc-supplemental-essays (updated Oct 8 2026)\n- CollegeVine — 3 USC Essay Examples: https://blog.collegevine.com/usc-essay-examples (Aug 2023)\n- USC Admission Blog — Major Mondays: IYA (older, 2023 context; some details superseded): https://www.admissionblog.usc.edu/p/major-mondays-iovine-and-young-academy (Aug 28 2023)\n- Ref file (verified prompts): /agent/workspace/advisor/ref/usc.md\n\n**What could not be verified:**\n- ED admit rate for Fall 2027 (first year of program; USC will report publicly starting with Fall 2028 data).\n- Exact admit rate for IYA (not publicly reported).\n- Whether the old \"IYA is first-choice only\" rule from the 2023 blog still applies; the current official page contradicts it. Treat current official page as authoritative.\n- Merit scholarship dollar amounts for Fall 2027 (Trustee/Presidential scholarships scaled back in 2025; verify current availability).\n- Current required test score policy: USC is test-optional through 2026-27; Fall 2027 policy not yet confirmed — check USC's admissions website in early 2027.\n","utaustin":"## Part 1 — Verified application facts (from the Founder's Compass dashboard)\n\n# University of Texas at Austin (id: utaustin)\n\nPlatform: ApplyTexas / Common App | Test: Test required | Selectivity: {\"rate\":\"≈27% overall\",\"tier\":\"Selective overall · far tougher for OOS, McCombs & CS\",\"note\":\"Texas residents in roughly the top 6% auto-admit to the university (top 5% for the Fall 2026 class) — but not to a specific major. Out-of-state competes for ~10% of seats; McCombs ≈9%.\",\"cycle\":\"prior\"}\n\nDeadlines: [{\"plan\":\"Early Action / priority\",\"date\":\"Oct 15\",\"note\":\"Honors and scholarship consideration align with this date\",\"cycle\":\"prior\"},{\"plan\":\"Regular Decision\",\"date\":\"Dec 1\",\"note\":\"Decisions by Feb 15\",\"cycle\":\"prior\"}]\n\nApp note: UT uses ApplyTexas / Common App: a required essay (Topic A / the personal statement) plus three short answers. Prompts are confirmed for the upcoming cycle on the UT site.\n\nEntrepreneurship headline: UT pairs the #2-ranked Harkey Institute — an 800-student, all-majors entrepreneurship minor — with Longhorn Startup and a Capital Factory pipeline: the best startup value in the country for a Texas resident.\n\nDegrees: Entrepreneurship Minor (Harkey, all majors) · McCombs BBA + Canfield Business Honors · Bridging Disciplines certificate in Innovation, Creativity & Entrepreneurship\n\n## Supplements (verified 2026-27 where cycle=ok)\nOne required essay plus three short answers (≈250–300 words each).\n\n- **Required essay (Topic A)** [Required, 500–650 words, cycle=ok]: Share an essay on any topic of your choice. It can be one you've already written, one that responds to a different prompt, or one of your own design. (The Common App personal statement satisfies this.)\n- **Short answer — your first-choice major** [Required, ≈250–300 words, cycle=ok]: Why are you interested in the major you indicated as your first-choice major?\n- **Short answer — proudest activity** [Required, ≈250–300 words, cycle=ok]: Think of all the activities — both in and outside of school — that you have been involved with during high school. Which one are you most proud of and why?\n- **Short answer — circumstances (optional)** [Optional, ≈250–300 words, cycle=ok]: Please share background on events or special circumstances that you feel may have impacted your high school academic performance.\n\n## Programs\n- Harkey Institute for Entrepreneurial Studies: McCombs hub running the campus-wide Entrepreneurship Minor — 800+ students from 150+ majors — plus a summer academy and seed fellowship.\n- Longhorn Startup: Joint McCombs/Cockrell program (Seminar, Lab, Demo Day) co-taught for 15 years by Capital Factory founder Joshua Baer, who passed away Jun 18, 2026; teams get coworking at Capital Factory. Program continuity should be confirmed directly with UT for Fall 2026.\n- The LaunchPad (Blackstone): All-majors hub with StartUP micro-grants ($15K/semester) and the SEAL summer accelerator (alumni have raised $100M+).\n- Discovery to Impact / UT Seed Fund: Commercialization arm with a $10M seed fund backing UT-IP startups (recently a $250K hydrogen-tech investment).\n\n## Funding/competitions\n- StartUP Innovation Grants: $15,000 awarded per semester in non-dilutive micro-grants ($250–$1,500) to any UT student.\n- SEAL Accelerator: Selective summer accelerator; alumni ventures have raised $100M+ with $400M+ in value.\n- Builder's Bullpen & Texas Venture Labs: Student-government pitch prize plus McCombs' investment competition feeding the Austin ecosystem.\n\n## Watch-outs\n- Out-of-state odds are brutal (~10% of seats, full sticker) — essentially a high reach for non-Texans.\n- Auto-admit gets you the university, not the major: McCombs (≈9%) and CS are far more competitive.\n- 43,000 undergrads means you must be self-directed to tap the resources.\n- Longhorn Startup co-founder and longtime instructor Joshua Baer died in June 2026 — worth asking admissions/Harkey directly whether the Seminar/Lab format and Capital Factory pipeline continue unchanged for Fall 2026.\n\n## Known successful-essay sources\n- UT major supplement — 7 admitted-student examples (Tex Admissions) https://www.texadmissions.com/blog/2025/4/29/ut-austin-major-supplement-tips-and-essay-examples — 2025 · 'why your major' essays (McCombs/CS/Eng)\n- UT 'proud activity' supplement — 11 examples (Tex Admissions) https://www.texadmissions.com/blog/2025/5/29/ut-austin-proud-supplement-tips-essay-examples — 2025 · full samples for the proud-activity prompt\n- How to write the UT Austin supplements — examples + guide (College Essay Guy) https://www.collegeessayguy.com/blog/university-texas-austin-supplemental-essay — 2025–26 · all current UT prompts with worked examples\n\n## Other fields\n- short: UT Austin\n- mono: U\n- city: Austin\n- state: TX\n- type: Public\n- setting: Urban · flagship\n- undergrad: ≈43,000\n- platform: ApplyTexas / Common App\n- ac: #BF5700\n- ac2: #333F48\n- fit: 92\n- verdict: Elite for builders\n- sub: {\"program\":10,\"experiential\":9,\"outcomes\":8,\"location\":9}\n- rateNum: 27\n- dlSort: 1015\n- testPolicy: Test required\n- sat: ≈1250–1510\n- act: ≈27–33\n- fee: $75\n- recs: Optional (required for some honors)\n- opens: August 1\n- bestFor: Texas residents above all: the #2-ranked undergraduate entrepreneurship program (Harkey / Princeton Review) at in-state value, inside one of America's hottest startup cities — with a Longhorn Startup pipeline that pairs CS/engineering builders with business co-founders.\n- strengths: [\"A top public flagship with elite CS, engineering (Cockrell) and business (McCombs).\",\"Austin is a top-5 U.S. tech hub — Capital Factory, Dell, Tesla, Apple and Oracle are minutes away.\",\"Michael Dell founded Dell in a UT dorm — founder mythology runs deep.\",\"A huge, powerful Texas Exes alumni network.\"]\n- entreRank: #2 undergraduate entrepreneurship — Princeton Review (also #6 U.S. News)\n- founders: Michael Dell (Dell), Apptronik and BeatBox among Longhorn founders; Austin's startup scene is substantially UT-seeded, keeping mentors and capital close.\n- optIntro: One required essay plus three short answers (≈250–300 words each).\n- cost: {\"mode\":\"public\",\"inState\":\"≈$32,000\",\"outState\":\"≈$66,000\",\"aid\":\"Texas Advance Commitment covers tuition for Texas residents with family income up to ~$100K (partial to ~$125K); tuition is frozen through 2026-27. Merit is largely honors-linked; limited aid for OOS.\"}\n- cardEnt: #2 Harkey minor · Longhorn Startup + Capital Factory · Austin.\n- src: Sourced from admissions.utexas.edu (essays confirmed Jun 2026; deadlines prior-cycle), McCombs/Harkey Institute (Princeton Review #2; 800 students), news.utexas.edu and onestop.utexas.edu. Auto-admit % and OOS rates should be verified each cycle.\n\n## Part 2 — Deep research: paths in, what readers want, admitted essays\n\n# UT Austin — Knowledge Base for Veer (Fall 2027 entry)\n\n## Paths in\n\n### Out-of-state odds — the core reality\n\nVeer is an Illinois resident. That means:\n\n- **No auto-admit benefit.** The automatic admission guarantee (top ~6% of Texas high school class for Fall 2027; the preceding cycle used top 5%) applies only to Texas residents. Veer competes entirely through holistic review.\n- **Roughly 10% of total undergraduate seats go to out-of-state and international students combined.** For Fall 2026 applicants, out-of-state applicants competed for a severely constrained pool. UT does not publish a separate OOS admit rate officially, but advisors and press reports consistently cite OOS admit rates below 10% — some sources put it in the 5-8% range for the most recent cycle. McCombs as a first-choice major compounds the difficulty.\n- **McCombs admit rate is ~9% overall** (all applicants). For OOS applicants specifically targeting McCombs, this is functionally a high reach. Texas residents who auto-admit to the university still must get holistic admit to McCombs (they are not guaranteed McCombs placement).\n- **No CAP for non-Texans.** The Coordinated Admission Program (CAP) is Texas-resident-only. Veer is not eligible.\n- **No merit aid scaled for OOS.** Texas Advance Commitment covers tuition only for Texas residents with AGI up to ~$100K. OOS sticker is ~$66,000/year with limited merit aid available.\n\nBottom line: UT Austin is a reach for Veer in all scenarios. McCombs as first-choice compounds that. He should apply because the program is exceptional and Austin is a founder's city — but not with realistic expectations.\n\nSource: admissions.utexas.edu/info-for/texas-residents and ref file (/agent/workspace/advisor/ref/utaustin.md).\n\n### Auto-admit explained (Texas context Veer should understand)\n\nTexas law requires UT Austin to automatically admit Texas residents who graduate in the top percentage of their Texas high school class. That threshold was top 5% for Fall 2026, adjusted to approximately top 6% for recent cycles. Auto-admit guarantees university admission — not major admission. An auto-admit student who wants McCombs, CS, or Cockrell Engineering still goes through holistic review for those specific programs. The top-10% rule is commonly confused with auto-admit; it is not the same thing. Auto-admit is a sub-range of that. Source: admissions.utexas.edu/info-for/texas-residents/ (updated Sep 29 2025).\n\n### Undergraduate programs and selectivity\n\n- **McCombs School of Business (BBA):** Applying directly to McCombs is the most selective path for a business-minded student. Stated overall admit rate ~9%. An entrepreneurship-track applicant should list McCombs as first-choice and select the BBA with a specific concentration if known.\n- **Canfield Business Honors Program (Canfield BHP / CBHP):** See dedicated section below. Top-tier honors track within McCombs. Requires specific first-choice major designation and expanded resume.\n- **Cockrell School of Engineering:** Highly selective, especially CS. Relevant if Veer's venture work has a strong technical angle.\n- **College of Liberal Arts + Bridging Disciplines:** Less selective than McCombs. Students can pursue the Bridging Disciplines certificate in Innovation, Creativity and Entrepreneurship from any college — this is an alternative route for students who don't get into McCombs directly.\n- **Harkey Institute for Entrepreneurial Studies:** The campus-wide entrepreneurship minor runs out of McCombs' Harkey Institute. 800+ students from 150+ majors participate. Getting into McCombs is not required to participate — any UT student can apply for the minor.\n\n### First-choice and second-choice major\n\nThe UT Austin application asks for a first-choice major and a second-choice major. This matters.\n\n- If you are not admitted to your first-choice major, UT may admit you to your second-choice major instead. This is especially important for competitive majors like McCombs and CS.\n- **If McCombs is your first choice:** Choose a second choice that is realistic and that you could genuinely pursue. Many applicants select something in Liberal Arts (Plan II, Economics, etc.). Natural Sciences or Communications are other options. Do not list another highly competitive major as your second choice.\n- **If Canfield BHP is your target:** You must list \"Unspecified Business\" as your first-choice major AND check the box to apply for honors. You cannot list BHP directly as a named major on the application. Listing McCombs with any other specific major (Finance, Marketing) will not route you to BHP review.\n- **OOS implication:** Even if Veer applies to McCombs as first choice and is not admitted there, being admitted to Liberal Arts as a second choice still gets him to UT Austin, where he can pursue the Harkey entrepreneurship minor and Longhorn Startup.\n\n### Coordinated Admission Program (CAP)\n\nCAP is **for Texas residents only.** Veer is ineligible. This section is included because it will come up in conversations about UT, and Veer should understand what it is and why it does not apply to him.\n\nCAP offers Texas residents who are not admitted to UT Austin regular admission a guaranteed transfer to UT Austin's College of Liberal Arts after completing their freshman year at a participating UT System school (UT Arlington, UT El Paso, UT Permian Basin, UT Rio Grande Valley, UT San Antonio, Stephen F. Austin, UT Tyler).\n\n**Requirements to activate the CAP guarantee:**\n1. Complete 30 semester credit hours from an approved UT System school course list (not AP, IB, or Maymester credit).\n2. Complete at least one math course beyond college algebra (Math 301).\n3. Earn a cumulative GPA of at least 3.2 in those 30 hours (no grade below C in any course).\n4. Submit final official transcript to UT Austin by June 1.\n\n**What CAP guarantees:** Admission to UT Austin's College of Liberal Arts. Specific Liberal Arts majors are not guaranteed. Students who want McCombs, Engineering, or other colleges outside Liberal Arts must submit additional credentials (essay, resume, official transcripts) by March 1 of their CAP year and compete in the transfer applicant pool. Architecture and Nursing are not available as CAP transfer targets.\n\n**Participation stats:** More than 1,000 Texas students participate in CAP per year. Approximately one-third complete the requirements and transfer to UT Austin. Accepting a CAP offer is not binding.\n\nSource: admissions.utexas.edu/apply/alternative-pathways-to-enrollment/cap/ and associated FAQ.\n\n### Canfield Business Honors Program (Canfield BHP)\n\nThe Canfield Business Honors Program is a four-year BBA program within McCombs, graduating about 650 students across all four years total — roughly 160 students per class. It is the most intensive and selective undergraduate business path at UT Austin.\n\n**Application requirements specific to BHP:**\n- Must list **\"Unspecified Business\"** as your first-choice major on Apply Texas or Common App. This exact designation is required — any other McCombs major designation will not route to BHP.\n- Must check the honors opt-in box on the application.\n- Must submit an **expanded resume** (see next section).\n- Must submit one **academic letter of recommendation**.\n- No separate application form beyond the standard UT/Common App — the honors designation and expanded resume are submitted as part of the same application.\n\n**Deadlines:** October 15 is the EA deadline for both BHP and UT Admissions. December 1 is the regular deadline. BHP strongly recommends applying by October 15.\n\n**Scholarships:** BHP has a limited number of merit-based scholarships. All BHP applicants are automatically considered — no separate scholarship application.\n\n**What BHP is:** Small discussion-based classes. Dedicated advisors. BBA in Business Honors degree. Most students double major, commonly within McCombs (Finance + BHP) or into Liberal Arts (Plan II is a popular combination), Natural Sciences, or Communications. BHP has formal partnerships with Texas CSB (Computer Science + Business) and Texas ECB (Electrical and Computer Engineering + Business). Strong pipeline into investment banking, consulting, and increasingly, venture capital.\n\n**For Veer:** BHP is the honors-track equivalent of Wharton's Jerome Fisher M&T or Northwestern's Kellogg integrated program for UT. It is small-cohort, discussion-based, and produces a tight alumni network. If Veer is committed to McCombs and has the profile, BHP is worth the expanded resume effort. OOS admission to BHP is even more competitive than regular McCombs admission.\n\nSource: mccombs.utexas.edu/undergraduate/canfield-business-honors-program/admissions/ (updated Jul 2024).\n\n### The expanded résumé (BHP requirement)\n\nThe expanded resume required by BHP is not a standard one-page activities list. It is an opportunity to describe activities in depth — leadership roles, context, and impact — beyond what the Common App activities section allows.\n\n**What the expanded resume should contain:**\n- A leadership activities section that emphasizes depth over breadth. BHP's admissions website asks: \"Have you challenged yourself by taking the most difficult classes available? Do you go above and beyond inside and outside the classroom?\" The resume should reflect that.\n- Specific roles, not generic titles. \"Vice President, DECA\" is weaker than \"Built DECA chapter from 4 to 31 members; placed second at state in marketing series.\"\n- Quantified outcomes wherever possible.\n- Entrepreneurship work described in terms of what was built, what was learned, what failed. BHP readers have seen pitch-deck résumés. They want to see what the applicant actually did, not what they launched.\n\n**Note:** The regular UT Austin application (for non-BHP applicants) does not require an expanded resume. The standard application uses the Common App activities section and UT's own short answers. The expanded resume is a BHP-specific requirement.\n\n---\n\n## What readers want\n\nUT Austin's admissions office has published guidance emphasizing holistic review. For McCombs specifically, the application is reviewed on academic achievement, rigor, essays, extracurriculars, and leadership.\n\nFrom the Canfield BHP admissions page (official): \"Are you a leader at your high school? Have you challenged yourself by taking the most difficult classes available and mastering them? Do you go above and beyond to succeed inside and outside of the classroom? Grades and test scores are important to the admissions process, as are leadership and extracurricular activities. Our admits go on to be successful students and top university leaders.\" Source: mccombs.utexas.edu/undergraduate/canfield-business-honors-program/admissions/\n\nFor the first-choice major essay (~250-300 words), the \"why your major\" prompt is asking for a specific intellectual rationale — not a career goal statement. The best answers name a specific problem the applicant is trying to solve, show how the major is the right framework for solving it, and connect to at least one specific UT program or course. The Tex Admissions blog (2025) describes the pattern across 7 admitted examples: each one had a specific intellectual hook, not a generic \"I love business\" framing. Source: texadmissions.com/blog/2025/4/29/ut-austin-major-supplement-tips-and-essay-examples\n\nFor the proudest activity essay (~250-300 words), the best essays go narrow and specific — they pick one activity and mine it for a genuine lesson, a specific moment of failure or growth, and why that activity shaped how the applicant thinks. Generic leadership stories (\"I was elected president and made us better\") are weak. Source: texadmissions.com/blog/2025/5/29/ut-austin-proud-supplement-tips-essay-examples\n\n---\n\n## Admitted essays: what they did\n\n**Note:** Verified admitted essay examples with full text for UT Austin are available via Tex Admissions and College Essay Guy (both cited in ref file). No verbatim quotes reproduced here per ethics guidelines, but structural patterns from those sources are:\n\n**First-choice major essay — McCombs pattern (from Tex Admissions 2025 analysis):**\nStrong essays: Name a specific problem in a specific industry they want to address using business tools. One admitted applicant opened with a description of watching a family restaurant fail and named the specific operational failure (cash flow, not the food) — then explained why she wanted to study finance at McCombs specifically to understand how small business capital works. Named: McCombs' Entrepreneurship minor, the Harkey Institute Summer Academy, and a specific professor-led practicum. Source: https://www.texadmissions.com/blog/2025/4/29/ut-austin-major-supplement-tips-and-essay-examples\n\nAnother admitted McCombs essay (from the same source): An applicant interested in healthcare entrepreneurship opened with a healthcare pricing problem she had personally experienced, named the interdisciplinary path she was building (BBA + pre-health Bridging Disciplines certificate), and connected to two specific McCombs resources. The common thread: the major is the answer to a real question, not the resume line.\n\n**Proudest activity essay — admitted patterns (from Tex Admissions 2025):**\nStrong essays: Zoom in on one specific moment within the activity rather than summarizing the entire arc. One admitted essay described the exact conversation that nearly killed a robotics team's state run — the disagreement, the choice made, and what the applicant learned about how they make decisions under pressure. Another described a specific failure in a startup — not \"I founded a company and learned a lot\" but the moment when a co-founder quit and what the applicant did the next day. Source: https://www.texadmissions.com/blog/2025/5/29/ut-austin-proud-supplement-tips-essay-examples\n\n**Topic A (personal statement, 500-650 words):**\nUT accepts the Common App personal statement for this prompt. Strong Topic A essays work the same way as strong Common App essays: one specific scene or experience, specific sensory details, a turn that reveals something non-obvious about the applicant, no moral spelled out in the last paragraph. Not found: admitted entrepreneurship-specific Topic A examples with full text for UT Austin in public sources for the current cycle.\n\n---\n\n## What separates strong essays\n\n**First-choice major essay:**\n- Strong: opens with a specific intellectual problem, not a career aspiration (\"I want to work in finance\"). Names a real UT course or program in the second half. Explains why UT Austin specifically — not just why business school — addresses that problem.\n- Strong: for McCombs applicants, the answer to \"why this major at this school\" should connect to Austin's startup ecosystem, the Harkey Institute, Longhorn Startup's Capital Factory pipeline, or a specific professor's research.\n- Weak: \"I have always loved business and want to help companies grow.\" Weak: any essay that could be sent to five other schools with name-swaps.\n\n**Proudest activity essay:**\n- Strong: picks one moment within one activity, not a summary of four years. Shows the applicant's decision-making, not just the outcome. Admits to a failure or doubt.\n- Weak: chronological summary of all activities in one essay. Weak: \"I was elected captain and improved team morale.\" Weak: an essay that talks about what the team did, not what the applicant specifically did.\n\n**Topic A (personal statement):**\n- Strong: reads like a person, not a resume. Uses concrete sensory details. Has a surprise.\n- Weak: opens with a dictionary definition. Opens with \"I have always been passionate about...\" Ends with \"This is why I want to attend UT Austin.\"\n\n**BHP-specific (expanded resume):**\n- Weak: lists positions without outcomes. Strong: every line has a number or a result.\n- Weak: fifteen activities listed at equal depth. Strong: three to five activities in depth, showing commitment over time.\n\n---\n\n## Clichés and mistakes to avoid\n\n- **Applying to McCombs and not mentioning Harkey, Longhorn Startup, or Capital Factory.** These are the most specific and differentiating programs. Omitting them and writing about \"Austin's business ecosystem\" instead is vague.\n- **Listing McCombs as first choice without knowing how BHP works.** If you want the honors track, you must list \"Unspecified Business\" — not \"Finance\" or \"Marketing.\" Getting this wrong routes your application away from BHP review.\n- **Treating the first-choice major essay as a personal statement.** It is not. It is 250-300 words about why that major. It should be specific and academic, not personal and narrative.\n- **The CAP misconception.** Some students and parents think CAP is a fallback path for OOS applicants. It is not. CAP is Texas-resident-only. Veer should not plan for it.\n- **Describing Longhorn Startup without noting Joshua Baer's death.** The co-founder and longtime instructor of Longhorn Startup's Seminar and Lab passed away June 18, 2026. The ref file flags this. Any reference to Longhorn Startup should acknowledge program continuity is unconfirmed and encourage Veer to ask UT directly about the current Seminar/Lab format.\n- **The \"I founded a startup\" résumé dump.** BHP readers and McCombs holistic reviewers see hundreds of students who launched ventures. The differentiator is what was learned, what failed, what was actually built — not the fact of founding.\n- **Ignoring the OOS math.** UT's out-of-state cost is ~$66,000/year with limited merit aid for non-Texans. The Texas Advance Commitment is Texas-resident-only. Veer should run the net price calculator and build a realistic cost model before making UT a strategic priority.\n\n---\n\n## Specifics an applicant can name\n\n**Harkey Institute for Entrepreneurial Studies:**\n- The Entrepreneurship Minor: 800+ students, 150+ majors. Open to all UT students regardless of college.\n- Harkey Institute Summer Academy: summer entrepreneurship program for prospective UT students.\n- Harkey Seed Fellowship: funding for student ventures.\n\n**Longhorn Startup (verify continuity for Fall 2026/2027):**\n- Longhorn Startup Seminar: introductory course on building companies\n- Longhorn Startup Lab: application-required, team-based venture building course\n- Longhorn Startup Demo Day: public pitch event\n- Capital Factory partnership: student teams get access to Capital Factory co-working space in Austin\n- Note: Joshua Baer, co-founder and longtime instructor, died June 18, 2026. Verify current program structure directly with UT.\n\n**The LaunchPad (Blackstone LaunchPad at UT Austin):**\n- StartUP Innovation Grants: $15,000 awarded per semester in non-dilutive micro-grants ($250-$1,500) to any UT student\n- SEAL Accelerator: selective summer accelerator; alumni ventures have raised $100M+\n\n**Texas Venture Labs:** McCombs' investment competition and ecosystem connector feeding Austin's startup scene.\n\n**Builder's Bullpen:** Student-government pitch competition inside UT.\n\n**Canfield BHP-specific opportunities:**\n- Texas CSB (Computer Science + Business): dual degree through Cockrell and McCombs\n- Texas ECB (Electrical and Computer Engineering + Business): dual degree\n- Plan II Honors: liberal arts honors track with a formal BHP partnership\n\n**Specific Austin ecosystem connections to name:**\n- Capital Factory: Austin's main startup accelerator, physically connected to Longhorn Startup\n- Michael Dell: Dell Computer founded in a UT dorm room — the most cited founder-mythology connection\n- Apptronik (robotics) and BeatBox (beverages): recent Longhorn-founded companies\n- Dell, Tesla, Apple, Oracle: all have major Austin campuses — internship and hiring relevance\n\n**What to avoid naming wrongly:**\n- Do not call the Harkey Institute \"the McCombs entrepreneurship department.\"\n- Do not refer to Longhorn Startup as just \"a club\" — it is a formal for-credit program.\n- Do not claim Capital Factory is a UT-run program. It is an independent accelerator with a UT partnership.\n- Do not name Joshua Baer as a current instructor — he passed away June 2026.\n\n---\n\n## Coaching questions\n\n1. You are an out-of-state applicant applying to one of the most Texas-tilted public flagship universities in the country. Why should a reader pick you over a Texas resident with comparable numbers? What do you offer that they do not? Be specific — not \"I bring an outside perspective.\"\n\n2. The first-choice major essay is 250-300 words on why you want to study business at UT Austin specifically. What is the one problem you want to solve — not the career you want, but the problem — and how does McCombs' curriculum or Austin's ecosystem give you the tools a Chicago school or New York school cannot?\n\n3. McCombs overall admit rate is ~9%. OOS applicants face even tighter odds. If you are not admitted to McCombs but are admitted to Liberal Arts — would you go to UT Austin for the Harkey minor and Longhorn Startup without the BBA? Think about that answer seriously before you write your application, because it shapes how you write every essay.\n\n4. Canfield BHP requires listing \"Unspecified Business\" as your first-choice major and an expanded resume. Walk me through what would be on your expanded resume that you cannot fit in the Common App activities section. What is the most specific, quantified result you have produced in any entrepreneurial work you have done?\n\n5. The proudest activity essay asks for one activity. You are building ventures. Which one moment within that work are you most proud of — not the launch, not the pitch, not the press hit, but one specific decision you made that you still think about?\n\n6. If Longhorn Startup is part of your \"why UT\" case, what have you learned about how it runs today, from current students or the Harkey Institute, beyond the website?\n\n7. Austin's startup ecosystem is its biggest selling point. Name one company in Austin's ecosystem — not Dell or Tesla, something smaller — where you have actually researched what they do, who runs it, and why it matters to you. If you cannot, what does that tell you about your \"why UT Austin\" answer?\n\n8. The \"why your major\" essay for McCombs — did you address UT specifically or did you write a generic \"why business school\" essay? Read it out loud and count how many lines could apply to any business school. If the answer is more than three, start over.\n\n9. BHP is described as producing \"top university leaders.\" What does that mean to you specifically? Name one BHP alumnus or current student whose path connects to what you are trying to build. If you cannot, look it up before you write your application.\n\n10. UT requires tests. Your middle-50% SAT target is 1250-1510. Where are your scores? For OOS applicants at the lower end of that range, the score matters more because there are fewer seats and every signal counts.\n\n11. The optional circumstances short answer exists. Is there anything in your academic record — a semester where grades dropped, a school switch, a family situation — that a reader would see without context? If so, this is not optional for you.\n\n12. You are applying to a school with 43,000 undergrads. At UT you must be self-directed to find and use the entrepreneurship resources. What is your plan for doing that in the first semester? Name two specific actions — not \"join clubs\" — you would take in your first 60 days.\n\n---\n\n## Sources\n\n- UT Austin Admissions — Texas Residents (auto-admit, CAP overview): https://admissions.utexas.edu/info-for/texas-residents/ (updated Sep 29 2025)\n- UT Austin Admissions — CAP main page: https://admissions.utexas.edu/apply/alternative-pathways-to-enrollment/cap/\n- UT Austin Admissions — CAP FAQ: https://admissions.utexas.edu/apply/alternative-pathways-to-enrollment/cap/cap-faq/\n- UT System Board of Regents — CAP Rule 40305: https://utsystem.edu/board-of-regents/rules/40305-coordinated-admission-program\n- UT Austin Admissions — CAP Transitioning page: https://admissions.utexas.edu/apply/alternative-pathways-to-enrollment/cap/transitioning-to-ut-austin/\n- McCombs — Canfield BHP Admissions: https://www.mccombs.utexas.edu/undergraduate/canfield-business-honors-program/admissions/ (updated Jul 2024)\n- Tex Admissions — UT Major Supplement tips + 7 examples (2025): https://www.texadmissions.com/blog/2025/4/29/ut-austin-major-supplement-tips-and-essay-examples\n- Tex Admissions — UT Proud Activity supplement + 11 examples (2025): https://www.texadmissions.com/blog/2025/5/29/ut-austin-proud-supplement-tips-essay-examples\n- College Essay Guy — UT Austin Supplemental Essay guide (2025-26): https://www.collegeessayguy.com/blog/university-texas-austin-supplemental-essay\n- Ref file (verified prompts, program details): /agent/workspace/advisor/ref/utaustin.md\n\n**What could not be verified:**\n- Exact OOS admit rate for UT Austin or McCombs — UT does not publish this figure officially. The ~10% OOS seat allocation is widely cited but should be confirmed with current cycle data.\n- Auto-admit threshold for Fall 2027 (the ref file says \"top 6% approximately\" based on prior cycle; confirm with admissions office for the current cycle).\n- Longhorn Startup program format, Seminar/Lab curriculum, and Capital Factory pipeline status for Fall 2026 and beyond — Joshua Baer's death in June 2026 creates uncertainty. Veer should contact the Harkey Institute directly.\n- BHP admit rates — not publicly reported.\n- McCombs OOS vs in-state split in admit rates — not publicly reported.\n- Whether UT Austin's December 1 deadline listed in the ref file has been updated for Fall 2027 (the ref file marks deadlines as \"prior\" cycle — verify at admissions.utexas.edu).\n","uiuc":"## Part 1 — Verified application facts (from the Founder's Compass dashboard)\n\n# University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign (id: uiuc)\n\nPlatform: Common App | Test: Test-optional | Selectivity: {\"rate\":\"≈37% overall · CS ≈7%\",\"tier\":\"Selective overall · elite-selective for CS/Engineering/Gies\",\"note\":\"You apply by major. Computer Science ≈7%, Engineering ≈21%, Gies Business ≈21% — far tougher than the overall rate. About 71% of students are Illinois residents.\",\"cycle\":\"prior\"}\n\nDeadlines: [{\"plan\":\"Early Action\",\"date\":\"Nov 1\",\"note\":\"Strongly recommended for CS / Engineering / Gies and merit\",\"cycle\":\"ok\"},{\"plan\":\"Regular Decision\",\"date\":\"Jan 5\",\"note\":\"Decisions by early March\",\"cycle\":\"ok\"}]\n\nApp note: UIUC uses the Common App: the personal statement plus short major-specific supplemental essays (≈150 words each). You apply directly to a college and a first-choice major.\n\nEntrepreneurship headline: Illinois is a technical-founder factory: the Cozad New Venture Challenge (a record $700K+ in 2026), the iVenture Accelerator (up to $20K non-dilutive) and a 'PayPal Mafia' alumni network turn engineering talent into companies.\n\nDegrees: Gies BS in Strategy, Innovation & Entrepreneurship · campus-wide Entrepreneurship Minor · ILEE dual degree (engineering + entrepreneurship) in Grainger\n\n## Supplements (verified 2026-27 where cycle=ok)\nMost applicants answer two major-focused prompts (≈150 words each); a second-choice essay is recommended for competitive majors.\n\n- **Experience related to your first-choice major** [Required, ≈150 words, cycle=ok]: Explain, in detail, an experience you've had in the past 3 to 4 years related to your first-choice major. This can be an experience from an extracurricular activity, in a class you've taken, or through something else.\n- **Goals and how your major helps** [Required, ≈150 words, cycle=ok]: Describe your personal and/or career goals after graduating from Illinois and how your selected first-choice major will help you achieve them.\n- **Second-choice major (recommended)** [Optional, ≈150 words, cycle=ok]: Please explain your interest in your second-choice major or your overall academic or career goals.\n\n## Programs\n- Landuyt Center for Entrepreneurship (Grainger): Engineering's entrepreneurship hub — renamed in 2026 from the Technology Entrepreneur Center after $20M+ in cumulative gifts from alumni Richard and Gayle Landuyt, which also fund a new Chicago outpost at the Discovery Partners Institute. Runs Cozad and a tech-venture course suite.\n- iVenture Accelerator: Campus-wide accelerator (via Gies) offering up to $20,000 non-dilutive funding, a summer intensive and mentorship to top student startups.\n- Gies — Strategy, Innovation & Entrepreneurship: A dedicated undergraduate entrepreneurship major plus a campus-wide Entrepreneurship Minor (16 credits, 70+ electives).\n- Research Park & EnterpriseWorks: On-campus incubator and a 200+ company research park where students intern and spin out ventures.\n\n## Funding/competitions\n- Cozad New Venture Challenge: Illinois' largest venture competition — a record $700,000+ with a first-ever $100,000 grand prize in 2026 (350+ teams).\n- iVenture Accelerator: Up to $20,000 non-dilutive per startup; alumni have gone to Y Combinator and Techstars and raised $160M+.\n- Orange & Blue Ventures: A new (2026) student-run venture fund letting Illinois students invest real capital in startups.\n\n## Watch-outs\n- Admission is by major: CS (≈7%) and Engineering/Gies (≈21%) are far harder than the headline rate — apply with a realistic major strategy.\n- College-town setting; the nearest big city (Chicago) is ~2.5 hours away.\n- Large and self-directed — the ecosystem is rich but you must seek it out.\n\n## Known successful-essay sources\n- Major-specific short-answer examples (official) (UIUC Admissions blog) https://blog.admissions.illinois.edu/apply-like-a-pro-examples-of-short-answer-college-application-questions/ — official · sample 150-word major essays from UIUC\n- How to write the UIUC supplements — examples + guide (College Essay Guy) https://www.collegeessayguy.com/blog/university-illinois-urbana-champaign-supplemental-essays — 2025–26 · full major-specific example, annotated\n- UIUC essay example + breakdown (CollegeVine) https://blog.collegevine.com/uiuc-essay-example — real admitted essay, paragraph-by-paragraph analysis\n\n## Other fields\n- short: UIUC\n- mono: I\n- city: Urbana-Champaign\n- state: IL\n- type: Public\n- setting: College town · central Illinois\n- undergrad: ≈36,000\n- platform: Common App\n- ac: #FF5F05\n- ac2: #13294B\n- fit: 85\n- verdict: Excellent fit\n- sub: {\"program\":8,\"experiential\":8,\"outcomes\":9,\"location\":5}\n- rateNum: 37\n- dlSort: 1101\n- testPolicy: Test-optional\n- sat: ≈1390–1520\n- act: ≈30–34\n- fee: $75\n- recs: Not required\n- opens: ≈September 1\n- bestFor: Technical founders — especially CS and engineering — who want a world-class engineering school, one of the densest tech-founder alumni networks anywhere (the 'PayPal Mafia' has roots here), and real on-campus funding through iVenture and Cozad.\n- strengths: [\"Top-5 U.S. engineering and computer science.\",\"An extraordinary tech-founder alumni base — YouTube, PayPal, Yelp, AMD and Mosaic/Netscape roots.\",\"Research Park + EnterpriseWorks incubator put 200+ companies on campus.\",\"Exceptional value, especially for Illinois residents.\"]\n- entreRank: Powerhouse tech-founder pipeline; entrepreneurship via Grainger's Landuyt Center + Gies\n- founders: Marc Andreessen (Netscape/a16z), Max Levchin and the PayPal Mafia, Jawed Karim & Steve Chen (YouTube), Jeremy Stoppelman (Yelp) — one of the most concentrated tech-founder alumni clusters of any public university.\n- optIntro: Most applicants answer two major-focused prompts (≈150 words each); a second-choice essay is recommended for competitive majors.\n- cost: {\"mode\":\"public\",\"inState\":\"≈$33,000\",\"outState\":\"≈$60,000–$69,000\",\"aid\":\"Illinois Commitment covers full tuition and fees for in-state families earning ≤$75K. Engineering and Gies carry differential tuition; out-of-state engineering cost rivals private universities.\"}\n- cardEnt: Cozad ($700K+) · iVenture ($20K) · PayPal-Mafia alumni.\n- src: Sourced from admissions.illinois.edu (major-specific admit rates 2025; EA Nov 1 / RD Jan 5 deadlines confirmed for 2026-27 as of Sept 12, 2026), landuyt.illinois.edu (Cozad 2026; confirms the former Technology Entrepreneur Center is now the Landuyt Center for Entrepreneurship after the Landuyts' $20M+ gift), iventure.illinois.edu and giesbusiness.illinois.edu.\n\n## Part 2 — Deep research: paths in, what readers want, admitted essays\n\n# University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign (uiuc)\n\nVeer is an Illinois resident. UIUC is in-state for him. That changes the financial math, the major strategy, and the urgency of EA significantly.\n\n---\n\n## Paths in\n\n### Admission plans\n\nUIUC offers Early Action (EA, Nov 1) and Regular Decision (RD, Jan 5). There is no Early Decision and no restrictive EA — applying EA is non-binding. Decisions for EA applicants come by January 31. RD decisions come by early March.\n\nUIUC does not publish separate EA vs RD admit rates by major. It does not report an ED lane in its Common Data Set. However, UIUC strongly recommends EA for CS, Engineering, and Gies, and for students seeking merit consideration. College Essay Guy's 2026-27 guide states that the major-specific essays for competitive programs \"are especially high-stakes\" and that applying EA matters for those programs.\nSource: https://www.collegeessayguy.com/blog/university-illinois-urbana-champaign-supplemental-essays\n\nThe 2024-25 Common Data Set (most recent filed) shows 73,742 applicants, 31,247 admitted, 9,008 enrolled — a 42% overall admit rate. (The ref file says ~37%; the CDS shows 42.4%. The ref figure is a prior-cycle estimate.) By college: CS ~7%, Engineering ~21%, Gies ~21% — all far harder than the headline rate.\nSource: https://www.collegedata.fyi/schools/university-of-illinois-urbana-champaign/2024-25\n\nThe CDS confirms UIUC marks \"alumni relation\" as Not Considered. Legacy carries zero weight.\nSource: https://www.collegedata.fyi/schools/university-of-illinois-urbana-champaign/2024-25\n\n### Which college to apply to\n\n**Gies College of Business (~21% admit rate)**\nAll first-year Gies applicants apply as \"Business Undeclared.\" Gies does not separate students by intended major at admission. Once admitted, students take core courses and declare a specific major by end of sophomore year. The Strategy, Innovation & Entrepreneurship (SIE) major is one of 11 Gies majors and is chosen after enrollment. SIE graduates average $70,253 starting salary; 100% employed or in grad school; in-state total cost ~$43,480/year.\n\nGies reviews holistically: coursework rigor, grades, essays, activities. No GPA/score cutoff. \"We highly recommend applying during the Early Action timeline,\" per the Gies admissions FAQ.\nSource: https://giesbusiness.illinois.edu/apply/admissions\n\n**Grainger College of Engineering (~21% admit rate)**\nThe home of CS (sub-7%) and all engineering majors. The Landuyt Center for Entrepreneurship (formerly the Technology Entrepreneur Center, renamed 2026 after a $20M+ gift) sits inside Grainger. The ILEE dual-degree program pairs engineering with entrepreneurship. An engineering applicant applying to, say, Computer Engineering alongside a Gies second-choice creates a very different risk profile than applying to CS.\n\n**College of Liberal Arts & Sciences (LAS)**\nMuch more accessible than Gies or Grainger. Economics is housed in LAS and is a reasonable backup major for an entrepreneurship-minded applicant. A student admitted to Economics in LAS can pursue the campus-wide Entrepreneurship Minor (16 credits, 70+ electives) and access all iVenture and Cozad programming. LAS does not have differential tuition, so in-state cost is lower.\n\n### Second-choice major mechanics — critical detail\n\nUIUC lets applicants list a second-choice major and requires a separate 150-word essay to explain it. The strategy matters:\n\n- If admitted to your first-choice (e.g., Gies), the second-choice is irrelevant.\n- If admitted only to the second-choice **outside Gies**, you can apply to transfer into Gies at the end of year 1 via Intercollegiate Transfer (ICT). ICT to Gies is competitive.\n- If admitted to the Division of General Studies (undeclared), you **cannot** transfer to Gies later. Per Gies: \"Students who are admitted into their second choice major, including students admitted to the Undeclared program in the Division of General Studies... will not be able to transfer into Gies Business at a later time.\"\nSource: https://giesbusiness.illinois.edu/apply/admissions\n\n**Strategy for Veer:** Apply to Gies (Business Undeclared) as first choice. List a specific major in LAS — Economics is the best bet — as second choice. Write the second-choice essay as a genuine backup, not a throwaway. Do not list DGS/undeclared as second choice.\n\n### Internal transfer (ICT to Gies)\n\nICT to Gies requires a minimum 3.0 GPA; the average admitted transfer GPA was 3.8 in Fall 2023. All required business core courses must be completed before applying. ICT is fall-only. It is competitive and the transfer handbook notes \"the probability of admission for those who are missing required coursework is extremely low.\"\nSource: https://www.admissions.illinois.edu/content/docs/handbook_bus.pdf\n\n### Out-of-state realities (not relevant for Veer, but important context)\n\nVeer is an Illinois resident. Base in-state tuition: ~$12,992 (CDS 2024-25). Differential tuition applies to Gies and Engineering, raising the in-state total to ~$43,480/year for Gies. Out-of-state total for Gies: ~$69,420/year — comparable to a private university.\n\n### Illinois Commitment (in-state students only)\n\nFor students entering fall 2025 or later, Illinois Commitment covers full tuition and campus fees for Illinois residents whose family income is ≤$75,000 AND family assets are ≤$75,000. The income threshold was raised from $67,100 in October 2024. No separate application — just file FAFSA by March 15. Covers tuition and fees only; not room/board, course fees, or study abroad.\nSource: https://www.osfa.illinois.edu/types-of-aid/other-aid/illinois-commitment/\n\n**Veer's reality:** Illinois Commitment applies only if family income and assets are both at or below the thresholds. The advisor does not know the family's finances, so treat this as something for the family to check. Even without it, UIUC is a strong in-state financial value.\n\n### Demonstrated interest, interviews, test policy\n\nUIUC marks \"level of applicant interest\" as Not Considered (CDS). No interviews. Test-optional. 41% of the 2024 class filed test-optional. Submitting strong scores (ACT 30-34, SAT 1390-1520) is still beneficial for competitive programs.\n\n---\n\n## What readers want\n\nUIUC's official essay tips emphasize five things: answer the specific prompt, reference your own experiences, be concise, edit carefully, be yourself.\nSource: https://blog.admissions.illinois.edu/4-simple-steps-to-writing-the-perfect-illinois-essay/\n\nFrom the official \"Apply Like a Pro\" blog, UIUC's sample major essays (fictional majors) show the expected structure: (1) a specific, concrete experience; (2) what it taught you; (3) a direct bridge to the major.\nSource: https://blog.admissions.illinois.edu/apply-like-a-pro-examples-of-short-answer-college-application-questions/\n\nCollege Essay Guy's 2026-27 guide (updated September 8, 2026) notes that UIUC readers \"can tell when a response is generic and not written with a specific school's mission in mind.\" For Gies and Grainger in particular, essays are evaluated more selectively. \"The major-specific essays for direct-admit programs like CS in Engineering (sub-5% acceptance rate) are especially high-stakes.\"\nSource: https://www.collegeessayguy.com/blog/university-illinois-urbana-champaign-supplemental-essays\n\nThe CDS rates Application Essay as **Important** (not Very Important). Rigor of secondary school record and Academic GPA are both **Very Important**. Essays break ties; they rarely override a weak transcript.\nSource: https://www.collegedata.fyi/schools/university-of-illinois-urbana-champaign/2024-25\n\n---\n\n## Admitted essays: what they did\n\n**Note:** UIUC's official essay examples on its blog use fictional majors (\"Music Listening,\" \"DNA Analysis\") and are deliberately illustrative rather than real admitted essays. No actual applicant names or credentials are attached. The structure is what matters.\n\n**Official example structure (Prompt 1 — Experience):**\nAngle: A student who played triangle in band, then formed a music-listening club. The essay starts with a concrete action (formed the club), names a specific program they launched (\"Playlist Friday\"), and ends with a direct statement connecting the experience to the major. Every sentence moves forward.\nSource: https://blog.admissions.illinois.edu/apply-like-a-pro-examples-of-short-answer-college-application-questions/\n\n**Official example structure (Prompt 2 — Goals):**\nThe DNA Analysis example opens with a career goal (use biology to help people), specifies a mechanism (DNA lab research), names an ethical dimension of the field (storage and analysis ethics), and closes with a \"well-rounded scientist\" framing. It is forward-looking and specific without being grandiose.\nSource: https://blog.admissions.illinois.edu/apply-like-a-pro-examples-of-short-answer-college-application-questions/\n\n**College Essay Guy annotated example (real admitted Gies essay):**\nThe guide describes essays that worked for Gies by naming specific resources — iVenture, the entrepreneurship minor — while grounding the interest in a real experience (a summer business the student ran). Generic \"I've always loved business\" opening sentences were cut. The successful version opened mid-scene.\nSource: https://www.collegeessayguy.com/blog/university-illinois-urbana-champaign-supplemental-essays\n\n**CollegeVine admitted essay breakdown:**\nCollegeVine's UIUC essay analysis notes that the strongest 150-word answers \"do not waste a single word on transitions or pleasantries.\" The student starts with the experience in sentence 1. By sentence 3 there is a specific detail (a named project, competition, class, or discovery). The essay ends with a future-facing sentence about UIUC, not a restatement of the past.\nSource: https://blog.collegevine.com/uiuc-essay-example\n\n---\n\n## What separates strong essays\n\n**Pattern 1: Specificity at the sentence level.** \"I love business\" loses. \"I built a spreadsheet model for our family's catering business and found a $12,000 revenue gap\" wins. The 150-word limit forces applicants to choose between generic framing and one sharp, true detail. Strong essays always choose the detail.\n\n**Pattern 2: The experience genuinely connects to the major.** Readers are checking for coherence between the experience (Prompt 1) and the goal (Prompt 2). A student who describes a coding project and then lists a business major as their goal without bridging the two looks unfocused. The two 150-word answers must feel like parts of the same argument.\n\n**Pattern 3: The goal is specific enough to be falsifiable.** \"I want to start a company\" is not a goal. \"I want to build a supply-chain analytics tool for small food businesses and use Gies's operations management curriculum to understand unit economics before I launch\" is. Strong goals name a problem, a mechanism, and a specific way UIUC helps.\n\n**Pattern 4 (second-choice essay): The backup has its own logic.** Strong second-choice essays are not a hedge. They describe a genuine alternative interest and explain what connects it to the first-choice path. A Gies applicant listing Economics as second choice should explain what Economics enables — not just say \"I'm also interested in the economy.\"\n\n---\n\n## Clichés and mistakes to avoid\n\n- **Opening with \"Ever since I was young, I've been passionate about...\"** — wastes 8-12 words of a 150-word essay.\n- **Mentioning UIUC's ranking.** Readers find this hollow. The CDS shows UIUC itself marks this as Not Considered.\n- **\"Founding a startup\" as the entire essay.** Naming a venture is fine; spending all 150 words on your business idea without showing what you learned or what failed signals that you lack self-awareness.\n- **Repeating resume content.** The essay prompts say \"explain, in detail.\" Listing the same club title that's in your activities section without adding depth wastes the slot.\n- **Writing the same Gies essay for a different college.** College Essay Guy explicitly warns that readers can tell when a response is generic. If you are applying to Gies and listing Engineering as a second choice, both essays must be authentically distinct.\n- **Ignoring the Prompt 2 word \"personal.\"** The goals prompt asks for personal and/or career goals. Strong essays include at least a brief acknowledgment of why this matters beyond income or job title.\n- **Using the second-choice essay as an afterthought.** This is a missed signal about your real interests — and a missed chance to get admitted to a good backup.\n\n---\n\n## Specifics an applicant can name\n\n**Programs:**\n- **Landuyt Center for Entrepreneurship** (inside Grainger) — formerly the Technology Entrepreneur Center, renamed in 2026 after a $20M+ gift from Richard and Gayle Landuyt. Runs Cozad and the tech-venture course suite. Source: landuyt.illinois.edu\n- **iVenture Accelerator** — campus-wide, housed in Gies. Up to $20,000 non-dilutive funding, summer intensive, mentorship. Alumni have gone to Y Combinator, Techstars, and collectively raised $160M+. Source: iventure.illinois.edu\n- **Cozad New Venture Challenge** — Illinois's largest venture competition. In 2026: 350+ teams, $700,000+ in prizes, first-ever $100,000 grand prize. Source: landuyt.illinois.edu\n- **Orange & Blue Ventures** — launched 2026, a student-run venture fund that invests real capital. Can name it as a goal, not just a resource. Source: uiuc.md ref file\n- **Research Park & EnterpriseWorks** — 200+ companies on campus, student internship pipeline. Source: researchpark.illinois.edu\n- **ILEE program** — dual-degree engineering + entrepreneurship, lives inside Grainger. Source: giesbusiness.illinois.edu / grainger.illinois.edu\n- **Entrepreneurship Minor** — 16 credits, 70+ electives, open campus-wide.\n\n**Majors to name correctly:**\n- The degree is called **Strategy, Innovation & Entrepreneurship** (not \"entrepreneurship\"). Apply as **Business Undeclared** — not as SIE. Getting this wrong in an essay signals you didn't research the school.\n\n**Founders to name credibly:**\nMarc Andreessen (Netscape, a16z), Max Levchin (PayPal Mafia), Jawed Karim and Steve Chen (YouTube), Jeremy Stoppelman (Yelp). These are facts, not legend — name the specific connections.\n\n**Common errors:**\n- Calling the center \"TEC\" or \"Technology Entrepreneur Center\" — it was renamed the Landuyt Center in 2026. Using the old name signals outdated research.\n- Applying as \"Strategy, Innovation & Entrepreneurship\" directly — all first-year Gies students apply as Business Undeclared.\n- Listing DGS/undeclared as a second choice — fatal for Gies aspirants (no ICT path to Gies from DGS).\n\n---\n\n## Coaching questions\n\n1. The Prompt 1 essay asks for \"an experience, in detail.\" What is the single most specific thing you did — not a role you held, but one action on one day — that changed how you think about business or building things?\n\n2. You can say \"I ran a business.\" What did you learn about a specific failure or miscalculation in that business? What number went wrong, and what did you do about it?\n\n3. The Prompt 2 goal question is about your life after Illinois, not your time there. What problem do you want to be working on at 30? Can you describe the customer, the market, or the sector?\n\n4. If you get into Gies, you'll declare your major by end of sophomore year. Why SIE specifically rather than Finance or Management? What does the entrepreneurship track offer that a Finance track doesn't?\n\n5. Your second-choice major essay needs its own logic. If you list Economics, what would you use an Econ degree for that Gies couldn't give you? What does studying markets through a theory-first lens add to your entrepreneurship thinking?\n\n6. The iVenture accelerator takes top student startups for a summer intensive and up to $20,000. If you were selected the summer after freshman year, what would you be working on? Be specific about the problem, not just the domain.\n\n7. UIUC's Illinois Commitment is needs-based, not merit-based. Does your family's financial situation make UIUC a genuinely compelling value proposition, or is the in-state price advantage the main draw?\n\n8. UIUC marks \"demonstrated interest\" as Not Considered. Given that, what in your application should make a reader believe you genuinely want UIUC — and not just any large engineering school with a business program?\n\n9. The Cozad competition had 350+ teams in 2026. What would your pitch be? What makes it different from a case-competition project?\n\n10. Marc Andreessen graduated from UIUC and built Netscape. Max Levchin (PayPal) is a UIUC alum. How does the PayPal Mafia connection actually change what UIUC could do for your career versus a school that does not have that alumni density?\n\n11. You have 150 words for each essay. In your current draft, how many words appear before your first specific, concrete fact? If the answer is more than 15, what would it look like to start with that fact instead?\n\n12. If you are admitted only to your second-choice major in LAS and must do ICT to Gies at the end of year 1, what is your plan? Which Gies core courses would you take in LAS? Do you know what GPA ICT historically requires?\n\n---\n\n## Sources\n\n- UIUC Admissions — official essay prompts: https://admissions.illinois.edu/Apply/freshman/essays\n- UIUC Admissions blog — \"Apply Like a Pro\" official major essay examples: https://blog.admissions.illinois.edu/apply-like-a-pro-examples-of-short-answer-college-application-questions/\n- UIUC Admissions blog — essay tips: https://blog.admissions.illinois.edu/4-simple-steps-to-writing-the-perfect-illinois-essay/\n- College Essay Guy — UIUC supplemental guide 2026/27 (updated September 8, 2026): https://www.collegeessayguy.com/blog/university-illinois-urbana-champaign-supplemental-essays\n- CollegeVine — UIUC admitted essay example and breakdown: https://blog.collegevine.com/uiuc-essay-example\n- Gies College of Business — undergraduate admissions FAQ: https://giesbusiness.illinois.edu/apply/admissions\n- Gies College of Business — transfer handbook: https://www.admissions.illinois.edu/content/docs/handbook_bus.pdf\n- UIUC myIllini — Strategy, Innovation & Entrepreneurship major detail: https://myillini.illinois.edu/Programs/MajorDetail/10KM6127BS\n- UIUC Common Data Set 2024-25: https://www.collegedata.fyi/schools/university-of-illinois-urbana-champaign/2024-25\n- Illinois Commitment — OSFA eligibility and FAQ: https://www.osfa.illinois.edu/types-of-aid/other-aid/illinois-commitment/ and https://www.osfa.illinois.edu/types-of-aid/other-aid/illinois-commitment/illinois-commitment-frequently-asked-questions/\n- Illinois Commitment income threshold expansion (October 2024): https://news.illinois.edu/illinois-commitment-expands-eligibility-for-tuition-free-college-to-more-illinois-families/\n- UIUC Senate Admissions Committee Annual Report 2024-25: https://www.senate.illinois.edu/2024-2025/20250428senate/AD2506_FINAL_20250428.pdf\n- CollegeVine — EA vs RD discussion: https://www.collegevine.com/faq/164791/how-does-uiuc-early-action-compare-to-regular-decision-acceptance-rates\n","indiana":"## Part 1 — Verified application facts (from the Founder's Compass dashboard)\n\n# Indiana University Bloomington (id: indiana)\n\nPlatform: Common App / IU app | Test: Test-optional | Selectivity: {\"rate\":\"≈80%\",\"tier\":\"Accessible — the most attainable of the 12\",\"note\":\"Most accessible overall; Kelley Direct Admit (now a holistic, KPI-based review) is the competitive gate. IU is generous with automatic merit for strong out-of-state students.\",\"cycle\":\"prior\"}\n\nDeadlines: [{\"plan\":\"Early Action / scholarship priority\",\"date\":\"Nov 1\",\"note\":\"Priority for Direct Admit, honors and scholarships\",\"cycle\":\"ok\"},{\"plan\":\"Regular (rolling thereafter)\",\"date\":\"Feb 1\",\"note\":\"Largely rolling and non-binding\",\"cycle\":\"ok\"}]\n\nApp note: IU uses the Common App (or the IU application). An IU-specific essay on your academic and career plans is requested; IU has historically been test-optional with a relatively light essay requirement.\n\nEntrepreneurship headline: Kelley's Entrepreneurship & Corporate Innovation major is a perennial top-5 undergraduate program — paired with IU Innovates' incubator and the JCEI's competitions — at an admit rate that makes it a rare elite-program Target.\n\nDegrees: Kelley BS in Entrepreneurship & Corporate Innovation · certificate in entrepreneurship · pairs with Luddy (informatics/CS) and the arts\n\n## Supplements (verified 2026-27 where cycle=ok)\nIU's academic-interest essay (most recent wording shown) — confirm the 2026-27 prompt at the Aug 1 launch.\n\n- **Academic & career interests** [Requested, 200–400 words, cycle=ok]: Describe your academic and career plans and any special interest (for example, undergraduate research, academic interests, or leadership opportunities) that you are eager to pursue as an undergraduate at Indiana University. If you encountered any unusual circumstances, challenges, or obstacles in pursuit of your education, share those experiences and how you overcame them.\n\n## Programs\n- Kelley — Entrepreneurship & Corporate Innovation: A dedicated, top-5-ranked undergraduate entrepreneurship major inside a leading public business school.\n- Johnson Center for Entrepreneurship & Innovation (JCEI): Kelley's entrepreneurship engine — courses, the Velocity living-learning center and pitch competitions.\n- IU Innovates: Campus-wide incubator and accelerator with seed funding, mentorship and the Founders & Funders Network.\n- Shoemaker Innovation Center: Luddy School maker/innovation hub feeding tech-leaning student ventures.\n\n## Funding/competitions\n- Clapp IDEA Competition: JCEI pitch competition awarding up to $35,000 to student startups (a 2026 winner took $25,000 for PilliPop).\n- IU Innovates incubator + Startup Summer: Seed funding, workspace and a paid summer build program at The Mill / Amplify Bloomington.\n- Founders & Funders Network Venture Summit: Connects student founders with IU-affiliated entrepreneurs and investors (incl. a Broadcast.com co-founder).\n\n## Watch-outs\n- Kelley Direct Admit is competitive — miss it and you reapply to Kelley as a sophomore via Standard Admit.\n- Bloomington is a classic college town; coastal VC density is lower (Indianapolis/Midwest is the network).\n- Strongest for business-model founders; less of a deep-tech hardware pipeline than the engineering powerhouses.\n\n## Known successful-essay sources\n- An IU Kelley supplemental essay that worked (Essays That Worked) https://essaysthatworked.com/essay-library/47-walking-into-school-on-the-first-day-of — full admitted 'academic & career plans' essay\n- How to write the Indiana University essay (CollegeVine) https://blog.collegevine.com/how-to-write-the-indiana-university-bloomington-essays — 2024–25 · guidance + illustrative excerpts for IU's prompt\n- A real admitted Kelley essay, annotated (Veridian Prep) https://www.veridianprep.com/blog/real-college-essays-that-worked-indiana — full personal statement with why-it-worked notes\n\n## Other fields\n- short: Indiana\n- mono: I\n- city: Bloomington\n- state: IN\n- type: Public\n- setting: Classic Big Ten college town\n- undergrad: ≈48,000\n- platform: Common App / IU app\n- ac: #9E1B32\n- ac2: #5c0a18\n- fit: 86\n- verdict: Excellent fit\n- sub: {\"program\":9,\"experiential\":8,\"outcomes\":7,\"location\":5}\n- rateNum: 80\n- dlSort: 1101\n- testPolicy: Test-optional\n- sat: ≈1230–1440\n- act: ≈26–33\n- fee: $65\n- recs: Optional\n- opens: August 1\n- bestFor: Students who want a top-5 undergraduate entrepreneurship major (Kelley's Entrepreneurship & Corporate Innovation) without a single-digit admit rate — an outstanding Target/Likely with strong merit aid that anchors a balanced college list.\n- strengths: [\"Kelley School of Business — a top public business school strong in entrepreneurship, finance and marketing.\",\"Genuinely accessible admission with generous automatic merit for strong applicants.\",\"A vibrant, quintessential Big Ten campus experience.\",\"Mark Cuban (Kelley alum) anchors a real founder-and-funder network.\"]\n- entreRank: #5 undergraduate entrepreneurship — U.S. News (Kelley)\n- founders: Mark Cuban (Kelley) headlines a deep alumni-investor network; the Indianapolis and Midwest startup scenes are the immediate ecosystem.\n- optIntro: IU's academic-interest essay (most recent wording shown) — confirm the 2026-27 prompt at the Aug 1 launch.\n- cost: {\"mode\":\"public\",\"inState\":\"≈$30,600\",\"outState\":\"≈$63,000\",\"aid\":\"IU is unusually generous with automatic merit for strong out-of-state students (Selective/Automatic Scholarships), which can substantially narrow the in-state/OOS gap.\"}\n- cardEnt: Kelley #5 entrepreneurship major · IU Innovates · accessible.\n- src: Sourced from Indiana University admissions (test-optional; rolling/EA dates prior-cycle), the Kelley School (U.S. News #5 entrepreneurship; Direct Admit now holistic), kelley.iu.edu and news.iu.edu. Overall rate and merit specifics should be verified.\n\n## Part 2 — Deep research: paths in, what readers want, admitted essays\n\n# Indiana University Bloomington (indiana)\n\nVeer is out-of-state from Illinois. IU is generous with merit for strong OOS applicants and Kelley's Entrepreneurship & Corporate Innovation program is a top-5 undergraduate entrepreneurship major with a much more accessible overall admit rate than peer programs. The key gate is Kelley Direct Admit — and that process changed significantly in August 2025.\n\n---\n\n## Paths in\n\n### Overall admission\n\nIU Bloomington's overall admit rate is approximately 80%. The school uses the Common App or its own Apply IU application. Admission is largely rolling. There is no ED or REA; EA (Nov 1) is non-binding. Apply EA to maximize scholarship and Kelley Direct Admit priority.\n\n### Kelley Direct Admit — the real gate\n\nGetting admitted to IU is easy. Getting Direct Admit to Kelley is the competitive step.\n\n**Change effective for Fall 2027 applicants:** As of August 2025, there are no automatic criteria for Kelley Direct Admit. The old automatic pathway (ACT 30+, SAT 1370+, GPA 3.8 — listed on a still-live legacy page at kelley.iu.edu/undergraduate/admissions/first-year-students/automatic-direct-admission/) no longer applies for Fall 2027. All applicants must complete the Kelley Prospect Inventory (KPI) and undergo a holistic review by the Kelley Bloomington Admissions Committee.\n\nFrom the official Kelley Direct Admit page (Fall 2027): \"There are no criteria for automatic direct admission for fall 2027. All students who submit the KPI will undergo a comprehensive academic review by the Kelley Bloomington Admissions Committee. This review will consider factors such as high school performance, rigor of high school coursework, academic achievements, and potential for success at the Kelley School.\"\nSource: https://kelley.iu.edu/undergraduate/admissions/first-year-students/index.html\n\n**What the KPI is:** 20 multiple-choice questions. No right or wrong answers. The KPI explores leadership style, business interests, personal aspirations, and problem-solving approach. It takes about 20 minutes. It is designed to reveal \"who you are\" beyond transcript data — but that does not mean preparation is irrelevant. Answers that reflect genuine thought about business, leadership, and entrepreneurship will be more coherent than rushed answers.\n\n**Kelley Direct Admit process for Fall 2027:**\n1. Apply to IU Bloomington by November 1. Select a Kelley business major on the application.\n2. Complete the KPI through the IU Admissions Portal by November 15 for priority consideration.\n3. If admitted to IU by January 15 and KPI is submitted, Kelley direct admit decision comes simultaneously — admitted, waitlisted, or pre-business.\n4. Students who miss the November 15 KPI deadline can still complete it by February 15 and receive a decision by April 16. Because the class is capacity-limited, earlier is better.\nSource: https://kelley.iu.edu/undergraduate/admissions/first-year-students/index.html\n\n**Kelley's stated criteria for the holistic review:** high school performance, rigor of coursework (especially quantitative/math), academic achievements, and potential for success. Letters of recommendation are not used. Test scores are not used in the Kelley review even if submitted to IU.\nSource: https://blog.kelley.iu.edu/2025/08/04/kelley-school-of-business-announces-new-direct-admission-process-expanding-opportunities-for-high-achieving-students/\n\n**Three outcomes when you apply as a business major:**\n- **Kelley Direct Admit** — immediate access to Kelley academic and career advisors, priority registration for business courses.\n- **Pre-business** — start as pre-business, then apply to Kelley through Standard Admission after year 1.\n- **Second-choice major** — admitted to your second-choice major. **Critical:** Students admitted to a second-choice major cannot switch to a Kelley major during undergraduate study. They may add a Kelley minor, certificate, or specialized master's, but cannot become a Kelley degree student.\nSource: https://kelley.iu.edu/undergraduate/admissions/first-year-students/index.html\n\n### Standard admission path (if not direct admit)\n\nStudents who land as pre-business can apply to Kelley after completing year 1. The standard admit requirements were tightened in August 2025:\n\n- For students entering IU in Fall 2025 to Spring 2026: must earn at least a B+ in all first-year business-track courses to qualify for automatic standard admission.\n- For students entering IU in Fall 2026 to Spring 2027 (Veer's cohort): must earn at least an **A-** in all relevant courses to qualify for automatic standard admission.\nSource: https://www.idsnews.com/article/2025/08/kelley-school-implements-new-admissions-policies\n\nThis is a meaningful change. Students who rely on the pre-business route now face a higher GPA bar. Planning for it — rather than assuming it will be easy — is essential.\n\n### Hutton Honors College\n\nHutton is IU's university-wide honors college. It accepts **fewer than 10% of each entering class** — genuinely selective.\n\n**What it offers:** smaller seminars, honors research opportunities, priority scheduling, access to interdisciplinary courses, and a dedicated advising community. Hutton is compatible with Kelley direct admit — an applicant can pursue both simultaneously.\n\n**How to apply:**\n- Check the Hutton Honors College box on the Common App. This unlocks the HHC application in your IU portal.\n- Complete the HHC application by **February 1** to be considered for an HHC Scholarship.\n- Apply to IU by **November 1** to be considered for Hutton with scholarship consideration.\n- Notifications by April 1.\n\n**Scholarship amounts:** HHC Scholars awards range from $1,000 to $4,000 per year, conditionally renewable. A small number of admits receive the formal \"Hutton Honors Scholar\" designation.\n\n**Evaluation criteria:** \"Greatest emphasis placed on grades earned in academic courses; performance in honors, dual-credit, IB, and AP courses; extracurricular activities; other achievements.\" Aptitude for interdisciplinary learning is a stated value.\nSource: https://hutton.indiana.edu/admissions/prospective-freshmen.html\n\nFor Veer as an OOS applicant: Hutton is worth pursuing. The honors designation strengthens the record for grad school or employment. The scholarship partially offsets OOS cost. Hutton membership persists regardless of Kelley admit status.\n\n### Out-of-state merit scholarships\n\nIU awards merit scholarships through the Office of Enrollment Management (OEM). The relevant scholarship for Veer is the **National Excellence Scholarship** (for out-of-state students).\n\nKey facts:\n- Apply to IU by **November 1**. That application is also the scholarship application — no separate form.\n- Scholarships are awarded based **solely on academic performance** — high school GPA, rigor of coursework (AP, IB, honors), and grades in required courses. **Test scores are not considered** in scholarship review, even if submitted.\n- Award amounts are not publicly disclosed. IU calls the internal criteria proprietary.\n- All scholarship decisions are made by April 1.\nSource: https://scholarships.indiana.edu/future-scholars/iu-academic-scholarships.html\n\nThe OEM scholarships can substantially narrow the OOS cost gap. Applying by November 1 is the single most important action for an OOS student concerned about cost.\n\n### Demonstrated interest and legacy\n\nIU's admissions page does not claim to track demonstrated interest. No formal legacy policy found. Campus visits are welcome but not required.\n\n---\n\n## What readers want\n\nIU's academic interest essay prompt (200-400 words, confirmed for 2026-27): \"Describe your academic and career plans and any special interest (for example, undergraduate research, academic interests, or leadership opportunities) that you are eager to pursue as an undergraduate at Indiana University. If you encountered any unusual circumstances, challenges, or obstacles in pursuit of your education, share those experiences and how you overcame them.\"\n\nThis is one essay. No Kelley-specific supplemental essays exist. The KPI is a separate multiple-choice questionnaire, not an essay.\n\nWhat the essay needs to do:\n1. Name Kelley and your intended major. Vague \"I'm interested in business\" without naming the Entrepreneurship & Corporate Innovation program signals you haven't done basic research.\n2. Connect your specific experience to specific Kelley resources. Name the JCEI, the Clapp IDEA Competition, the Velocity living-learning community, or a specific professor. Generic \"Kelley has a great reputation\" sentences are the most common weakness.\n3. Demonstrate genuine entrepreneurial thinking. Kelley's USASBE award and Dr. Kuratko's program design center on \"the entrepreneurial mindset\" — not just starting companies. Show you understand the distinction between starting and building.\n4. Keep the obstacle/challenges section proportionate. If you do not have a meaningful obstacle to discuss, do not invent one. The prompt says \"if you encountered\" — it is optional.\n\nFrom the Kelley admissions blog (August 2025): \"The new admission approach gives students a chance to show us who they are, what they've achieved academically, and why they're interested in business — something a GPA or test score alone can't capture.\"\nSource: https://blog.kelley.iu.edu/2025/08/04/kelley-school-of-business-announces-new-direct-admission-process-expanding-opportunities-for-high-achieving-students/\n\n---\n\n## Admitted essays: what they did\n\n**Example 1 — Essays That Worked (full admitted essay, ~300 words):**\nPrompt: Describe your academic and career plans and any special interest you are eager to pursue at Indiana University.\n\nOpening: \"Walking into school on the first day of my senior year, the excitement about college was evident as I passed through the halls.\" The essay connects interest in Kelley to Chinese language study and international business, emphasizes collaborative learning, and closes with a reference to Kelley's alumni network size.\n\n*Why it worked (narrowly) and what it got wrong:* The essay named Kelley and connected a specific skill (Chinese language) to a business angle (global leadership). But it is generic by today's standards. It names no specific program, no professor, no competition. \"The Kelley School has the largest alumni population of any other business school\" is a résumé-data point, not an insight. A 2027 applicant submitting this essay would be outcompeted by anyone who names the JCEI, Dr. Kuratko, or the Clapp IDEA Competition. Use this as a floor, not a model.\nSource: https://essaysthatworked.com/essay-library/47-walking-into-school-on-the-first-day-of\n\n**Example 2 — CollegeVine guidance (illustrative excerpts, 2024-25 cycle):**\nCollegeVine's analysis of strong IU essays notes that the best responses \"demonstrate research into IU's specific programs\" and show \"a clear connection between what you've done, what you want to do, and what IU specifically offers.\" Essays that refer only to Kelley's ranking or Mark Cuban as an alum without connecting those facts to a specific goal are noted as weak.\nSource: https://blog.collegevine.com/how-to-write-the-indiana-university-bloomington-essays\n\n**Example 3 — Veridian Prep annotated essay (admitted personal statement):**\nThe annotated essay at Veridian Prep shows that the successful IU applicant used the personal statement (Common App essay) to establish a compelling personal narrative and then used the IU essay to translate that narrative into a specific academic plan. The two essays worked together rather than repeating the same story.\nSource: https://www.veridianprep.com/blog/real-college-essays-that-worked-indiana\n\n---\n\n## What separates strong essays\n\n**Pattern 1: Specific program knowledge, named correctly.** Strong essays name the Johnson Center for Entrepreneurship & Innovation (JCEI), not just \"Kelley's entrepreneurship program.\" They name Dr. Donald Kuratko (Dr. K) by role, not just as a professor. They mention the Clapp IDEA Competition ($35,000 award to student startups) or the Velocity living-learning community — not generic \"competitions and living-learning programs.\"\n\n**Pattern 2: The arc from past experience to future goal passes through a JCEI resource.** The strongest structure is: here is what I have built or done → here is what I learned that I still need → here is exactly how Kelley/JCEI provides that. Veer's entrepreneurial work should feed directly into a named Kelley program or course sequence.\n\n**Pattern 3: The essay demonstrates an entrepreneurial mindset, not just entrepreneurial résumé.** Dr. Kuratko's program explicitly values the entrepreneurial mindset across organizational contexts — not just startups. An essay that shows how Veer thinks about problems (sees opportunity, tests assumptions, tolerates ambiguity) will outperform an essay that just lists his ventures.\n\n**Pattern 4 (OOS-specific): The Illinois-to-Indiana argument.** An out-of-state applicant should not need to explain why they chose IU over a closer school — readers at a large state school are used to strong OOS students. But if the essay implicitly signals \"I'm applying here as a safety,\" that is detectable. The essay should read as if IU is genuinely a top choice, which means naming things only a researched applicant would know.\n\n---\n\n## Clichés and mistakes to avoid\n\n- **\"Mark Cuban went here.\"** Naming a famous alum without connecting it to what you would actually do at IU is filler. Everyone knows. Say nothing about Cuban unless you can connect it to a specific reason you want to attend IU over a school with different alumni density.\n- **\"Kelley is ranked top 5 in entrepreneurship.\"** Citing rankings to a school is rarely a winning move. The JCEI won USASBE's 2023 Model Entrepreneurship Program Award — cite that instead, which signals you actually researched the program.\n- **Spending half the essay on obstacles.** The obstacle/challenge section is optional and secondary. Use it only if you have a genuine story that adds something the rest of the application doesn't show. Do not manufacture a challenge.\n- **Describing the KPI as an essay to prepare separately.** The KPI is multiple-choice. Applicants sometimes conflate it with the IU academic interest essay. They are two separate pieces.\n- **Applying to a second-choice major intending to transfer to Kelley.** Per IU's official page: students admitted to a second-choice major cannot switch to a Kelley major. The only paths to Kelley are direct admit or the pre-business standard admit route.\n- **Assuming the old automatic criteria still apply.** The ACT 30 / SAT 1370 / GPA 3.8 thresholds for automatic direct admit ended for Fall 2026 entrants and beyond. The old webpage still exists but the current page explicitly says \"no criteria for automatic direct admission for fall 2027.\"\n\n---\n\n## Specifics an applicant can name\n\n**The correct name of the entrepreneurship center:**\n- **Johnson Center for Entrepreneurship & Innovation (JCEI)** — not \"Shaffer Center.\" Research for this file found no evidence of a \"Shaffer Center\" at IU or Kelley. The BRIEF's reference to \"Shaffer Center\" appears to be an error. The correct name is JCEI.\n- JCEI is led by **Dr. Donald F. Kuratko**, the Jack M. Gill Distinguished Chair of Entrepreneurship. Known to students as \"Dr. K.\" He has led the center since 2004 and authored the leading entrepreneurship textbook. Name him and his work in the essay.\nSource: https://kelley.iu.edu/faculty-research/centers-institutes/entrepreneurship-innovation/index.html\n\n**The correct description of venture funding:**\n- \"Kelley Venture Fund\" as described in the BRIEF does not appear to exist as a named program. Research found no student-run fund with that name at Kelley.\n- What does exist: **IU Ventures** manages the **IU Philanthropic Venture Fund** ($11M committed), the **IU Angel Network** (alumni invest in IU-affiliated startups), and the **Shoebox Fund** (seed fund for student startups built inside the Shoemaker Innovation Center at Luddy). These are not student-run investment funds; they are university-managed programs.\n- **UIUC** has Orange & Blue Ventures (launched 2026) — a student-run venture fund — but that is at Illinois, not Indiana.\nSource: https://iuventures.com/portfolio/ and https://engagement.iu.edu/engagement/venture-fund/index.html\n\n**Competitions:**\n- **Clapp IDEA Competition** — JCEI's flagship pitch competition. Awards up to $35,000. In 2026, a winner (PilliPop) took $25,000. Can name this specifically in an essay.\n\n**Living-learning:**\n- **Velocity Business Living-Learning Community** — themed residence community for Kelley students interested in entrepreneurship and innovation. Naming it shows you have gone beyond the admissions website.\n\n**Cross-campus certificate:**\n- A customizable **Certificate in Entrepreneurship** that lets students from any IU school (music, engineering, arts) take 3 Kelley entrepreneurship courses + 2 courses from their own school. Dr. Kuratko created it in 2010 specifically to reach non-business students.\n\n**Faculty to name credibly:**\n- Dr. Donald Kuratko (\"Dr. K\") — Executive & Academic Director, JCEI; Jack M. Gill Distinguished Chair; author of the leading entrepreneurship textbook.\n- Dr. Greg Fisher — top researcher and teaching award winner; researches \"entrepreneurial hustle.\"\n- Dr. Jeff McMullen — editor-in-chief of the *Journal of Business Venturing* (the #1 academic journal in the field); researches entrepreneurial decision-making.\n- Dr. Sophie Bacq — authority in social entrepreneurship.\nSource: https://poetsandquants.com/2023/02/21/indiana-kelleys-pioneering-entrepreneurship-program-is-chosen-as-a-model-for-the-field/\n\n**How an OOS Illinois student should approach it:**\nVeer should not downplay the OOS angle or apologize for it. A strong Chicago-area applicant who names specific Kelley resources is exactly the profile IU wants. The essay should position IU as a deliberate, researched choice — not a geographic fallback. Given that UIUC is in-state and financially compelling for Veer, the IU essay needs to articulate something specific about Kelley's entrepreneurship program, JCEI culture, or Midwest founder network that UIUC cannot offer.\n\n---\n\n## Coaching questions\n\n1. The IU essay asks you to describe your academic and career plans \"at Indiana University.\" That means you need to describe something you can only do at Kelley, not just something you want to do in general. What specifically does JCEI offer that you can't get at UIUC's Gies or at a Chicago-area school?\n\n2. Dr. Kuratko distinguishes between \"starting a business\" and developing \"the entrepreneurial mindset across organizations.\" Which one describes your actual goal, and what's the difference in how you would write the essay for each?\n\n3. The Clapp IDEA Competition awards up to $35,000. If you entered as a freshman, what would your pitch be? Not the business — the pitch. What problem, for whom, and why now?\n\n4. The KPI is 20 multiple-choice questions about how you approach leadership, problem-solving, and business. These are not essay questions. What does it mean to you that the new Kelley review process is holistic rather than automatic? Does your profile work better under a holistic review, and why?\n\n5. The old automatic criteria required ACT 30, SAT 1370, and GPA 3.8 for automatic direct admit. Do your stats meet that bar? If not, what does your application show that would differentiate you in a holistic review?\n\n6. If you do not receive Kelley Direct Admit and start as pre-business, the standard admit route now requires an A- in all relevant first-year courses. Is that realistic for you? What does your track record in challenging courses tell you?\n\n7. The Hutton Honors College accepts fewer than 10% of applicants and emphasizes \"interdisciplinary learning.\" You are interested in entrepreneurship. Where in your application do you show breadth — curiosity outside business that makes you a better entrepreneur?\n\n8. You are an OOS student from Illinois. UIUC is in-state and financially advantageous. What is the honest answer to the question \"why IU instead of UIUC?\" Your essay should answer this question even if it never asks it directly.\n\n9. The IU Philanthropic Venture Fund invests in IU-affiliated companies at the seed stage. The IU Angel Network connects student investors with founders. In four years, do you see yourself on the founder side, the investor side, or both? How does your answer change what you name in the essay?\n\n10. The failed essay example from Essays That Worked mentions Kelley's \"largest alumni population of any business school.\" Is that true? (It is not verified.) What happens if a reader knows it's wrong? What should you name instead that you have verified?\n\n11. Your essay can be 200-400 words. That is short. How many words does it take you to get to your first specific fact — a named program, competition, professor, or experience? If it is more than 50 words, what would you cut?\n\n12. IU has a West Coast Advisory Board and a partnership with Plug & Play in Silicon Valley (a JCEI initiative). Does that matter to you? If your entrepreneurial goals are tied to Chicago or the Midwest, is the Indianapolis-Bloomington network sufficient, or does that create a gap?\n\n---\n\n## Sources\n\n- Kelley Direct Admit — current Fall 2027 process: https://kelley.iu.edu/undergraduate/admissions/first-year-students/index.html\n- Kelley automatic direct admission legacy page (old criteria, no longer applicable for Fall 2027): https://kelley.iu.edu/undergraduate/admissions/first-year-students/automatic-direct-admission/index.html\n- Kelley blog — new direct admission process announced August 4, 2025: https://blog.kelley.iu.edu/2025/08/04/kelley-school-of-business-announces-new-direct-admission-process-expanding-opportunities-for-high-achieving-students/\n- Indiana Daily Student — Kelley new admissions policies, August 2025: https://www.idsnews.com/article/2025/08/kelley-school-implements-new-admissions-policies\n- Hutton Honors College — prospective freshmen admissions: https://hutton.indiana.edu/admissions/prospective-freshmen.html\n- Hutton Honors College — freshman scholarships: https://hutton.indiana.edu/funding/scholarships/freshmen-scholarship.html\n- IU OEM Academic Excellence Scholarships: https://scholarships.indiana.edu/future-scholars/iu-academic-scholarships.html\n- IU Scholarships — test-optional FAQ: https://scholarships.indiana.edu/test-optional/faq.html\n- Johnson Center for Entrepreneurship & Innovation: https://kelley.iu.edu/faculty-research/centers-institutes/entrepreneurship-innovation/index.html\n- Poets & Quants — Dr. Kuratko and JCEI Model Program Award 2023: https://poetsandquants.com/2023/02/21/indiana-kelleys-pioneering-entrepreneurship-program-is-chosen-as-a-model-for-the-field/\n- Inside Indiana Business — Kelley Model Entrepreneurship Program award: https://www.insideindianabusiness.com/articles/kelley-school-named-model-entrepreneurship-program\n- IU Ventures portfolio and programs: https://iuventures.com/portfolio/\n- IU Philanthropic Venture Fund: https://engagement.iu.edu/engagement/venture-fund/index.html\n- IU Angel Network / STARTedUP partnership: https://iuventures.com/iu-angel-network-and-the-startedup-foundation-form-partnership-for-students-studying-entrepreneurship-at-indiana-university/\n- Essays That Worked — admitted IU academic interest essay: https://essaysthatworked.com/essay-library/47-walking-into-school-on-the-first-day-of\n- CollegeVine — how to write the Indiana University essays (2024-25): https://blog.collegevine.com/how-to-write-the-indiana-university-bloomington-essays\n- Veridian Prep — real admitted Kelley essay annotated: https://www.veridianprep.com/blog/real-college-essays-that-worked-indiana\n","berkeley":"## Part 1 — Verified application facts (from the Founder's Compass dashboard)\n\n# University of California, Berkeley (id: berkeley)\n\nPlatform: UC Application | Test: Test-blind | Selectivity: {\"rate\":\"10.5%\",\"tier\":\"Most selective · EECS & M.E.T. far lower\",\"note\":\"UC Application; test-blind. 10.5% for fall 2026 (Class of 2030), reclaiming Berkeley's title as the most selective UC campus — just edging UCLA at 10.8%. EECS admits under 5%; the M.E.T. dual degree is ≈2–3%. Out-of-state and international pay full freight.\",\"cycle\":\"prior\"}\n\nDeadlines: [{\"plan\":\"UC application filing period\",\"date\":\"Oct 1–Dec 2\",\"note\":\"One filing window; start Aug 1, submit by Dec 2. M.E.T. may add steps.\",\"cycle\":\"ok\"}]\n\nApp note: Berkeley uses the UC Application (not the Common App). You answer 4 of 8 Personal Insight Questions, 350 words each. No Berkeley-specific supplements for most applicants; M.E.T. applicants complete extra steps.\n\nEntrepreneurship headline: Berkeley is the #1 public-university founder factory: the Sutardja Center (SCET) and its Collider Cup feed Berkeley SkyDeck — a top global accelerator — minutes from the world's deepest venture capital.\n\nDegrees: Certificate in Entrepreneurship & Technology (SCET) · M.E.T. dual degree (Haas + Engineering) · Haas undergraduate program (junior entry) · pairs with EECS\n\n## Supplements (verified 2026-27 where cycle=ok)\nChoose any 4 of these 8 prompts (350 words each) — standardized across every UC campus.\n\n- **PIQ 1 — Leadership** [Pick 4 of 8, 350 words, cycle=ok]: Describe an example of your leadership experience in which you have positively influenced others, helped resolve disputes or contributed to group efforts over time.\n- **PIQ 2 — Creativity** [Pick 4 of 8, 350 words, cycle=ok]: Every person has a creative side, and it can be expressed in many ways: problem solving, original and innovative thinking, and artistically, to name a few. Describe how you express your creative side.\n- **PIQ 3 — Greatest talent or skill** [Pick 4 of 8, 350 words, cycle=ok]: What would you say is your greatest talent or skill? How have you developed and demonstrated that talent over time?\n- **PIQ 4 — Educational opportunity or barrier** [Pick 4 of 8, 350 words, cycle=ok]: Describe how you have taken advantage of a significant educational opportunity or worked to overcome an educational barrier you have faced.\n- **PIQ 5 — Significant challenge** [Pick 4 of 8, 350 words, cycle=ok]: Describe the most significant challenge you have faced and the steps you have taken to overcome this challenge. How has this challenge affected your academic achievement?\n- **PIQ 6 — Academic subject that inspires you** [Pick 4 of 8, 350 words, cycle=ok]: Think about an academic subject that inspires you. Describe how you have furthered this interest inside and/or outside of the classroom.\n- **PIQ 7 — Making your community better** [Pick 4 of 8, 350 words, cycle=ok]: What have you done to make your school or your community a better place?\n- **PIQ 8 — What makes you a strong candidate** [Pick 4 of 8, 350 words, cycle=ok]: Beyond what has already been shared in your application, what do you believe makes you a strong candidate for admissions to the University of California?\n\n## Programs\n- Sutardja Center (SCET): Engineering's entrepreneurship center; a Certificate in Entrepreneurship & Technology open to undergrads, plus the Collider Cup showcase.\n- Berkeley SkyDeck: Berkeley's flagship accelerator (Pad-13) backing student, alumni and external startups with funding and a deep VC network.\n- M.E.T. (Management, Entrepreneurship & Technology): A freshman-admit dual degree (Haas + Engineering), ≈2–3% admit — built expressly for technical founders.\n- Big Ideas / Free Ventures / Jacobs Institute: Campus-wide contest, student-run accelerator and a design-innovation hub for early ventures.\n\n## Funding/competitions\n- Berkeley SkyDeck Pad-13: Selective incubator track with investment and demo days; SCET's top Collider Cup teams advance here.\n- Big Ideas Contest: Long-running campus innovation competition awarding seed funding across categories.\n- Silicon Valley VC pipeline: Bay Area proximity means student teams routinely raise from top VCs (recent ACC InVenture and SkyDeck wins).\n\n## Watch-outs\n- Massive scale and 'impacted' majors; EECS (<5%) and M.E.T. (≈2–3%) are brutally selective.\n- Test-blind: the four PIQs, course rigor and GPA carry everything.\n- Out-of-state students pay full freight with very limited aid.\n\n## Known successful-essay sources\n- 20 UC Personal Insight Question examples (College Essay Guy) https://www.collegeessayguy.com/blog/uc-essay-examples-personal-insight-questions — 2026–27 · all 8 PIQs (shared across UCs), AO commentary\n- 18 UC Berkeley essays that worked (Essays That Worked) https://essaysthatworked.com/uc-berkeley-essay-examples — 2021–26 · Berkeley-admitted PIQ essays, by prompt\n- 12 UC essay examples (CollegeVine) https://blog.collegevine.com/university-of-california-essay-examples — 2023 · real PIQ essays with analysis\n\n## Other fields\n- short: UC Berkeley\n- mono: B\n- city: Berkeley\n- state: CA\n- type: Public\n- setting: Urban · SF Bay Area\n- undergrad: ≈33,100\n- platform: UC Application\n- ac: #003262\n- ac2: #010133\n- fit: 94\n- verdict: Elite for builders\n- sub: {\"program\":9,\"experiential\":9,\"outcomes\":10,\"location\":10}\n- rateNum: 10.5\n- dlSort: 1202\n- testPolicy: Test-blind\n- sat: —\n- act: —\n- fee: $80 / campus\n- recs: Not used for freshmen\n- opens: Aug 1 opens · Oct 1–Dec 2 submit\n- bestFor: Technical founders who want the single best public-university launchpad into Silicon Valley — Berkeley produces among the most VC-backed founders of any school — via SCET, SkyDeck and (for business + engineering) the ultra-selective M.E.T. program.\n- strengths: [\"The #1 public university, with world-class CS, EECS and engineering.\",\"Adjacent to Silicon Valley — unmatched proximity to venture capital and talent.\",\"PitchBook ranks Berkeley #1 among universities for producing VC-backed founders.\",\"A research powerhouse feeding a constant stream of deep-tech startups.\"]\n- entreRank: #1 university for VC-backed founders (PitchBook); SCET certificate + M.E.T. dual degree\n- founders: Berkeley produces among the most VC-backed founders of any university (from Apple's Steve Wozniak to a deep modern roster) — and the Bay Area keeps capital and mentors next door.\n- optIntro: Choose any 4 of these 8 prompts (350 words each) — standardized across every UC campus.\n- cost: {\"mode\":\"public\",\"inState\":\"≈$45,000–$54,000\",\"outState\":\"≈$76,000–$94,000\",\"aid\":\"Strong need-based aid for California residents (Blue and Gold); Regents' is the top merit award. Out-of-state aid is very limited.\"}\n- cardEnt: #1 VC-founder factory · SCET + SkyDeck · M.E.T. dual degree.\n- src: Sourced from UC Berkeley Admissions and the UC Application (test-blind; 8 PIQs confirmed 2026-27), Berkeley OPA (11.43% rate), scet.berkeley.edu, skydeck.berkeley.edu and haas.berkeley.edu. EECS/M.E.T. rates are secondary estimates.\n\n## Part 2 — Deep research: paths in, what readers want, admitted essays\n\n# UC Berkeley — Advisor Knowledge Base\n\n*Applicant: Veer (out-of-state, Illinois). Entrepreneurship focus. Applying Fall 2027.*\n\n---\n\n## Paths in\n\n### The one gate: UC Application\nBerkeley uses only the UC Application (no Common App). One filing window, Oct 1–Nov 30, decisions in late March. No Early Decision, Early Action, or rolling admission. No demonstrated interest. No legacy preference (UC Regents Policy 2102, amended July 2022, explicitly prohibits preferential treatment based on alumni or donor affiliation). No interviews for most applicants.\n\n### Overall selectivity\nFall 2026: 10.5% overall admit rate (13,967 admits / 133,157 applicants). Fall 2025: 11% (14,502 / 126,843). Fall 2024: 11% (13,710 / 125,317). Middle 50% weighted GPA for 2026 admits: 4.36–4.72. Middle 50% unweighted: 3.90–4.00. (Sources: https://admissions.berkeley.edu/apply-to-berkeley/student-profile/ and https://admission.universityofcalifornia.edu/campuses-majors/berkeley/first-year-admit-data.html.)\n\n### Out-of-state realities\nBerkeley must prioritize California residents under UC policy and state budget agreements. For fall 2026, of Berkeley's 13,967 admitted first-years: 68% were California residents (9,445), 23% were domestic OOS (3,155), and 10% were international (1,367). (Source: UCOP Fall 2026 Preliminary Admissions Summary, https://www.ucop.edu/institutional-research-academic-planning/_files/factsheets/2026/admissions-fact-sheet-data-tables.pdf.) The effective domestic OOS admit rate is materially lower than the headline 10.5%. For fall 2024 Berkeley data: total domestic OOS admits were approximately 2,184 while total OOS applicants are not published separately — but with ~50,000+ OOS applicants and ~2,200 OOS admits, the OOS-specific rate is estimated at roughly 4–6%. Out-of-state cost: approximately $76,000–$94,000 per year (2024–25). Berkeley's Blue and Gold Program applies to California residents only; OOS financial aid is very limited.\n\n### Spring admission\nBerkeley **discontinued spring freshman admission in 2015**. There is no spring admit path for first-year students. The Fall Program for First Semester (FPF) — which originally served spring admits — was sunsetted: its final cohort ran in Fall 2025 and the program is now closed. (Source: https://fpf.berkeley.edu/.) Do not reference FPF as a current option. For Fall 2027 applicants, FPF does not exist.\n\n### Direct admit paths to Haas School of Business\nThis is Berkeley's most distinctive feature for business-minded freshmen. Three separate direct-admit tracks, each requiring the standard UC Application plus a Haas-specific supplemental application:\n\n**1. Berkeley Haas Spieker Undergraduate Business Program**\nA four-year BS in Business Administration. You submit the UC App listing the Spieker program as your primary major. After submitting the UC App, Haas sends a supplemental application (email arrives within 5–7 business days). The supplement includes one essay question and a video interview, both due in early January. Admission decisions are released in late March. Critical rules: (a) If you are not admitted to Spieker, **you will not be considered for admission to Berkeley at all** — there is no fallback to Letters & Science. (b) You must list a Haas program as your primary major, not alternate major. (Source: https://haas.berkeley.edu/undergrad/admissions/application-process/high-school-students/.) This is the highest-stakes choice among the three Haas freshman paths.\n\n**2. Global Management Program (GMP)**\nDirect-admit BS in Business Administration with a concentration in global management. Students must hold a valid passport and commit to a study abroad semester at the University of Cape Town in their third year. Same UC App + supplemental process as Spieker: essay about your interest in GMP, video interview, supplemental due early January. If not admitted to GMP, you are considered for L&S Undeclared — you do not lose your Berkeley consideration entirely, unlike Spieker. The GMP is the most internationally oriented of the three Haas freshman programs. (Source: https://haas.berkeley.edu/undergrad/programs/gmp/admissions/.)\n\n**3. Management, Entrepreneurship & Technology (M.E.T.)**\nThe most relevant track for Veer. A dual-degree program: one BS from the College of Engineering and one BS from Haas School of Business. You earn both degrees simultaneously. Eight engineering tracks available at application: Engineering Undeclared + Business, EECS + Business, Bioengineering + Business, Aerospace + Business, Civil + Business, Industrial Engineering & Operations Research + Business, Materials Science + Business, Mechanical Engineering + Business.\n\nAdmit rate: estimated 2–3%. The most selective freshman-admit path at Berkeley.\n\n**M.E.T. supplemental application (first-year):**\n- 350-word written essay: \"Share with us the world you come from (for example, your family, school, community, city, or town). What unique experiences from your world motivated you to apply to this business and engineering dual degree program?\"\n- 4-minute video essay: unscripted, evaluates genuine perspective and communication style. Video questions are revealed in the MAP@Berkeley portal.\n- Both are due by 11:59 pm PT on December 14, 2026 (for the Fall 2027 cycle, this date is approximate — check the official M.E.T. site for the current cycle deadline).\n- If you are not admitted to M.E.T., your application is still fully considered by the College of Engineering for your selected engineering major. (Source: https://met.berkeley.edu/admissions/freshman-admissions/ and https://met.berkeley.edu/admissions/supplemental-essay-prompts/.)\n\n**Key distinction:** M.E.T. is explicitly \"for students who want to be at the intersection of engineering and business\" and \"built expressly for technical founders.\" It is the only one of the three that does not put your entire Berkeley consideration at risk if you are not admitted.\n\n### The standard L&S and engineering paths\nFor the College of Letters & Science: you apply to a major (or undeclared), but major is not determinative in the review for L&S. EECS (Electrical Engineering and Computer Science) in the College of Engineering is a separate major with a separate review and an admit rate under 5%. Engineering Undeclared is also highly selective. For entrepreneurial students not going the M.E.T. route, L&S or College of Engineering (EECS or IEOR) are the standard entry points, with access to SCET entrepreneurship programs after enrollment.\n\n### Letters of recommendation — by invitation only\nBerkeley is the only UC campus to invite select applicants to submit letters of recommendation. This is a formal policy called Augmented Review. Details:\n- At most 15% of applicants are invited. Under UC policy (BOARS), invitations are targeted at first-generation college students, students qualifying for fee waivers, and students in early academic outreach programs. In practice, some high-achieving applicants in selective majors (EECS, M.E.T.) have reported receiving invitations, though Berkeley has not confirmed additional selection criteria.\n- Invitations are sent by email from October through early December, after you submit the UC App.\n- Letters are entirely optional. Students without a letter are not disadvantaged.\n- Up to two letters may be submitted. At least one must come from an academic teacher or instructor. The second can be from anyone who knows you well — a coach, employer, mentor, or clergy member.\n- Letters are due January 10.\n- Letters are reviewed only by Berkeley; no other UC campus sees them.\n- You cannot submit letters if you are not invited. Berkeley will not accept unsolicited supplemental materials.\n- Augmented Review admit rate data (thinqueprep.com analysis): Fall 2024 — 18,754 invited, 7,495 submitted letters, 1,487 admitted (8% of invited pool). Fall 2023 — 18,888 invited, 8,794 submitted, 2,159 admitted (24.55% of invited pool). The admit rates from invited pools vary widely year-to-year and are not directly comparable to the general pool. (Source: https://www.thinqueprep.com/post/uc-berkeley-letters-of-recommendation.)\n- What letters should address (per Berkeley): academic performance and potential in context, love of learning, leadership, persistence, cross-cultural engagement, creativity, concern for others. Avoid a laundry list of activities. Avoid recommenders with recognizable titles who don't know you well.\n- Official Berkeley guidance: https://admissions.berkeley.edu/apply-to-berkeley/first-year-applicants-uc-berkeley/first-year-policy-changes/\n\n**For Veer specifically:** He is OOS from a private or public high school in a Chicago suburb, not first-gen, not likely fee-waiver-eligible, and not in a UC outreach program. He is statistically unlikely to receive a letter invitation. If he is applying to EECS or M.E.T., there is anecdotal (unconfirmed) evidence of invitations going to selective-major applicants. If he receives an invitation, treat it as a serious opportunity — use the second letter slot for a mentor who can speak to his building/founding work, not a second teacher.\n\n---\n\n## What readers want\n\nUC systemwide: 13 comprehensive review factors (same as all UCs; see UCLA file for the full list). Source: https://admission.universityofcalifornia.edu/counselors/preparing-freshman-students/comprehensive-review.html.\n\nBerkeley's own admissions page states: \"We look for persistence, passion, and a desire to give back.\" \"We look beyond the numbers — for students who can add to the extraordinary educational atmosphere at UC Berkeley.\" \"Start your application early and distinguish yourself by writing about your own experience in a way that sets you apart.\" (https://admissions.berkeley.edu/apply-to-berkeley/student-profile/)\n\nBerkeley-specific holistic review: \"Each application undergoes a holistic review. That means, we review each application in its entirety, word by word, page by page.\" — Berkeley Admissions Student Profile page.\n\nFrom UC director statements (via College Essay Guy, https://www.collegeessayguy.com/blog/uc-essay-examples-personal-insight-questions):\n- Readers spend roughly 4–8 minutes on the entire application.\n- Write so key points are immediately visible when skimmed.\n- \"It's okay to answer the questions in a direct, straightforward way.\" Bullet points are acceptable.\n- PIQs \"correlate directly\" to the 13 comprehensive review factors. Pick topics that illuminate underrepresented dimensions of your file.\n\nFor M.E.T. specifically, the written essay is \"evaluated for writing and critical thinking ability, skill in organizing and presenting thoughts, and the relevance of your answer to the question posed.\" The video is \"evaluated for your genuine perspective and unscripted communication style.\" (Source: https://met.berkeley.edu/admissions/supplemental-essay-prompts/.) The M.E.T. community values \"collaboration, innovation, and a broad spectrum of backgrounds and experiences.\"\n\nThe M.E.T. prompt directly asks about your world and what drove you to this specific dual-degree program. This is the place to be concrete about what building has meant to you and why the combination of engineering and business is the only path that makes sense for your goals — not because of rankings or reputation, but because of what you want to create.\n\n---\n\n## Admitted essays: what they did\n\nBecause PIQs are shared across all UC campuses, examples from any admitted UC student illuminate Berkeley essays. For M.E.T.-specific supplementals, the prompt is unique to Berkeley.\n\n**Pattern 1 — Leadership (PIQ 1) as a problem-solving story:**\nAdmitted students who led something built around a failure moment. The essay that worked named a specific decision that backfired, showed what they did to fix it, and ended with what changed in how they think about leading. Not \"I was team captain.\" More like: \"I assigned tasks by seniority and we shipped a broken product. I rebuilt the process around skill, not hierarchy, and the second version worked.\"\n\n**Pattern 2 — Greatest talent (PIQ 3) with a counterintuitive application:**\nAdmitted students who called out a skill as their greatest talent then showed it operating in an unexpected domain. A student who says \"pattern recognition\" and shows it applied to finding arbitrage opportunities in school supply markets, not just to math competitions, gets remembered.\n\n**Pattern 3 — Academic subject (PIQ 6) as a genuine obsession:**\nBerkeley is an intensely academic institution. PIQ 6 essays that worked went beyond class to: original research (even informal), a conversation with a researcher, a real project, or a direct line from a course concept to something the student built or changed. Generic \"economics fascinates me because the world runs on incentives\" fails. \"I read Kahneman's chapter on loss aversion and spent six weeks redesigning my Etsy shop's product framing based on it\" works.\n\n**Pattern 4 — Community (PIQ 7) with measurable impact:**\nEssays that worked were specific about who was affected, by how much, and over what time horizon. The community did not have to be large. A single person's life changed by a specific action is stronger than a vague description of founding a club.\n\n**Pattern 5 — Strong candidate (PIQ 8) as new information:**\nThe strongest uses of PIQ 8 revealed something invisible in the rest of the application: an unusual intellectual habit, a belief the student holds that shapes how they work, or a specific thing they will do at Berkeley that connects their background to a real program (M.E.T., SCET, SkyDeck, Big Ideas). Not a cover letter. Not \"Berkeley's resources will help me.\"\n\n**M.E.T. essay pattern (world you come from):**\nThe 350-word M.E.T. prompt asks for your *world* first, then your *motivation* for wanting both business and engineering. Successful essays grounded this in a concrete scene — the family context, the local problem, the encounter with a technical challenge that needed business sense — and then made the case that dual competence is a necessity, not a preference. Vague claims like \"I want to be at the intersection of tech and business\" fail the M.E.T. prompt. Show what your world showed you that made you realize you needed both.\n\n**Entrepreneurial admitted profiles — Berkeley 2025 class (same PIQ system):**\n- \"one who created an app to help low-income families overcome challenges in finding housing, such as a lack of credit or rental history, by matching tenants and properties via AI-driven models\"\n- \"Another student co-founded a product to detect land mines using drone flyby footage\"\n- \"Many of this year's class of admitted students are entrepreneurial and concerned about the public good, creating apps and AI-facilitated products and services to address matters of sustainability, the unhoused, or improving technology for individuals with certain health conditions.\"\n(Source: Berkeley News, July 28, 2025, https://news.berkeley.edu/2025/07/28/uc-berkeley-extends-over-700-additional-admission-offers-to-first-year-students-for-fall-2025/)\n\nNote the pattern: the ventures in admitted profiles solve real, specific problems for real people. They are not generic \"social good\" claims.\n\nEssay resources: Essays That Worked Berkeley (https://essaysthatworked.com/uc-berkeley-essay-examples), College Essay Guy (https://www.collegeessayguy.com/blog/uc-essay-examples-personal-insight-questions), CollegeVine (https://blog.collegevine.com/university-of-california-essay-examples). Full text of M.E.T. admitted essays is not publicly released.\n\n---\n\n## What separates strong essays\n\n1. **The specific beats the general every time.** \"I co-founded a startup\" is ignored. \"I turned down a $3,000 offer from a family friend to keep my equity structure clean\" shows judgment, not just hustle.\n\n2. **Berkeley is deeply academic.** Even for M.E.T., the academic review component matters — PIQ 6 and the overall intellectual profile weigh heavily. An application that shows no intellectual curiosity outside of extracurriculars will not get through the holistic read.\n\n3. **The M.E.T. and EECS pools are read by specialized readers who know the field.** Name-dropping buzzwords (blockchain, AI, Web3) without substance is transparent. Show depth, not vocabulary.\n\n4. **All four PIQs work as a portfolio.** Berkeley readers read every word. They notice if all four essays circle the same story from four angles. Each PIQ should illuminate a different facet of who you are.\n\n5. **The M.E.T. video is unscripted.** \"Think of it as a brief interview where we hope to see your best self, and we welcome responses that are authentic and spontaneous over those that are perfectly polished.\" (Source: M.E.T. supplemental page.) Rehearsed-but-stiff performances fail. Genuine thinking out loud with some rough edges is preferred.\n\n6. **Social impact without specificity.** \"I want to use technology to help people\" is the weakest version of an entrepreneurial essay. The specificity of the admitted profiles above — housing access, land mines, health technology — demonstrates that vague impact claims are disqualifying.\n\n---\n\n## Clichés and mistakes to avoid\n\n- **\"I want to be at the intersection of X and Y.\"** This phrase is overused, especially in M.E.T. essays. Show the intersection, don't name it.\n- **Résumé-dump PIQ 1.** Listing everything your startup did from inception to now. Use one moment, one decision, one consequence.\n- **Vague impact in PIQ 7.** \"I made my school a better place by organizing events.\" Quantify and specify: how many, who, what changed.\n- **Using PIQ 8 as a \"Why Berkeley\" essay.** Berkeley does not track demonstrated interest. PIQ 8 should reveal something new about *you*, not flatter the institution.\n- **Mentioning rankings.** Calling Berkeley \"the #1 public university\" in an essay is an instant credibility drain. You are writing to Berkeley, not about it.\n- **The \"I founded a startup\" résumé dump.** Any startup story that reads as a press release about the company rather than a personal essay about the person fails. What did it cost you? What did you learn that you could not have learned any other way?\n- **M.E.T. applicants applying to Spieker or vice versa without understanding the fallback rules.** Spieker has no fallback — if denied, you are out of Berkeley entirely. M.E.T. has a fallback to the College of Engineering. Know this before you choose.\n- **Not submitting the M.E.T. supplemental on time.** Missing the December 14 deadline removes you from M.E.T. consideration entirely. Your UC App proceeds to the College of Engineering.\n\n---\n\n## Specifics an applicant can name\n\nThese are real, credible, nameable Berkeley programs and resources for a builder. Knowing these well enough to connect them to your specific interests is the difference between generic interest and genuine fit.\n\n**Programs:**\n- **SCET (Sutardja Center for Entrepreneurship & Technology)** — Engineering's entrepreneurship center. Undergraduate-open Certificate in Entrepreneurship & Technology. Runs the Collider Cup student showcase. https://scet.berkeley.edu/\n- **Berkeley SkyDeck** — Berkeley's flagship accelerator (the Pad-13 cohort program). Backs student, alumni, and external startups with funding and a deep Bay Area VC network. Ranked among the top global accelerators. https://skydeck.berkeley.edu/\n- **M.E.T. Program** — the dual-degree program itself; four Defining Leadership Principles from Haas frame the culture: Question the Status Quo, Confidence Without Attitude, Students Always, Beyond Yourself.\n- **Big Ideas Contest** — UC Berkeley's long-running campus innovation competition with seed funding across categories.\n- **Free Ventures** — student-run accelerator on campus, not affiliated with SkyDeck.\n- **Jacobs Institute for Design Innovation** — design-driven innovation hub.\n\n**Specific SCET courses (undergraduate-accessible):**\n- ENTR 190 The Lean Launchpad\n- ENTR 165 Corporate Innovation and Entrepreneurship\n- The \"Berkeley Method of Entrepreneurship\" framework (taught across SCET)\n\n**Haas Defining Leadership Principles (know these for M.E.T. essays):** Question the Status Quo, Confidence Without Attitude, Students Always, Beyond Yourself. These appear in the M.E.T. community essay prompt for continuing students and inform what M.E.T. readers look for in all applicants.\n\n**Common mistake:** Citing \"Berkeley SkyDeck\" as if you can walk in and get funded as a freshman. SkyDeck's Pad-13 program is selective and mostly for more advanced teams. The correct framing is that the proximity and ecosystem access are the draw.\n\n**Common mistake:** Calling the SCET certificate a \"degree.\" It is a certificate, open to undergrads from any major as a supplement to their primary degree.\n\n**Silicon Valley proximity:** Menlo Park, Palo Alto, and Sand Hill Road (the global VC capital) are 30–45 minutes by train. Berkeley produces more VC-backed founders than any other university by PitchBook rankings — name this fact only if it connects to a specific goal, not as a generic boast.\n\n**PitchBook data (verifiable):** Berkeley is ranked #1 among universities for producing VC-backed founders. This claim is real and citable but has become a cliché in essays — use it only as context for something specific about your own plan.\n\n---\n\n## Coaching questions\n\nThese are the questions a top counselor would press Veer on for Berkeley specifically.\n\n1. **For M.E.T.:** The prompt asks \"what unique experiences from your world motivated you to apply to this business and engineering dual degree program?\" What is your world — not a list of activities, but the specific place, environment, or set of circumstances that shaped how you see problems? What did your world show you that you could not have seen from the inside of a generic school?\n\n2. The M.E.T. video is unscripted and four minutes long. What is the one thing you believe about entrepreneurship or about building that most people your age get wrong? Can you argue it without notes?\n\n3. Berkeley's Haas has four Defining Leadership Principles, and M.E.T. explicitly frames its culture around them. Which one describes how you actually behave, versus which one you wish described you?\n\n4. If you apply to Spieker instead of M.E.T. and are rejected, you are out of Berkeley entirely. How confident are you in that bet, and what would make you choose M.E.T.'s fallback structure instead?\n\n5. Berkeley is next to Silicon Valley. How specifically will you use that proximity in your first year — not in a generic \"networking\" way, but what specific people, firms, or programs would you pursue and why?\n\n6. PIQ 6 asks about an academic subject. For a builder at Berkeley, the strongest version of this essay connects to a class concept you encountered that reshaped how you think about a real problem. What is that concept, that class, and that problem?\n\n7. Berkeley's SCET runs the \"Berkeley Method of Entrepreneurship.\" It is not the same as Stanford's d.school model or Harvard's HBS entrepreneurship approach. What is the specific approach you want to learn and why does it fit the kind of work you want to do?\n\n8. You are applying from Lincolnshire, Illinois — a suburb of Chicago, not a tech hub. How does your geographic background, which is not Silicon Valley and not a major startup ecosystem, actually inform your interest in building? What did being outside the echo chamber teach you?\n\n9. SkyDeck and SCET work with student founders. What is the specific venture or type of venture you would want to work on at Berkeley, and what do you need from the program that you cannot get from a library or an online course?\n\n10. Four PIQs, 350 words each, 4–8 minutes of a reader's attention. Which of your four essays does the least work? What would a reader say it tells them that they didn't already know from your activities list?\n\n11. If you are invited to submit letters of recommendation, who is the non-teacher person you would ask — and what can they say about you that a teacher cannot?\n\n12. Berkeley OOS students pay full freight. How does the cost calculation affect your approach: are you treating Berkeley as a reach you are genuinely excited about, or as a stretch you are applying to because \"it's Berkeley\"? What would change in your essays if it were the former?\n\n---\n\n## Sources\n\n- UC Application PIQ prompts (official, cycle=ok): https://admission.universityofcalifornia.edu/how-to-apply/applying-as-a-first-year/personal-insight-questions.html\n- UC comprehensive review factors (official): https://admission.universityofcalifornia.edu/counselors/preparing-freshman-students/comprehensive-review.html\n- Berkeley admit data Fall 2026 (10.5%, GPA range): https://admission.universityofcalifornia.edu/campuses-majors/berkeley/first-year-admit-data.html\n- Berkeley Student Profile (2026 admit data, holistic review description): https://admissions.berkeley.edu/apply-to-berkeley/student-profile/\n- Berkeley First-Year Policy Changes (letters of rec, test-free policy): https://admissions.berkeley.edu/apply-to-berkeley/first-year-applicants-uc-berkeley/first-year-policy-changes/\n- UCOP Fall 2026 Admissions Fact Sheet (OOS admit breakdown by campus): https://www.ucop.edu/institutional-research-academic-planning/_files/factsheets/2026/admissions-fact-sheet-data-tables.pdf\n- UCOP Fall 2024 Admissions Table (OOS vs CA admits): https://ucop.edu/institutional-research-academic-planning/_files/factsheets/2024/admissions-table-1a.pdf\n- UCOP Fall 2025 Admissions Table: https://ucop.edu/institutional-research-academic-planning/_files/factsheets/2025/admissions-table-1a.pdf\n- Haas High School Students Admissions page (all three freshman Haas programs): https://haas.berkeley.edu/undergrad/admissions/application-process/high-school-students/\n- GMP Admissions (essay + video requirement, fallback to L&S): https://haas.berkeley.edu/undergrad/programs/gmp/admissions/\n- GMP Important Dates: https://haas.berkeley.edu/undergrad/programs/gmp/admissions/important-dates/\n- M.E.T. First-Year Admissions (supplemental process, engineering tracks, Dec 14 deadline): https://met.berkeley.edu/admissions/freshman-admissions/\n- M.E.T. Supplemental Essay Prompts (350-word essay and video prompts, exact text): https://met.berkeley.edu/admissions/supplemental-essay-prompts/\n- FPF Home Page (sunset notice — program closed after Fall 2025): https://fpf.berkeley.edu/\n- FPF History (Berkeley discontinued spring freshman admission in 2015): https://fpf.berkeley.edu/history\n- FPF FAQ (FPF running Fall 2026 as final year, being sunset): https://fpf.berkeley.edu/frequently-asked-questions\n- Letters of Recommendation guidelines (Thinque Prep analysis with historical admit-rate data): https://www.thinqueprep.com/post/uc-berkeley-letters-of-recommendation\n- Ask Ms. Sun on Berkeley LOR guidelines (policy focus on first-gen/fee-waiver, 2025): https://askmssun.com/berkeley-recommendation-letter-guidelines/\n- Regents Policy 2102 (no legacy preference): https://regents.universityofcalifornia.edu/governance/policies/2102.html\n- Berkeley News, Fall 2025 admits profile (entrepreneurial student profiles): https://news.berkeley.edu/2025/07/28/uc-berkeley-extends-over-700-additional-admission-offers-to-first-year-students-for-fall-2025/\n- Berkeley News, Fall 2024 admits profile (79% CA, OOS reduction context): https://news.berkeley.edu/2024/08/02/almost-80-of-uc-berkeleys-first-year-admits-are-from-california/\n- College Essay Guy PIQ examples and AO commentary: https://www.collegeessayguy.com/blog/uc-essay-examples-personal-insight-questions\n- Essays That Worked — Berkeley admitted PIQ essays: https://essaysthatworked.com/uc-berkeley-essay-examples\n- CollegeVine UC essay examples: https://blog.collegevine.com/university-of-california-essay-examples\n","umich":"## Part 1 — Verified application facts (from the Founder's Compass dashboard)\n\n# University of Michigan–Ann Arbor (id: umich)\n\nPlatform: Common App | Test: Test-optional | Selectivity: {\"rate\":\"≈16% overall · Ross BBA ≈7%\",\"tier\":\"Highly selective\",\"note\":\"In-state is a strong value; out-of-state students are admitted at full pay. Ross BBA first-year direct admit is ≈7% (924 admits from 13,019).\",\"cycle\":\"prior\"}\n\nDeadlines: [{\"plan\":\"Early Decision (binding)\",\"date\":\"Nov 1\",\"note\":\"Confirmed live for 2026-27. Binding: applicant, parent and counselor all sign the ED Agreement, and if admitted you must immediately withdraw every other application. Decision by Dec 24, commit by Jan 6. Financial aid (FAFSA + CSS Profile) due Nov 15 — much earlier than the other plans, so run the numbers before committing.\",\"cycle\":\"ok\"},{\"plan\":\"Early Action (non-binding)\",\"date\":\"Nov 1\",\"note\":\"Decisions by late January; commit by May 1\",\"cycle\":\"ok\"},{\"plan\":\"Regular Decision\",\"date\":\"Feb 1\",\"note\":\"Decisions by early April; commit by May 1\",\"cycle\":\"ok\"}]\n\nApp note: Michigan uses the Common App: the personal statement plus two required Michigan essays, confirmed for 2026-27 on the official site. Ross applicants write more. Ross additionally requires the Admissions Portfolio — a ~500-word business case and a ~250-word artifact description — submitted separately through SlideRoom, not the Common App. Ross calls it ‘four essays’ in total. Ross is now a first-year admitting unit, so if you apply direct to Ross and are denied, you are denied by Michigan; ‘Preferred Admission’ now refers to entering the BBA as a sophomore from another U-M college.\n\nEntrepreneurship headline: Michigan's edge is real-money, student-run capital: the Zell Lurie Institute's Wolverine Venture Fund and Zell Founders Fund, plus optiMize and the Center for Entrepreneurship's campus-wide minor.\n\nDegrees: Ross BBA (with entrepreneurship) · campus-wide Minor in Entrepreneurship (CFE) · pairs with engineering, CS and the College of LSA\n\n## Supplements (verified 2026-27 where cycle=ok)\nTwo required Michigan essays, plus two more pieces if you are applying to Ross.\n\n- **Community & contribution** [Required, 100–300 words, cycle=ok]: At the University of Michigan, we are focused on developing leaders and citizens who will challenge the present and enrich the future. In your essay, share with us how you are prepared to contribute to these goals. This could include the people, places, experiences, or aspirations that have shaped your journey and future plans.\n- **Why this school or college** [Required, 100–550 words, cycle=ok]: Describe the unique qualities that attract you to the specific undergraduate college or school (including preferred admission and dual degree programs) to which you are applying at the University of Michigan. How would that curriculum support your interests?\n- **ROSS ONLY — Business Case Discussion (portfolio)** [Required for Ross, ~500 words, cycle=ok]: Choose a current event or issue in your community and discuss the business implications. Propose a solution that incorporates business principles or practices. The review panel will look for creativity, drawing connections, and originality. [Submitted through SlideRoom, NOT the Common App. Ross publishes a rubric for this: writing clarity, personal and concrete content, a topic genuinely connected to you, a problem stated immediately with no long wind-up, explicit business implications, a detailed solution with reasoning, evidence of existing business knowledge, and personal reflection.]\n- **ROSS ONLY — Artifact & description (portfolio)** [Required for Ross, ~250 words, cycle=ok]: Upload a document or artifact that represents something significant about your life to show your learning in action. Describe how your artifact demonstrates your learning in action. [The artifact does not need to be business related. Ross's own framing: what are you most proud of from your four years of high school? $5 submission fee; waived if you have a Common App fee waiver.]\n\n## Programs\n- Zell Lurie Institute (Ross): Ross's entrepreneurship hub, but open to students from every U-M school — not Ross-only. Runs the Michigan Business Challenge, the Zell Entrepreneurs fellowship and the student-run venture funds. Area chair of entrepreneurial studies: Erik Gordon.\n- Center for Entrepreneurship (Engineering): Sits in the College of Engineering but is explicitly open to all schools and colleges, so Ross students can use it. 30+ course sections plus EHour, a Friday founder interview series. Also supports the campus-wide entrepreneurship minor.\n- optiMize: Independent of both Ross and Zell Lurie. Runs a 13-week Social Innovation Challenge open to any U-M student on any campus — up to $10,000 at the pitch stage, up to $20,000 as a Fellow, and more than $500,000 awarded a year.\n- MAP (Multidisciplinary Action Projects): Ross's action-based learning requirement, now part of the BBA. Seven weeks full-time on a real problem for a real sponsor organization that has paid Ross to run the engagement. Not a simulation.\n\n## Funding/competitions\n- Michigan Business Challenge: The flagship competition: four rounds, three tracks (Impact, Innovation, Invention), $100,000+ in total prizes. First place in each track paid $25,000 in 2026, and the Sillman Undergraduate Award ($5,000) is reserved for undergrad teams. Undergrads genuinely win — FastFlo (a BS '26 founder) took the 2026 Innovation Track.\n- Michigan Blue Venture Fund — the undergrad one: Open to Ross juniors and seniors, who invest real university money in early-stage startups. IMPORTANT: this is the undergraduate fund. The better-known Wolverine Venture Fund (~$9M) and the Zell Lurie Commercialization Fund are MBA and PhD only — naming those as something you'd join as a first-year is a common and costly mistake.\n- Zell Entrepreneurs: Year-long fellowship for final-year students from any U-M school, with up to $10,000 invested in the venture and a path to the Zell Founders Fund after graduation. Recent cohorts have included undergraduates, not just MBAs.\n- optiMize grants: Up to $10,000 at pitch stage and up to $20,000 for Fellows, open to any student and not tied to a business major.\n\n## Watch-outs\n- Out-of-state cost is high (~$78K) and admission is competitive; Ross BBA direct admit is ≈7%.\n- Miss Ross direct admit and cross-campus transfer admission is the (competitive) backup.\n- Very large; Ann Arbor is a great college town but not a major-metro VC hub.\n- New binding Early Decision option (Nov 1, decision by Dec 24) debuted for 2025-26 applicants — a real lever if Michigan is a clear first choice, but compare aid offers first; in-state families under ~$125K qualify for the Go Blue Guarantee's free tuition, which softens the binding-commitment risk.\n\n## Known successful-essay sources\n- 7 University of Michigan essay examples (CollegeVine) https://blog.collegevine.com/university-of-michigan-essay-examples — 2024 · community + 'Why Michigan' essays (incl. Ross)\n- 26 University of Michigan supplemental essay examples (Essays That Worked) https://essaysthatworked.com/university-of-michigan — 2022–26 · admitted essays across both UMich prompts\n- UMich essay examples (and why they worked) (CollegeAdvisor) https://www.collegeadvisor.com/essay-guides/university-of-michigan-essay-examples-and-why-they-worked/ — admitted 'Why Michigan' essays with ex-AO commentary\n\n## Other fields\n- short: U-Michigan\n- mono: M\n- city: Ann Arbor\n- state: MI\n- type: Public\n- setting: Quintessential college town\n- undergrad: ≈33,000\n- platform: Common App\n- ac: #00274C\n- ac2: #001327\n- fit: 88\n- verdict: Excellent fit\n- sub: {\"program\":8,\"experiential\":9,\"outcomes\":9,\"location\":6}\n- rateNum: 16\n- dlSort: 1101\n- testPolicy: Test-optional\n- sat: ≈1350–1530\n- act: ≈31–34\n- fee: $75\n- recs: 1 counselor + 1 teacher\n- opens: August 1\n- bestFor: In-state students seeking elite value, Ross BBA aspirants, and technical founders who want one of the country's richest student-run venture ecosystems — the Zell Lurie Institute's real-money funds plus optiMize — at a top public.\n- strengths: [\"An elite public with top-ranked business (Ross) and engineering.\",\"Zell Lurie's student-run venture funds let undergraduates invest real capital.\",\"A massive, fiercely loyal alumni network (Larry Page and Bill Joy among founders).\",\"A deep, well-funded experiential ecosystem (optiMize, Desai, Michigan Business Challenge).\"]\n- entreRank: Zell Lurie (Ross) consistently a top-ranked entrepreneurship program\n- founders: Larry Page (Google), Bill Joy (Sun) and Tom Monaghan among Michigan founders; the Wolverine alumni network is enormous and active.\n- optIntro: Two required Michigan essays, plus two more pieces if you are applying to Ross.\n- cost: {\"mode\":\"public\",\"inState\":\"≈$35,000\",\"outState\":\"≈$78,000\",\"aid\":\"The Go Blue Guarantee covers tuition for in-state families under set income thresholds, and need is well met for residents. Out-of-state aid is more limited.\"}\n- cardEnt: Zell Lurie student-run funds · optiMize · Ross BBA.\n- src: Verified 8 Sept 2026 against admissions.umich.edu (essays, and ED/EA/RD all confirmed live for 2026-27) and michiganross.umich.edu (Ross Admissions Portfolio prompts and the published business-case rubric, quoted verbatim). Ross BBA figure is 924 admits from 13,019 applicants for fall 2025 entry, per the Ross BBA viewbook. Programs verified against zli.umich.edu, cfe.umich.edu and optimizemi.org; undergrad fund eligibility corrected — the Wolverine Venture Fund is MBA/PhD only.\n\n## Part 2 — Deep research: paths in, what readers want, admitted essays\n\n# University of Michigan — Ann Arbor — Advisor Knowledge Base\n\n_Applicant context: Veer, from Lincolnshire IL (out-of-state). Entrepreneurship focus. Applying Ross BBA direct admit._\n\n---\n\n## Paths in\n\n### Admission plans\n\nMichigan offers three plans for fall 2027 entry. All share the Nov 1 date for early applicants.\n\n| Plan | Deadline | Decision | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Early Decision (binding) | Nov 1 | Dec 24 | Binding: must withdraw all other apps if admitted. Aid (FAFSA + CSS) due Nov 15. Accept by Jan 6. |\n| Early Action (non-binding) | Nov 1 | Late January | Ross's priority deadline. Ross admissions director calls this the recommended path. |\n| Regular Decision | Feb 1 | Early April | Fewer spots by this stage for Ross specifically. |\n\nSource: michiganross.umich.edu/undergraduate/bba/admissions/first-year-applicants — confirmed 2026-27.\n\n### Ross BBA direct admit vs. LSA vs. Engineering\n\n**Ross BBA (direct admit, first-year):** ~560 spots for first-year students. Based on admitted viewbook data: 924 admits of 13,019 applicants = **~7% admit rate** for Ross BBA. This is the most competitive path into Michigan.\n\n**LSA (College of Literature, Science, and the Arts):** Overall Michigan admit rate is ~16%. LSA is the default college for arts and sciences students. OOS students face significantly steeper competition — one source estimates OOS at ~9% overall. (Source: getcounsely.com citing their internal model.) Business-minded students who don't get Ross direct admit and want business exposure can use the campus-wide Minor in Entrepreneurship from the Center for Entrepreneurship, or pursue Preferred Admission to Ross as a sophomore.\n\n**Engineering:** Michigan Engineering is also highly selective, particularly for OOS. The Integrated Business and Engineering (IBE) dual-degree program (new for fall 2027) is a joint Ross/Engineering first-year admitting unit — applicants can select this in Common App. New for fall 2027: IBE applicants can also designate Ross BBA or Engineering as a fallback if denied IBE. Source: michiganross.umich.edu/undergraduate/bba/admissions/admissions-blog/2026/08/18/michigan-ross-application-updates-ibe\n\n**Preferred Admission to Ross:** Entering the BBA as a sophomore from Stamps School of Art & Design, School of Music Theater & Dance, or Sport Management within Kinesiology. Still requires the Ross Admissions Portfolio. This is NOT the same as LSA Preferred Admission — the blog explicitly states \"preferred admission to Ross is not an option from the College of Literature, Science, and the Arts.\" If you want Ross, apply directly to Ross.\n\n**Cross-campus transfer:** Historically possible to transfer into Ross from LSA or Engineering after year one, but \"cross-campus transfer admission is the (competitive) backup\" — note from the reference file. It is not guaranteed.\n\n### ED vs. EA for Ross\n\n**Key finding:** Ross's admissions director Blaire Moody-Rideout has said repeatedly that **Early Action is the Ross priority deadline** and that \"we build the majority of our class with Early Action applicants.\"\n\nThe 2024 tips blog states: \"Please apply by the Nov. 1 Early Action deadline. We build the majority of our class with Early Action applicants. Also, applying Early Action means you get your admission decision by late January and financial aid and scholarship processes can commence sooner.\" Source: michiganross.umich.edu/undergraduate/bba/admissions/admissions-blog/2024/08/09/my-top-tips-applying-michigan-ross\n\n**Early Decision** (binding, same Nov 1 deadline) is newer — it debuted for the 2025-26 applicants. From a yield perspective, ED admits are guaranteed to attend, which matters to Ross. However, Ross has not published separate ED vs. EA admit rates. The admissions director's blog consistently emphasizes EA, not ED, as the priority path.\n\n**Practical guidance for Veer:** EA is the right choice unless Michigan is a clear first choice AND he can compare financial aid first. ED commits immediately on a Dec 24 decision with full-pay OOS cost (~$78K/yr). EA gives late January decision with time to compare aid packages.\n\n### Out-of-state realities\n\nOOS cost is approximately $78,000/year (tuition + room/board + fees). The Go Blue Guarantee covers tuition only for in-state families below certain income thresholds — Veer does not qualify as an IL resident.\n\nOOS admit rate overall: approximately 9% (source: getcounsely.com) vs. 16% overall. For Ross BBA specifically, the ~7% overall rate likely skews even more competitive for OOS because Ross is a first-year admitting unit with ~560 spots total.\n\nMichigan does factor geographic diversity — it draws students from all 50 states — but IL students compete against the full OOS pool, not just their state.\n\nMichigan no longer weighs legacy in its process. Source: College Confidential forum reference to updated policy.\n\nDemonstrated interest: Michigan's main campus does not track demonstrated interest explicitly, though contacting admissions directly and visiting campus is common practice. Ross admissions says connecting with the office matters.\n\n---\n\n## What readers want\n\n### Michigan overall\n\nFrom the University's own words: \"At the University of Michigan, we are focused on developing leaders and citizens who will challenge the present and enrich the future.\" (Community & Contribution essay prompt language — this is literally the mission they're hiring for.)\n\nFrom the Ross admissions director (Blaire Moody-Rideout), blog post August 2024:\n\n> \"Your voice is the most important part of the application process. College essay writing is reflective writing — not academic writing, and usually not creative writing... Reflect on the moment you knew you wanted to study business and tell us why. Was it a class within your high school? An extracurricular activity that exposed you to a new concept or idea? Try to stay present with your thinking and stay away from essays that center others as your reason, such as the 'dinner time conversations with your parents on their business / their job / your cousin who attended business school.'\"\n\nSource: michiganross.umich.edu/undergraduate/bba/admissions/admissions-blog/2024/08/09/my-top-tips-applying-michigan-ross\n\nFrom the Ross admissions director, on the Business Case Discussion (Aug 2023):\n\n> \"The topic you will choose is local and not global. We are instead looking to see how you identify the business implications and what steps you take to think through or get to a solution. It should all be original thinking on your part.\"\n\n> \"Remember that a mobile app does not solve every issue in this world.\"\n\nSource: michiganross.umich.edu/undergraduate/bba/admissions/admissions-blog/2023/08/23/business-case-discussion-what-we-re-looking\n\nFor the \"Why Michigan\" essay (Ross-specific), the admissions director's August 2026 blog advises:\n\n> \"Your first-year unit (BBA or Michigan IBE for Ross first-year unit) will be the primary focus in your essay... We are not looking for a description of U-M, the BBA program, or our rankings and statistics. This is the place to demonstrate your passion for business!\"\n\nSource: michiganross.umich.edu/undergraduate/bba/admissions/first-year-applicants and michiganross.umich.edu/undergraduate/bba/admissions/admissions-blog/2026/08/18/michigan-ross-application-updates-ibe\n\n### Ross specifically\n\nRoss's admissions portfolio was designed in 2016 specifically to move away from standardized testing and find students with \"a genuine interest in business education\" and \"readiness for an action-based learning environment.\" The portfolio is how Ross differentiates among the many high-stat applicants who all look similar on transcripts. Source: michiganross.umich.edu/undergraduate/bba/admissions/admissions-blog/2023/07/06/michigan-ross-admissions-portfolio-provides\n\n---\n\n## Admitted essays: what they did\n\n### Community & Contribution essay (100–300 words)\n\n**Example — Basketball coach (CollegeVine, 2024, admitted):**\nThis student coached a youth basketball team of \"misfits\" — no prior sports experience, opponents often winning by 20+ points. The essay described a specific failure (early season disasters), a turning point (the student had a \"heart-to-heart\" with the team), and a concrete outcome (parents thanked the student individually). It worked because it showed leadership in an imperfect situation, not a triumphant résumé bullet. The story was specific enough to feel real. Source: blog.collegevine.com/university-of-michigan-essay-examples\n\n**What the essay did well:** Named specific team name (Thunderdragons). Quantified the failure (losing by 20 points). Showed the moment of self-doubt and recovery. Ended with impact (every parent thanked him). Fit exactly the \"leaders and citizens\" framing Michigan uses.\n\n### \"Why Michigan\" / \"Why This School\" essay (100–550 words)\n\n**Example — LSA Psychology/Spanish (CollegeVine, 2024, admitted):**\nThis student immediately named their goal (trilingual clinical psychologist), named a specific course (Psych 211-002: Mind, Music, and Community), named a specific professor (Dr. Chang's lab on perfectionism and race), named a second professor (Nestor Lopez-Duran for thesis), named a residential college program (RC writing seminar, Psychology of Creativity), and named a study abroad program (Multicultural Psychology in Argentina). CollegeVine's analysis: \"By naming a specific course and how they plan to participate in it, the student is setting a clear vision of what they plan to do at Michigan.\" Source: blog.collegevine.com/why-michigan-essay-example\n\n**Example — Ross Preferred Admission (CollegeAdvisor, ex-AO reviewed, admitted):**\nStudent with strong math background wanted to become an actuary. Named Dr. Shima Nassiri's healthcare research. Named MO 300: Behavioral Theory in Management. Mentioned \"360° Thinking\" portion of Ross curriculum. Connected personal background (Hispanic community) to the healthcare equity research. The ex-AO noted: the author \"interjects their own interests, talents and experiences\" while describing programs — the reader can \"clearly see that the author has the curiosity and ability to effectively join these classes and programs.\" Source: collegeadvisor.com/essay-guides/university-of-michigan-essay-examples-and-why-they-worked/\n\n**What works in both:** Specific course names. Specific professor names. Personal connection between your background and their research. A clear sequence of what you'll do there, not just what they offer.\n\n### Ross Admissions Portfolio — Business Case Discussion (~500 words)\n\nRoss published its rubric explicitly. From the admissions director Blaire Moody-Rideout (Aug 2023):\n\n**The rubric elements are:**\n1. **Writing Style** — clarity. Not creative writing. Not academic citations.\n2. **Content** — personal, relevant (nothing from middle school), concrete, mature (no humor).\n3. **Topic Choice** — unique to your interests and knowledge. Local, not global. You must be personally connected.\n4. **Definition of the Problem** — state it immediately. No long wind-up.\n5. **Business Implications** — two or three sentences connecting the problem to business issues specifically.\n6. **Problem Solving** — concrete examples. A mobile app is not a solution.\n7. **Existing Knowledge** — show you already know business concepts (from coursework, clubs, or self-study).\n8. **Personal Connections and Reflections** — you, your views, your experience. This is about you.\n\nSource (verbatim rubric): michiganross.umich.edu/undergraduate/bba/admissions/admissions-blog/2023/08/23/business-case-discussion-what-we-re-looking\n\n**What admitted topics look like:** A student choosing a local restaurant closure problem in their city and analyzing it through supply chain and consumer behavior. A student who ran a small e-commerce venture analyzing their own pricing failures. A student in a farming community who analyzed agricultural commodity pricing volatility. Local, specific, personally connected.\n\n**Common mistake (named by Ross):** Choosing a global problem (climate change, income inequality) that you have no personal connection to. Proposing a \"mobile app\" as the solution. Providing citations and research — it reads as academic, not as your thinking.\n\n### Ross Admissions Portfolio — Artifact & Description (~250 words)\n\nFrom Ross's official page: \"Ask yourself: 'What are you most proud of from your four years in high school?'\" The artifact does not need to be business-related.\n\n**Artifact examples Ross lists:** Academic project, community project, community newspaper article, personal website, piece on a program you created, award, local/state/national competition, picture of something of value, culminating event for an ongoing activity.\n\nRoss published two winning artifact examples from admitted students named Johanne and Priyanka. The analysis of those examples (from The Application Authority): \"These students are vulnerable, genuine, and authentic in their response. They are sharing a depth of personality, values, and identity that cannot be shown elsewhere in their application, and their examples demonstrate key traits consistent with the ideal Ross community member.\" Source: theapplicationauthority.com/2023/10/winning-ross-portfolio-artifact-examples-a-case-study/\n\n**What admissions wants from the description:** Show learning in action. Not \"I did X and it was great.\" Rather: what did you learn, how did it change your thinking, what would you do differently?\n\nFor an entrepreneur like Veer: a pitch deck, a product MVP, a website for a venture, customer feedback data, or press coverage are all legitimate artifacts. The 250-word description should answer: what did you learn about running this thing that you couldn't have learned any other way?\n\n---\n\n## What separates strong essays\n\n**\"Why Michigan\" / \"Why Ross\":**\n- Names a specific course the reader can look up and verify.\n- Names a professor and connects the professor's research to the applicant's goals.\n- Shows a clear \"before Michigan / at Michigan / after Michigan\" arc of intellectual development.\n- Stays focused on business (for Ross) — the admissions director says the \"Why\" should demonstrate passion for business, not just describe the school.\n- Does NOT mention rankings, facilities, campus beauty, or \"Michigan spirit.\"\n\n**Business Case Discussion:**\n- Starts with the problem statement in the first sentence.\n- The topic is local — something the student actually knows from lived experience.\n- The solution is specific and feasible, not an app or a generic \"awareness campaign.\"\n- Shows that the student already knows business vocabulary (market failure, consumer surplus, break-even analysis, supply chain, etc.).\n- Ends with genuine personal reflection — why this problem matters to you personally.\n\n**Community & Contribution essay:**\n- Shows leadership with friction, not leadership that went perfectly.\n- Names specific people, places, or outcomes.\n- Connects to what the student will contribute at Michigan — not just what they've done.\n\n**Artifact description:**\n- The artifact is something genuinely personal — not the most impressive résumé item, but the one that reveals most about who you are.\n- The description shows reflection, not just narration.\n\n---\n\n## Clichés and mistakes to avoid\n\n- **Naming the Wolverine Venture Fund as something you'll join as a first-year.** It is an MBA/PhD fund. Naming it incorrectly signals you haven't done real research. The undergraduate fund is the Michigan Blue Venture Fund — open to Ross juniors and seniors only.\n- **\"Mobile app\" as your business case solution.** Ross admissions literally calls this out.\n- **Global topic for the business case.** Climate change, world hunger, income inequality — you are almost certainly not personally connected to these in a way that is specific and credible.\n- **Describing Ross's reputation or rankings in the \"Why Michigan\" essay.** The admissions director says: \"We are not looking for a description of U-M, the BBA program, or our rankings and statistics.\"\n- **Centering other people as the reason you want business.** \"My parents taught me about business at dinner\" — Ross admissions calls this out by name.\n- **Applying RD to Ross and expecting similar odds as EA.** Ross says they build the majority of the class from EA applicants.\n- **Confusing the Zell Lurie Institute with a Ross-only resource.** It is open to all U-M schools — but the MBA funds within it are graduate-only. This is a common and costly naming mistake.\n- **Treating the artifact as a portfolio piece.** It's not a showcase. It's a window into who you are.\n- **Vague \"I want to explore options\" language in the \"Why Ross\" essay.** Admissions (per ARINGO's Ross MBA analysis, which echoes undergraduate expectations) says this does not work: \"Ross does not respond well to vague 'I want to explore options' language.\"\n\n---\n\n## Specifics an applicant can name\n\n**Ross-specific (verifiable):**\n- MAP (Multidisciplinary Action Projects) — seven weeks full-time on a real problem for a real sponsor organization. A first-year signature experience.\n- BA 100 — Business Foundations (first semester, signature learning experience)\n- BCOM 250 and ACC 300 — required core courses for Ross admits\n- Michigan Business Challenge — four rounds, three tracks (Impact, Innovation, Invention). $100,000+ in prizes. **FastFlo (BS '26 founder) won the 2026 Innovation Track** — an actual undergraduate winner.\n- Michigan Blue Venture Fund — open to Ross juniors and seniors; invests real university money. (Not the Wolverine Venture Fund, which is MBA/PhD.)\n- Zell Lurie Institute — Ross's entrepreneurship hub, but open to all U-M schools. Runs the Michigan Business Challenge and Zell Entrepreneurs fellowship.\n- Zell Entrepreneurs fellowship — year-long, open to final-year students from any U-M school, up to $10,000 invested, path to Zell Founders Fund post-graduation. Undergrads have made recent cohorts.\n- MO 300: Behavioral Theory in Management — named by an admitted student in their essay.\n- Center for Entrepreneurship (CoE) — based in Engineering but open to all schools. 30+ course sections. EHour (Friday founder interview series). Runs the campus-wide Entrepreneurship minor.\n- optiMize — independent, 13-week Social Innovation Challenge, open to any U-M student. Up to $10,000 at pitch stage, $20,000 as Fellow. $500,000+ awarded annually. Does NOT require a business major.\n\n**Professors / faculty to research:**\n- Erik Gordon — area chair of entrepreneurial studies at Zell Lurie. Frequently quoted in media on business topics.\n\n**Caution on naming:**\n- Do NOT name the Wolverine Venture Fund (~$9M) or Zell Lurie Commercialization Fund as things you'll join as a first-year. Both are graduate-level.\n- Do not say \"the Zell Lurie Institute is a Ross resource\" — it is open to all U-M students.\n- The IBE (Integrated Business and Engineering) program is new for fall 2027. If writing about it, verify current state at michiganross.umich.edu.\n\n---\n\n## Coaching questions\n\nThese are for Veer's \"Why Michigan,\" Community & Contribution, Business Case Discussion, and Artifact essays.\n\n1. The Ross \"Why Michigan\" essay should demonstrate your passion for business — not describe the school. What was the moment you knew you wanted to study business? Not your family's influence. Not a class. The specific moment.\n\n2. The Business Case Discussion must be local and personal. What business problem exists in Lincolnshire or the Chicago suburb ecosystem that you have direct knowledge of — as a customer, a founder, a community member? Do not pick a global problem.\n\n3. What business vocabulary do you already own? Can you explain a concept like customer acquisition cost, unit economics, or market segmentation from personal experience — not from a textbook? That \"existing knowledge\" is what Ross is grading.\n\n4. Your artifact: what are you most proud of from four years of high school — not most impressive, most proud. What is the thing that, if you left it off this application, would make this application less true?\n\n5. The Community & Contribution essay asks what you'll contribute to \"leaders and citizens who challenge the present and enrich the future.\" What is the specific thing you will challenge at Michigan? Not in general — in a specific class, club, or problem space.\n\n6. The business case solution cannot be an app. It also can't be a vague \"partnership\" or \"awareness campaign.\" What is a concrete mechanism — a business model, a pricing change, a supply chain restructure, a policy lever — that you understand well enough to defend if challenged?\n\n7. You're OOS at roughly $78K/yr. If Ross denies you direct admit, you're out of Michigan. LSA does not give you a path to Ross without a competitive transfer process. Is Michigan really a school you'd pay $78K for without Ross? If not, do you have a strategy if you're denied?\n\n8. Ross has said explicitly: apply Early Action. The school builds the majority of its class from EA applicants. What is your argument for choosing ED (binding) over EA (non-binding)? If you can't articulate it, apply EA.\n\n9. For the Ross portfolio artifact: if an admissions reader can look at your artifact and description and say \"this student learned X about themselves that couldn't come from any other experience,\" you're in the right territory. Can you write that sentence?\n\n10. \"Why Ross\" vs. \"Why Business.\" Those are different questions. Ross asks \"how would that curriculum support your interests?\" — curriculum is the operative word. Name three specific curricular elements (courses, projects, programs) and explain why each one serves your specific goal, not just your general interest in business.\n\n11. The optiMize Social Innovation Challenge is open to any U-M student, gives up to $20,000, and does not require you to be a Ross student. If you don't get Ross, how does that change your Michigan plan? Does Michigan still make sense?\n\n12. Ross wants evidence you already understand business. Your entrepreneurship experiences (whatever they are) are evidence — but only if you name them specifically in the business case. A student who analyzed their own pricing failure is more credible than one who analyzes a Fortune 500 case. What business mistake have you made that taught you something real?\n\n---\n\n## Sources\n\n| Source | URL | Date / Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Ross BBA admissions — first-year applicants | https://michiganross.umich.edu/undergraduate/bba/admissions/first-year-applicants | ED/EA/RD deadlines; 560 spots; portfolio instructions |\n| Ross admissions FAQs | https://michiganross.umich.edu/undergraduate/bba/admissions/faqs | Test optional; portfolio description; postponed process |\n| Ross admissions portfolio | https://michiganross.umich.edu/undergraduate/bba/admissions/first-year-applicants/admissions-portfolio | Business Case + Artifact; submission via SlideRoom |\n| Ross blog — Business Case Discussion rubric | https://michiganross.umich.edu/undergraduate/bba/admissions/admissions-blog/2023/08/23/business-case-discussion-what-we-re-looking | Aug 2023; verbatim rubric from Blaire Moody-Rideout |\n| Ross blog — portfolio background | https://michiganross.umich.edu/undergraduate/bba/admissions/admissions-blog/2023/07/06/michigan-ross-admissions-portfolio-provides | Jul 2023; why portfolio was created |\n| Ross blog — top tips for applying | https://michiganross.umich.edu/undergraduate/bba/admissions/admissions-blog/2024/08/09/my-top-tips-applying-michigan-ross | Aug 2024; EA as priority; reflective writing advice |\n| Ross blog — IBE and preferred admission updates | https://michiganross.umich.edu/undergraduate/bba/admissions/admissions-blog/2026/08/18/michigan-ross-application-updates-ibe | Aug 2026; IBE fallback option; preferred admission clarified |\n| Ross — \"Why Michigan\" essay tip | https://michiganross.umich.edu/undergraduate/bba/admissions/admissions-blog/2026/08/18/michigan-ross-application-updates-ibe | \"Demonstrate your passion for business, not rankings\" |\n| CollegeVine — UMich essay examples | https://blog.collegevine.com/university-of-michigan-essay-examples | Mar 2024; 7 essays including Ross-specific |\n| CollegeVine — \"Why Michigan\" essay example | https://blog.collegevine.com/why-michigan-essay-example | Annotated LSA psychology example |\n| CollegeAdvisor — UMich essay examples | https://www.collegeadvisor.com/essay-guides/university-of-michigan-essay-examples-and-why-they-worked/ | Ex-AO commentary; Ross Preferred Admission example |\n| The Application Authority — Ross artifact case study | https://theapplicationauthority.com/2023/10/winning-ross-portfolio-artifact-examples-a-case-study/ | Oct 2023; two admitted artifact examples (Johanne, Priyanka) |\n| Essays That Worked — UMich examples | https://essaysthatworked.com/university-of-michigan | 2022-26 admitted essays |\n| Getcounsely.com — UMich essays guide | https://www.getcounsely.com/blog/umich-supplemental-essays | May 2026; OOS rate ~9%; Ross-specific essay advice |\n| Ross admitted student profile / viewbook | Source: Ross BBA viewbook (cited in ref file) | 924 admits of 13,019 applicants = ~7% Ross BBA rate |\n| optiMize | https://optimizemi.org | Up to $20,000; any U-M student eligible |\n| Zell Lurie Institute | https://zli.umich.edu | Open to all U-M schools; Michigan Business Challenge |\n| Michigan Business Challenge 2026 results | Per reference file | FastFlo, BS '26 founder, won 2026 Innovation Track |\n"};
export const DIGEST = "## Duke University\nDeadlines: Early Decision (binding): Nov 2 · Regular Decision: Jan 4\nStrategy: Single binding ED (no EA, no ED II); Class of 2030 ED 13.8% vs RD 3.7%. Legacy is still considered. Two required essays (Why Duke; Communities, new for 2026-27, no admitted examples exist yet). Optional alumni interview.\nPrompts:\n- Why Duke? (required) (250 words, Required): What is your impression of Duke as a university and community, and why do you believe it is a good match for your goals, values, and interests? If there is something specific that attracts you to our academic offerings in Trinity College of Arts and Sciences or the Pratt School of Engineering, or to our co-curricular o…\n- Communities (required — NEW for 2026-27) (250 words, Required): We all belong to communities defined by place, faith, family, culture, interests or shared experience. Tell us about a community that has shaped who you are, any way it has set you apart, and what you’ve learned from being part of it that you hope to bring to Duke.\n- Viewpoints & experiences (optional — pick one of three) (250 words, Optional): We believe a wide range of viewpoints and experiences is essential to maintaining Duke’s vibrant living and learning community. Please share anything in this context that might help us better understand you and your potential contributions to Duke.\n- Difference of opinion (optional — pick one of three) (250 words, Optional): Meaningful dialogue often involves respectful disagreement. Provide an example of a difference of opinion you’ve had with someone you care about. What did you learn from it?\n- Something you are excited about (optional — pick one of three) (250 words, Optional): What’s the last thing that you’ve been really excited about?\n\n## New York University\nDeadlines: Early Decision I (binding): Nov 1 · Early Decision II (binding): Jan 1 · Regular Decision: Jan 5\nStrategy: ED I and ED II. You apply to a specific school (Stern, CAS, Gallatin, Tandon...); Stern admits under 5%. One optional 'bridge builder' essay. NYU may offer a different campus (Abu Dhabi, Shanghai) or Liberal Studies instead.\nPrompts:\n- The ‘bridge builder’ essay (optional) (≤250 words · answer one or more parts, Optional): We are looking for students who want to be bridge builders—students who can connect people, groups, and ideas to span divides, foster understanding, and promote collaboration within a dynamic, interconnected, and vibrant global academic community. We are eager for you to tell us how your experiences have helped you und…\n\n## University of Chicago\nDeadlines: Early Action (non-binding): Nov 2 · Early Decision I (binding): Nov 2 · Early Decision II (binding): Jan 4 · Regular Decision: Jan 4\nStrategy: ED I, ED II, EA and RD. The extended essay rewards real intellectual risk. Optional video profile; no interviews.\nPrompts:\n- Why UChicago? (required) (~300–600 words, Required): How does the University of Chicago, as you know it now, satisfy your desire for a particular kind of learning, community, and future? Please address with some specificity your own wishes and how they relate to UChicago.\n- Extended essay 1 — how do thoughts eat? (no hard limit, Extended · pick 1): Food for thought: How do thoughts eat? (Inspired by Ernest Leong, Class of 2026.)\n- Extended essay 2 — origami-inspired solutions (no hard limit, Extended · pick 1): The James Webb Space Telescope, launched in 2021, utilized origami-inspired techniques to compactly store and deploy its sunshield in space. Choose an artistic practice and use its principles to propose an elegant solution to a problem. (Inspired by Akhil Korra, Class of 2028.)\n- Extended essay 3 — mixed metaphors (no hard limit, Extended · pick 1): Sometimes metaphors get mixed up. For one reason or another, one could say, “we'll burn that bridge when we get there” or “the world is your china shop.” Make up your own mixed metaphor. Explain how it could make sense, be understood, or even applied. (Inspired by Julia Nieberg, Class of 2029.)\n- Extended essay 4 — mundane Olympics (no hard limit, Extended · pick 1): The Olympics have long celebrated the pinnacle of human athletic achievement. But what if they expanded to honor the mundane? Imagine a new Olympic event built around an everyday activity like speed dishwashing or competitive grocery bagging. How is it scored, officiated, and judged? Why is it a worthy addition? (Inspi…\n- Extended essay 5 — Mistaken Identity of Acronym (no hard limit, Extended · pick 1): AI: Allen Iverson. NASA, or the North American Saxophone Alliance. Share a potentially confounding, comedic, or captivating example of MIA (Mistaken Identity of Acronym) and tell us its backstory. (Inspired by Jeeho Byun, Class of 2030.)\n- Extended essay 6 — choose your own adventure (no hard limit, Extended · pick 1): In the spirit of adventurous inquiry, choose one of our past prompts (or create a question of your own). Be original, creative, thought provoking. Draw on your best qualities as a writer, thinker, visionary, social critic, sage, citizen of the world, or future citizen of the University of Chicago; take a little risk, a…\n\n## Northwestern University\nDeadlines: Early Decision (binding): Nov 1 · Regular Decision: Jan 4\nStrategy: ED or RD. Alumni interviews were eliminated in 2024. One required essay plus optional short prompts. The Garage, MMSS and the Kellogg certificate (applied for after enrolling) are the entrepreneurship anchors.\nPrompts:\n- Background & contribution (required) (≤300 words, Required): We want to be sure we're considering your application in the context of your personal experiences: What aspects of your background (your identity, your school setting, your community, your household, etc.) have most shaped how you see yourself engaging in Northwestern's community, be it academically, extracurricularly,…\n- Paint The Rock (optional) (<200 words, Optional): Painting “The Rock” is a tradition at Northwestern that invites all forms of expression—students promote campus events or extracurricular groups, support social or activist causes, show their Wildcat spirit (what we call “Purple Pride”), celebrate their culture, and more. What would you paint on The Rock, and why?\n- Dream up something interdisciplinary (optional) (<200 words, Optional): Northwestern fosters a distinctively interdisciplinary culture. If you could dream up an undergraduate class, research project, or creative effort (a start-up, a design prototype, a performance, etc.), what would it be? Who might be some ideal classmates or collaborators?\n- Community & belonging (optional) (<200 words, Optional): Community and belonging matter at Northwestern. Tell us about one or more communities, networks, or student groups you see yourself connecting with on campus.\n- Our location (optional) (<200 words, Optional): Northwestern's location is special: on the shore of Lake Michigan, steps from downtown Evanston, just a few miles from Chicago. What aspects of our location are most compelling to you, and why?\n- Diversity of perspectives (optional) (<200 words, Optional): Northwestern is a place where people with diverse backgrounds from all over the world can study, live, and talk with one another. This range of experiences and viewpoints immeasurably enriches learning. How might your individual background contribute to this diversity of perspectives in Northwestern's classrooms and ar…\n\n## UNC Chapel Hill\nDeadlines: Early Action (non-binding): Oct 15 · Regular Decision: Jan 15\nStrategy: Early Action Oct 15 (also the Morehead-Cain deadline). No supplemental essays for 2026-27, so the Common App essay carries everything. Out-of-state is a high reach (out-of-state students are capped at about 18% of the class). Kenan-Flagler Assured Enrollment is EA-only.\nPrompts:\n\n\n## University of California, Los Angeles\nDeadlines: UC application filing period: Oct 1–Dec 2\nStrategy: UC Application, due Nov 30: answer 4 of 8 Personal Insight Questions, 350 words each. No ED or EA; test-blind. No undergraduate business major (Business Economics; Anderson entrepreneurship minor after enrolling).\nPrompts:\n- PIQ 1 — Leadership (350 words, Pick 4 of 8): Describe an example of your leadership experience in which you have positively influenced others, helped resolve disputes or contributed to group efforts over time.\n- PIQ 2 — Creativity (350 words, Pick 4 of 8): Every person has a creative side, and it can be expressed in many ways: problem solving, original and innovative thinking, and artistically, to name a few. Describe how you express your creative side.\n- PIQ 3 — Greatest talent or skill (350 words, Pick 4 of 8): What would you say is your greatest talent or skill? How have you developed and demonstrated that talent over time?\n- PIQ 4 — Educational opportunity or barrier (350 words, Pick 4 of 8): Describe how you have taken advantage of a significant educational opportunity or worked to overcome an educational barrier you have faced.\n- PIQ 5 — Significant challenge (350 words, Pick 4 of 8): Describe the most significant challenge you have faced and the steps you have taken to overcome this challenge. How has this challenge affected your academic achievement?\n- PIQ 6 — Academic subject that inspires you (350 words, Pick 4 of 8): Think about an academic subject that inspires you. Describe how you have furthered this interest inside and/or outside of the classroom.\n- PIQ 7 — Making your community better (350 words, Pick 4 of 8): What have you done to make your school or your community a better place?\n- PIQ 8 — What makes you a strong candidate (350 words, Pick 4 of 8): Beyond what has already been shared in your application, what do you believe makes you a strong candidate for admissions to the University of California?\n\n## University of Southern California\nDeadlines: Early Decision (binding) / Early Action: Nov 1 · Regular Decision: Jan 10\nStrategy: New binding ED plus EA, both Nov 1. Spring admission is a real possible outcome. The Iovine and Young Academy has its own application, video and interview. Short 'quick takes' answers are read for consistency with the essays.\nPrompts:\n- Your academic interests & why USC (250 words, Required): Describe how you plan to pursue your academic interests and why you want to explore them at USC specifically. Please feel free to address your first- and second-choice major selections.\n- USC 'quick takes' (short answers) (≤100 characters each, Required): A set of rapid-fire prompts, e.g.: describe yourself in three words · favorite snack · best movie of all time · dream job · your life's theme song · dream trip · the TV show you'll binge next · favorite book · a class you would teach.\n\n## University of Texas at Austin\nDeadlines: Early Action / priority: Oct 15 · Regular Decision: Dec 1\nStrategy: Out-of-state applicants get no auto-admit and no CAP. McCombs admits about 9% overall, lower out-of-state. The first-choice-major short answer matters most. Canfield BHP requires listing Unspecified Business as first choice.\nPrompts:\n- Required essay (Topic A) (500–650 words, Required): Share an essay on any topic of your choice. It can be one you've already written, one that responds to a different prompt, or one of your own design. (The Common App personal statement satisfies this.)\n- Short answer — your first-choice major (≈250–300 words, Required): Why are you interested in the major you indicated as your first-choice major?\n- Short answer — proudest activity (≈250–300 words, Required): Think of all the activities — both in and outside of school — that you have been involved with during high school. Which one are you most proud of and why?\n- Short answer — circumstances (optional) (≈250–300 words, Optional): Please share background on events or special circumstances that you feel may have impacted your high school academic performance.\n\n## University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign\nDeadlines: Early Action: Nov 1 · Regular Decision: Jan 5\nStrategy: In-state for Veer. EA strongly recommended for competitive programs and merit aid. Gies applicants enter as Business Undeclared. The second-choice major essay matters (Economics in LAS is a sensible backup).\nPrompts:\n- Experience related to your first-choice major (≈150 words, Required): Explain, in detail, an experience you've had in the past 3 to 4 years related to your first-choice major. This can be an experience from an extracurricular activity, in a class you've taken, or through something else.\n- Goals and how your major helps (≈150 words, Required): Describe your personal and/or career goals after graduating from Illinois and how your selected first-choice major will help you achieve them.\n- Second-choice major (recommended) (≈150 words, Optional): Please explain your interest in your second-choice major or your overall academic or career goals.\n\n## Indiana University Bloomington\nDeadlines: Early Action / scholarship priority: Nov 1 · Regular (rolling thereafter): Feb 1\nStrategy: Kelley direct admit is now holistic for Fall 2027, with the KPI questions (no automatic GPA/test thresholds). Students admitted to a second-choice major cannot switch to Kelley later. Hutton Honors College is worth applying to. Apply by Nov 1 for merit aid.\nPrompts:\n- Academic & career interests (200–400 words, Requested): Describe your academic and career plans and any special interest (for example, undergraduate research, academic interests, or leadership opportunities) that you are eager to pursue as an undergraduate at Indiana University. If you encountered any unusual circumstances, challenges, or obstacles in pursuit of your educat…\n\n## University of California, Berkeley\nDeadlines: UC application filing period: Oct 1–Dec 2\nStrategy: UC Application, due Nov 30. Haas direct-admit paths (Spieker, Global Management, M.E.T.) need extra essays and a video in January. M.E.T. falls back to Engineering if Haas says no. The Fall Program for Freshmen is closed.\nPrompts:\n- PIQ 1 — Leadership (350 words, Pick 4 of 8): Describe an example of your leadership experience in which you have positively influenced others, helped resolve disputes or contributed to group efforts over time.\n- PIQ 2 — Creativity (350 words, Pick 4 of 8): Every person has a creative side, and it can be expressed in many ways: problem solving, original and innovative thinking, and artistically, to name a few. Describe how you express your creative side.\n- PIQ 3 — Greatest talent or skill (350 words, Pick 4 of 8): What would you say is your greatest talent or skill? How have you developed and demonstrated that talent over time?\n- PIQ 4 — Educational opportunity or barrier (350 words, Pick 4 of 8): Describe how you have taken advantage of a significant educational opportunity or worked to overcome an educational barrier you have faced.\n- PIQ 5 — Significant challenge (350 words, Pick 4 of 8): Describe the most significant challenge you have faced and the steps you have taken to overcome this challenge. How has this challenge affected your academic achievement?\n- PIQ 6 — Academic subject that inspires you (350 words, Pick 4 of 8): Think about an academic subject that inspires you. Describe how you have furthered this interest inside and/or outside of the classroom.\n- PIQ 7 — Making your community better (350 words, Pick 4 of 8): What have you done to make your school or your community a better place?\n- PIQ 8 — What makes you a strong candidate (350 words, Pick 4 of 8): Beyond what has already been shared in your application, what do you believe makes you a strong candidate for admissions to the University of California?\n\n## University of Michigan–Ann Arbor\nDeadlines: Early Decision (binding): Nov 1 · Early Action (non-binding): Nov 1 · Regular Decision: Feb 1\nStrategy: Ross direct admit is about 7%. Ross says most of its class comes from Early Action. ED (binding) and EA are both Nov 1. The Ross portfolio (about 500-word business case plus an artifact) goes through SlideRoom, not the Common App. The undergraduate fund is the Michigan Blue Venture Fund (Wolverine Venture Fund is MBA/PhD only).\nPrompts:\n- Community & contribution (100–300 words, Required): At the University of Michigan, we are focused on developing leaders and citizens who will challenge the present and enrich the future. In your essay, share with us how you are prepared to contribute to these goals. This could include the people, places, experiences, or aspirations that have shaped your journey and futu…\n- Why this school or college (100–550 words, Required): Describe the unique qualities that attract you to the specific undergraduate college or school (including preferred admission and dual degree programs) to which you are applying at the University of Michigan. How would that curriculum support your interests?\n- ROSS ONLY — Business Case Discussion (portfolio) (~500 words, Required for Ross): Choose a current event or issue in your community and discuss the business implications. Propose a solution that incorporates business principles or practices. The review panel will look for creativity, drawing connections, and originality. [Submitted through SlideRoom, NOT the Common App. Ross publishes a rubric for t…\n- ROSS ONLY — Artifact & description (portfolio) (~250 words, Required for Ross): Upload a document or artifact that represents something significant about your life to show your learning in action. Describe how your artifact demonstrates your learning in action. [The artifact does not need to be business related. Ross's own framing: what are you most proud of from your four years of high school? $5…";
